hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4201","4489 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shaqiq, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said - regarding a man divorcing the virgin thrice -: She is not lawful to him until she marries a husband other than him."
"4202","4490 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, he said: Shaqiq narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, from 'Umar the like of it. So if a speaker says: We have seen servants commanded not to marry women except upon conditions, among them that they are prohibited from marrying them in their waiting periods, so whoever marries a woman in her waiting period, his marriage to her is not established, and he is in the ruling of one who did not contract marriage upon her, so by analogy upon that, it is likewise when he contracts upon her divorce at a time divorce effecting in it is forbidden, that his divorce not occur, and he be in the ruling of one who did not effect divorce. So the answer in that: That what is mentioned of marriage contracting is likewise, and likewise all contracts by which servants enter into things they enter only from where commanded to enter them. As for exiting from them, it may be by other than commanded to exit by, of that that we have seen prayers servants commanded to enter them, not to enter them except with takbir and the causes by which they enter them, and commanded not to exit them except with taslim. So whoever entered prayer without purification and without takbir was not entering it, and everyone who spoke in it prohibited speech or did in it something not done in it, of eating and drinking, and walking, and like it, exited by it from prayer, and was wrong in what he did of that in his prayer. So likewise entering marriage is only from where servants commanded to enter it. And exiting from it may be by commanded to exit from it and by other than that. So this all is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4203","3 - Chapter of Qur' (Menstrual Periods/Purities). Abu Ja'far said: People differed regarding the quru' obligatory upon the woman when divorced. So a group said: They are menstruations, and others said: They are purities. So among the proof of one who went to that they are purities is the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to 'Umar when 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar divorced his wife while menstruating: Command him to take her back, then leave her until she is pure, then divorce her if he wishes, so that is the waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for, and we have mentioned that with its chain in the chapter before this chapter. They said: So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to divorce her in purity, and made it the waiting period without her, and forbade him to divorce her in menstruation, and removed it from being a waiting period, it is established thereby that the quru' are purities. So among the proof against them for the others is that this hadith has been narrated from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, as they mentioned. And there has been narrated from him what is more complete than that. So it is narrated from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded 'Umar to command him to take her back then wait, until she is pure, then menstruates, then is pure, then divorce her if he wishes, and said: That is the waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for. And we have mentioned that also with its chain in the chapter before this chapter. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him divorce effecting in the purity after the menstruation in which he divorced, until there be a purity and another menstruation after it, it is established thereby that had he intended by his saying: (So that is the waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for, purities) then he would have made for him to divorce her after her purity from this menstruation, and not wait what is after it; because that is a purity. So when he did not permit him divorce in that purity until there be another purity between it and that purity a menstruation, it is established thereby that that waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for, is only the time when women are divorced, and not because it is a waiting period women are divorced for, necessitating thereby that it be the waiting period by which women count; because the waiting period is different. Of them: waiting period of one whose husband died, four months and ten. And of them: waiting period of the divorced, three quru'. And of them: waiting period of the pregnant that she deliver her burden, so the waiting period was one name for different meanings. And not everything bearing the name (waiting period) must be a qur'. So likewise when the name waiting period attached to the time in which women are divorced, it is not established thereby the name qur'. So this is a sound opposition, and if we wanted to increase here, and argue with the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the mustahadah: Leave prayer the days of your quru', and say: The quru' are: menstruation upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that would be what some predecessors attached to, but we do not do that; because the Arabs name menstruation qur', and name purity qur', and combine menstruation and purity, and name them qur'. 4491 - Mahmud ibn Hassan al-Nahwi narrated that to me, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Abu Zayd, from Abu 'Amr ibn al-'Ala. And in that also is another proof, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is the one the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed with his saying: So that is the waiting period which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded women be divorced for, and that was not - with him - proof that the quru' are purities, since he made the quru' menstruations, in what is narrated from him. So when this with 'Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed him with it, is no proof that the qur' is purity, one after him in it is also likewise, and we will mention what is narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this, in its place of this chapter, Allah Almighty willing. And among what those who made the quru' purities argued with also, is what:"
"4204","4492 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, that she moved Hafsah bint 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, when she entered into blood of the third menstruation. Ibn Shihab said: So I mentioned that to 'Amrah, and she said: 'Urwah spoke truth; people disputed her in that, and said: Allah Almighty says: Three quru'. So 'A'ishah said: (You spoke truth; do you know what the quru' are? The quru' are only purities.)"
"4205","4493 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, he said: Ibn Shihab said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman saying: I did not meet anyone of our jurists except he saying this, meaning what 'A'ishah said."
"4206","4494 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he said: When a man divorces his wife and she enters into the blood of the third menstruation, she is cleared of him and he is cleared of her; she does not inherit from him nor does he inherit from her."
"4207","4495 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: When the divorced woman enters into the third menstruation, she is cleared of him and he is cleared of her."
"4208","4496 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4209","4497 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit ruled, and he mentioned the same. Ibn Shihab said: And 'Urwah informed me of that, from 'A'ishah."
"4210","4498 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', that Mu'awiyah wrote to Zayd ibn Thabit asking him, and he wrote: When she enters into the third menstruation, she is separated from him. Nafi' said: And Ibn 'Umar used to say it. They said: So these are the statements of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that, indicating what we mentioned. It was said to them: That would hold if the Companions had not differed about it; but since they differed, some of them saying what you mentioned and others saying the opposite, what you mentioned does not establish a proof for you. Among what was narrated contrary to these reports is what indicates that quru' are not the periods of purity."
"4211","4499 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: Her husband has more right to her so long as she has not washed from the third menstruation."
"4212","4500 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, that a man divorced his wife and she menstruated twice, and when it was the third time and she entered the washing place, her husband came to her and said: I have taken you back, three times. They brought the case to 'Umar, and 'Umar and 'Abdullah agreed that he has more right to her so long as prayer has not become lawful for her; so 'Umar returned her to him."
"4213","4501 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, he used to say: When a slave divorces his wife twice, she becomes unlawful for him until she marries another husband, whether she is free or a slave; the waiting period of a free woman is three menstrual cycles, and that of a slave woman is two. Abu Ja'far said: So this is 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, the one who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his statement to 'Umar: That is the waiting period which Allah commanded women to be divorced for; yet that did not lead him to take quru' as purity, since he made them menstruation."
"4214","4502 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Makhul, that he came to Madinah, and Sulayman ibn Yasar mentioned to him that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say: When a man divorces his wife and she sees the first drop of blood of her third menstruation, he has no return to her. He said: So I asked about that in Madinah, and it reached me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Abu al-Darda' held that he retains return to her until she washes from the third menstruation."
"4215","4503 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb informed me that he heard Zayd ibn Thabit saying: Divorce belongs to the man and the waiting period to the woman; if the man is free and the woman a slave, there are three divorces and the waiting period is that of a slave, two menstrual cycles; and if he is a slave and his wife free, he divorces the divorce of a slave, two divorces, and she observes the waiting period of a free woman, three menstrual cycles. When this disagreement came from them, it is established that none of their statements can be used as proof in that, for whenever one argues with the statement of some of them, his opponent argues against him with a like statement, so all of that ceases to be a proof for either party against the other. Among the proof of those who made quru' menstruation against their opponent is that they said: If quru' were purity, and a man divorced a woman while pure and she menstruated an hour later and that counted as one quru' along with two consecutive ones, her waiting period would be two quru' and part of one, while Allah said three quru'. Those who held quru' to be purity answered: Allah said the Hajj is known months, and that is two months and part of a month, so likewise we made the three quru' two and part of one. Our proof against them is that Allah said regarding quru' three quru', and did not say regarding Hajj three months; had He said three months and all agreed it means two months and part of a month, the opponent's claim would be established, but He only said months without saying three. As for what He limited with three, He limited it with a known number, so it cannot be less than that number, just as when He said regarding those who despair of menstruation and those who have not menstruated that their period is three months, limited by number, it is not less than that. Likewise when He limited quru' by number, saying three quru', it is not less than that. They also argued that the ta' is affirmed in counting masculines, as three men, and dropped in counting feminines, as three women; and since Allah said three quru' with the ta', it must mean a masculine, purity not menstruation. The answer is that when a thing has two names, one masculine and one feminine, and it is counted with the masculine it takes the ta', and with the feminine it drops it; as you say this garment and this sheet for one thing, counting three garments with the ta' and three sheets without it, and likewise this house and this dwelling. So menstruation and quru' are two names with one meaning, menstruation; counted with menstruation it is three hayad without ta', counted with quru' it is three quru' with ta', both names for one thing, so the opponent's argument falls. As for the rational aspect: a slave woman bears half the waiting period of a free woman; if she does not menstruate it is a month and a half, and if she menstruates all agree it is two cycles, meaning half of the free woman's. 'Umar said before the Companions: If I could make it a cycle and a half I would. Since the slave's share is menstruation not purity, half of the free woman's, what is upon the free woman is of the same kind, menstruation not purity. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the slave's waiting period what follows."
"4216","4504 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muzahir ibn Aslam, from al-Qasim, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The slave woman observes a waiting period of two menstrual cycles and is divorced with two divorces. So that also indicates what we mentioned. And there has:"
"4217","4505 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Shabib al-Musalli, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Isa, from 'Atiyyah, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. So that also indicates what we mentioned, and success is with Allah."
"4218","Chapter: the woman divorced with an irrevocable divorce, what she is owed by her husband during her waiting period. Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah, Husayn, Ash'ath, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, Dawud, Yasar and Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I entered upon Fatimah bint Qays in Madinah and asked her about the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruling concerning her. She said: My husband divorced me irrevocably, and I disputed with him before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about dwelling and maintenance, and he assigned me neither dwelling nor maintenance, and ordered me to observe my waiting period in the house of Ibn Maktum. And Mujalid said in his narration: O daughter of Qays, maintenance and dwelling are only upon one who retains return."
"4219","4506 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Fatimah bint Qays narrated to me that Abu 'Amr ibn Hafs al-Makhzumi divorced her thrice, and he assigned maintenance for her but she deemed it small; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent him toward Yemen. Khalid ibn al-Walid went with some men of Banu Makhzum to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in Maymunah's house, and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu 'Amr ibn Hafs divorced Fatimah thrice, does she have maintenance? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She has neither maintenance nor dwelling. And he sent to her to move to Umm Sharik, then sent to her that the early Muhajirun visit Umm Sharik, so move to Ibn Umm Maktum, for when you remove your headcover he will not see you."
"4220","4507 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4221","4508 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: It was read to Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth: Your father informed you, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Abu Salamah, that he said: I asked Fatimah bint Qays and she informed me that her Makhzumi husband divorced her and refused to spend on her, so she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have no maintenance; move to Ibn Umm Maktum and stay with him, for he is a blind man, you may lay aside your garments with him."
"4222","4509 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4223","4510 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, that he asked 'Abd al-Hamid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Amr ibn al-Hafs about the divorce of his grandfather Abu 'Umar and Fatimah bint Qays. 'Abd al-Hamid said to him: He divorced her irrevocably then went to Yemen and appointed 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, and 'Ayyash sent her some maintenance which she disdained. 'Ayyash said to her: You are owed neither maintenance nor dwelling from us; here is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so ask him. She asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he said, and he said: You have neither maintenance nor dwelling, but a fair provision; leave them. She said: Shall I leave for the house of Umm Sharik? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Her house is frequented; move to the house of 'Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum the blind, it is more fitting."
"4224","4511 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Fatimah bint Qays herself, with the same as al-Layth's narration from Abu al-Zubayr, letter for letter."
"4225","4512 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Fatimah bint Qays, that Abu 'Amr ibn Hafs divorced her irrevocably while absent, and sent her barley through his agent which she disdained, and he said: By Allah, you are owed nothing from us. She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned it to him, and he said: You are owed no maintenance from him, and observe your waiting period in the house of Umm Sharik."
"4226","4513 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me that Fatimah bint Qays narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same alike."
"4227","4514 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, and he mentioned the same with its chain, and added: The people objected to her for what she narrated of leaving before she became lawful."
"4228","4515 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Fatimah bint Qays, that she was under a man of Banu Makhzum and he divorced her irrevocably; she sent to his family seeking maintenance, and they said: You are owed no maintenance from us. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You are owed no maintenance from them, and the waiting period is upon you, so move to Umm Sharik. Then he said: Her brothers among the Muhajirun visit Umm Sharik; move to Ibn Umm Maktum."
"4229","4516 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Fatimah bint Qays, that she sought a ruling from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when her husband divorced her, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You have neither maintenance nor dwelling from him. His companions used to visit her, so he said: Observe your waiting period with Ibn Umm Maktum, for he is blind."
"4230","4517 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Asim ibn Thabit informed me that Fatimah bint Qays informed him; she was with a man of Banu Makhzum, and she informed him that he divorced her thrice and went to some campaign and ordered an agent to give her some maintenance which she deemed small. She went to one of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while she was with her, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Fatimah bint Qays; so-and-so divorced her and sent her some maintenance which she returned, claiming it was a voluntary favor. He said: He spoke the truth. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Move to Umm Sharik and observe your waiting period with her, then he said: Umm Sharik has many visitors, but move to 'Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, for he is blind. So she moved to 'Abdullah and observed her waiting period with him until it ended."
"4231","4518 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm, he said: Abu Salamah and I entered upon Fatimah bint Qays, and she narrated that her husband divorced her with an irrevocable divorce and ordered Abu Hafs ibn 'Amr to send her maintenance of five wasqs; she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My husband divorced me and assigned me neither dwelling nor maintenance. He said: He spoke the truth; observe your waiting period in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum. Then he said: Ibn Umm Maktum is a man who is visited; observe your waiting period in the house of so-and-so's mother."
"4232","4519 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to me, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Sukhayr, he said: Abu Salamah and I entered upon Fatimah bint Qays; her husband had divorced her thrice, and she said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he assigned me neither dwelling nor maintenance."
"4233","4520 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah informed me, from Fatimah bint Qays, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar. Abu Ja'far said: Some people adopted these reports and followed them, saying maintenance and dwelling are due only to one subject to return. Others differed, saying every divorced woman has dwelling in her waiting period until it ends, whether the divorce is irrevocable or not; as for maintenance it is due if the divorce is revocable, and if irrevocable they differ: some said she has maintenance with dwelling whether pregnant or not, among them Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad; some said she has no maintenance unless pregnant. And they countered Fatimah's report with what follows."
"4234","4521 - Abu Bakrah informed us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: 'Ammar ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I was with al-Aswad ibn Yazid in the great mosque along with al-Sha'bi; they mentioned the woman divorced thrice, and al-Sha'bi said: Fatimah bint Qays narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You have neither dwelling nor maintenance. Al-Aswad threw a pebble at him and said: Woe to you, do you narrate such? That was raised to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and he said: We do not abandon the Book of our Lord and the Sunnah of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the word of a woman of whom we do not know whether she remembered or forgot; Allah said: Do not expel them from their houses nor let them leave."
"4235","4522 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir informed us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Salamah, from al-Sha'bi, from Fatimah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when her husband divorced her he assigned her neither dwelling nor maintenance. I mentioned that to Ibrahim and he said: That was raised to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and he said: We do not leave the Book of our Lord and the Sunnah of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the word of a woman; she has dwelling and maintenance."
"4236","4523 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father informed me, he said: al-A'mash informed me, from Ibrahim, from 'Umar and 'Abdullah that they used to say: The woman divorced thrice has maintenance and dwelling. And al-Sha'bi used to mention from Fatimah bint Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: She has neither maintenance nor dwelling."
"4237","4524 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from al-Sha'bi, from Fatimah bint Qays, that her husband divorced her thrice and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You have neither maintenance nor dwelling. He said: I informed al-Nakha'i of that and he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said when informed of that: We do not abandon a verse of Allah's Book and the word of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the word of a woman who may have been made to imagine; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: She has dwelling and maintenance."
"4238","4525 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to me, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to me, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from al-Aswad, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said regarding the woman divorced thrice: She has dwelling and maintenance. They said: So 'Umar rejected this report of Fatimah and did not accept it, and Usamah ibn Zayd also rejected it against her."
"4239","4526 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: Fatimah bint Qays used to narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said to her: Observe your waiting period in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum. And Muhammad ibn Usamah ibn Zayd used to say: Whenever Fatimah mentioned anything of that, Usamah would throw at her whatever was in his hand. Abu Ja'far said: So Usamah ibn Zayd rejected of that what 'Umar rejected, and 'A'ishah also rejected it."
"4240","4527 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Sulayman ibn Yasar mentioning that Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As divorced the daughter of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hakam, and 'Abd al-Rahman moved her. 'A'ishah sent to Marwan, the governor of Madinah: Fear Allah and return the woman to her house. Marwan said in Sulayman's narration: 'Abd al-Rahman overcame me, and said in al-Qasim's narration: Has the report of Fatimah bint Qays not reached you? 'A'ishah said: It will not harm you not to mention the report of Fatimah bint Qays. Marwan said: If evil is with you, what is between these two of evil suffices you."
"4241","4528 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4242","4529 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar informed us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from his father, he said: 'A'ishah said: Fatimah has no report worth mentioning this narration for, meaning her saying no maintenance and no dwelling. So 'A'ishah did not see acting upon Fatimah's report either, and Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib diverted it to other than the meaning given by the first party."
"4243","4530 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, from his father, he said: I said to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: Where does the woman divorced thrice observe her waiting period? He said: In her house. I said to him: Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order Fatimah bint Qays to observe it in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum? He said: That woman tempted people and was harsh with her tongue against her in-laws, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to observe it in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum, a blind man. Abu Ja'far said: So what Fatimah narrated, his saying to her you have neither dwelling nor maintenance, carries no proof with Sa'id that a thrice-divorced woman has neither, since he diverted it to the meaning we mentioned from him. And there has:"
"4244","4531 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Fatimah bint Qays informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Observe your waiting period in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum. The people objected to what she narrated of leaving before she became lawful. So Abu Salamah also informs that the people, among them Companions and Successors, objected to Fatimah. 'Umar, Usamah and Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib with those named with them rejected it and did not act upon it, and that from 'Umar was before the Companions without any objector, indicating their view was like his. Those who acted upon Fatimah's report said 'Umar only rejected it because she contradicted Allah's verse: Lodge them where you dwell, but that concerns a revocable divorce while Fatimah was irrevocably divorced with no return, and she reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said maintenance and dwelling are only for one subject to return; what Allah mentioned concerns the woman subject to return, and Fatimah had no return, so her report is not repelled by Book or Sunnah. And others followed her, among them 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas and al-Hasan."
"4245","4532 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas."
"4246","4533 - And Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that they both used to say regarding the woman divorced thrice and the widow: They have no maintenance and they observe their waiting period wherever they wish. They said: If 'Umar, 'A'ishah and Usamah rejected what Fatimah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said the opposite, here Ibn 'Abbas agreed with her in what she narrated and acted upon it, and al-Hasan followed him. Our proof against this party is that what 'Umar used to repel Fatimah's report is a sound proof: Allah said O Prophet, when you divorce women divorce them for their waiting period, then He said you know not, perhaps Allah will bring about after that a matter, and all agree that matter is return; then He said lodge them where you dwell, then He said do not expel them from their houses nor let them leave, meaning in the waiting period. So when a woman is divorced twice according to the Sunnah, then taken back, then divorced again according to the Sunnah, she becomes unlawful and the waiting period in which Allah assigned dwelling and forbade leaving becomes due, and Allah did not distinguish between the Sunnah-divorced woman with no return and the one subject to return. When Fatimah reported that he said to her dwelling and maintenance are only for one subject to return, she contradicted the Book in text, for the Book assigned dwelling to one with no return, and contradicted the Sunnah, for 'Umar narrated the opposite from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so 'Umar's rejection stands sound and Fatimah's report falls and need not be acted upon. An objector said: The confusion came only from what al-Sha'bi narrated from her, that he assigned neither. Abu Ja'far said: He was inattentive, for he did not narrate the whole chapter as others did and imagined he had gathered all of it; it is not as he imagined, for al-Sha'bi is more precise, firm and trustworthy than he thinks, and those we mentioned at the chapter's start agreed with him, sparing repetition. And Malik's narration from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid without you have no dwelling was narrated by al-Layth from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid from Abu Salamah from Fatimah like al-Sha'bi's, so the confusion is not from al-Sha'bi but from one who narrated from Abu Salamah omitting part; the basis is as al-Sha'bi narrated. The opponent also said: Even as al-Sha'bi narrated, it agrees with us, for you have no maintenance means because you are not pregnant, and you have no dwelling because you are foul-tongued, and foulness is the clear indecency Allah mentioned. And he mentioned regarding that what follows."
"4247","4534 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he was asked about His saying nor let them leave except they commit a clear indecency, and he said: The clear indecency is to be foul against the husband's family and harm them; he said: So Fatimah was deprived of dwelling for her foulness, and of maintenance because she was not pregnant. He said: This is our proof that the irrevocably divorced woman is owed no maintenance unless pregnant. It was said to him: If Fatimah's report came out as you said, error would fall upon 'Umar, 'A'ishah, Usamah and all who rejected it with them, whereas they ought to be left upon correctness until certainty of otherwise; moreover even if Fatimah's report were sound, its meaning could be other than yours: it could be that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deprived her of dwelling for her foulness, deeming it the indecency Allah mentioned, and deprived her of maintenance for her defiance by which she left her husband's house, for a divorced woman who leaves in her waiting period is owed no maintenance until she returns; so Fatimah was barred for her defiance in leaving. That is a possible meaning he may have intended if the report is sound, as is what you described, or another we do not know. And it is not ruled upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he meant one specific meaning as you ruled, for speaking about him by conjecture is unlawful as speaking about Allah by conjecture is unlawful. And there was narrated from Ibn 'Umar regarding the clear indecency other than what Ibn 'Abbas said."
"4248","4535 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar said regarding His saying do not expel them from their houses nor let them leave except they commit a clear indecency: Her leaving her house is a clear indecency. Others said: The clear indecency is that she commits adultery and leaves for the penalty to be applied. Who gave you to affirm Ibn 'Abbas's interpretation and argue with it, leaving what Ibn 'Umar said. And there was narrated from Fatimah bint Qays in her report another meaning than we mentioned, for Abu Shu'ayb al-Basri Salih ibn Shu'ayb."
"4249","4536 - That is that Abu Shu'ayb al-Basri Salih ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna al-Zamin narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Fatimah bint Qays, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, my husband divorced me and he means to burst in upon me. He said: Move away from him. So Fatimah informs in this report that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to move when she feared her husband. An objector said: How can this be, when some reports say he divorced her while absent, or divorced then left and she disputed with his cousin about maintenance, while this says she feared him, one saying absent and the other present, so they contradict. It was said to him: They do not, for she may have feared assault when divorced and asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who granted the move, then he later left and appointed his cousin over her maintenance, and she disputed while he was absent and he said to her: You have neither dwelling nor maintenance; so 'Urwah's report agrees with al-Sha'bi's, Abu Salamah's and their fellows from Fatimah. This is the chapter by reports. As for reason: all agree the irrevocably divorced pregnant woman is owed maintenance by her husband, as Allah ruled: If they are pregnant spend upon them until they deliver. It may be that maintenance is upon the divorcer because through it the child in her belly is nourished, due for his child as he must nourish him in suckling through whoever nurses him, then after with food and drink. Or it may be that maintenance for the fetus is due upon the father as nourishment through spending on the mother, since that conveys nourishment to him. Or it may be due to the divorced woman herself for the waiting period, not for the child. If for the waiting period, those who said the irrevocably divorced woman has maintenance and dwelling, pregnant or not, stand; if for the child, it does not prove maintenance for the non-pregnant. We examined: a man must spend on his small child in suckling until independent and after as his like needs; if the child is rich by inheritance from his mother or gift, the father need not spend from his wealth but from the child's; if the father spent by a judge's ruling thinking him poor then learned he already owned wealth, he may recover from the child's wealth. But when a man divorces his pregnant wife and the judge rules maintenance and he spends until she bears a live child, and a maternal brother of the child had died before while she was pregnant so the child inherited him, the father may not in all their view recover from his son what he spent on the mother by the ruling. So maintenance for the pregnant divorcee is for the waiting period from her divorcer, not for the pregnancy. Hence every woman in a waiting period from an irrevocable divorce has maintenance like the pregnant divorcee, by analogy. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, and was narrated from 'Umar and 'Abdullah as we mentioned, and from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."
"4250","4537 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The woman divorced thrice has maintenance and dwelling."
"4251","4538 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, the same."
"4252","Chapter: the widow, may she travel in her waiting period, and what enters of the divorced woman's ruling in obligatory mourning during her waiting period. 4539 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us. 4540 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us; both said: From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir, he said: An aunt of mine was divorced and wanted to go out in her waiting period to her palms, and a man said to her: That is not yours. She came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Go out to your palms and harvest them, for you may give charity and do good."
"4253","4541 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Jabir saying: My aunt informed me that she was divorced irrevocably and wanted to harvest her palms, and men forbade her to leave, so she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Yes, harvest your palms, for you may give charity and do good. Abu Ja'far said: Some held the divorced woman and the widow may travel in their waiting periods wherever they wish, arguing with this report. Others differed, saying: The widow may leave her house by day but sleep only in her house; the divorced woman leaves neither by night nor day. They distinguished, for the divorced woman in their view has maintenance and dwelling upon her divorcer, sparing her leaving, while the widow has no maintenance, so she may go out in daylight seeking her Lord's bounty. Their proof regarding Jabir's report is that it may have been when mourning was not yet due through the whole waiting period, as indeed was the case."
"4254","4542 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us. 4543 - And Abu Bakrah also narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us. 4544 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us. 4545 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Jabbarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us. 4546 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us; they said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Asma' bint 'Umays, she said: When Ja'far was stricken, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me: Stay three, then do as you wish. So in this report mourning was not upon the waiting woman through all her waiting period but in a particular time of it; then that was abrogated and she was ordered to mourn four months and ten days. And among what was narrated regarding that is what follows."
"4255","4547 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn a dead person more than three days except for a husband; for she mourns him four months and ten days."
"4256","4548 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, she said: When the news of Abu Sufyan came, Umm Habibah called for yellow dye and wiped her forearms and cheeks and said: I have no need of this, were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then she mentioned the same as 'A'ishah's report alike."
"4257","4549 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Umm Salamah, she said: While I was with Umm Habibah, then she mentioned the same as Yunus's report."
"4258","4560 - Humayd said: And Zaynab bint Umm Salamah narrated to me from her mother Umm Salamah, that she said: A woman named 'Atikah of Quraysh, daughter of al-Nahham, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We fear for her eyesight. He said: No, four months and ten days; one of you used to mourn her husband a year then throw a dung upon the year's head."
"4259","4561 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Humayd ibn Nafi', freedman of the Ansar, that he heard Zaynab bint Umm Salamah narrating from her mother, and from Umm Habibah, whose name was Ramlah, the same as in Rabi's report from them. Humayd said: I said to Zaynab: What is the year's head? She said: In the Jahiliyyah when a woman's husband died she betook herself to the worst of her houses and sat there a year; when the year passed she went out and threw a dung behind her."
"4260","4562 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, that she informed him of these three reports. She said: Umm Habibah entered upon me, then she mentioned from her the same as we mentioned from her above from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: And I heard Umm Salamah saying: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then she mentioned similar to what we mentioned from her above. She said: I entered upon Zaynab bint Jahsh and mentioned from her from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Yunus's report from 'Ali, and in Rabi's report from Shu'ayb, of what we mentioned in their reports from Umm Salamah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the daughter of al-Nahham."
"4261","4563 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi 'Ubayd, from Hafsah bint 'Umar, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or from 'A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or from both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn a deceased more than three nights except for a husband."
"4262","4564 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi 'Ubayd, from one of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives, and she is Umm Salamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. And he added: For she mourns him four months and ten days."
"4263","4565 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi' narrating from Safiyyah bint Abi 'Ubayd, from one of the Mothers of the Believers, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn a dead person more than three days except for a husband."
"4264","4566 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4265","4567 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Hafsah, from Umm 'Atiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us that a woman not mourn more than three days except for a husband, nor line her eyes, nor perfume herself, nor wear a dyed garment except an 'asb garment."
"4266","4568 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Hafsah, from Umm 'Atiyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except he did not mention his saying except an 'asb garment."
"4267","4569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad informing from Zaynab that her mother Umm Salamah informed her that the daughter of Nu'aym ibn 'Abdullah al-'Adawi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My daughter's husband died while she is mourning, and her eyes complain, may she line them? He said: No. She said: O Prophet of Allah, she complains of her eyes beyond what you think, may she line them? He said: It is not lawful for a Muslim woman to mourn more than three days except for a husband. Then he said: Have you forgotten? In the Jahiliyyah a woman would mourn a year, placed alone in a house for the year except she was fed and given drink; when the year's head came she was brought out, then a dog or beast was brought, and when she touched it, it died; so that was lightened for you and made four months and ten days. So in these reports is proof that the widow's mourning was placed through all her waiting period, whereas before it was in three days of it upon what is in Asma's report. Then there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding al-Furay'ah bint Malik what follows."
"4268","4570 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad informed me, he said: Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn 'Ujrah al-Ansari informed me, from Zaynab bint Ka'b, she said: al-Furay'ah bint Malik ibn Sinan, sister of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, informed me that her husband's death news came to her; he went seeking some of his slaves and overtook them at the edge of al-Qadum and they killed him. She said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my husband's death news came while I am in a distant house of the Ansar far from my family's houses, and I dislike staying in it, and he left me neither in a dwelling he owned nor wealth nor maintenance to be spent upon me; if you see fit, let me join my brother so our affair is together, for it gathers my matter and is dearer to me. He said: If you wish, join your family. She said: I went out glad with that, until when I was in the chamber or the mosque he called me, and said: What did you claim? I repeated the account from its start, and he said: Remain in the house in which your husband's death news came to you until the decree reaches its term. She said: So I observed my waiting period there four months and ten days. She said: 'Uthman sent to her and asked her, and she informed him, and he ruled with it."
"4269","4571 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Abi Habib, from Yazid ibn Muhammad, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b, then he mentioned similar with its chain."
"4270","4572 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4271","4573 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to me, he said: Shu'bah and Rawh ibn al-Qasim both narrated to me from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned similar with its chain."
"4272","4574 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Salim informed me, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4273","4575 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Sa'd, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"4274","4576 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Sa'd, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except he did not mention 'Uthman's asking her, and there is no contradiction in it."
"4275","4577 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Sa'd, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except he said: al-Fari'ah, not al-Furay'ah. And he also mentioned 'Uthman's asking her, but did not mention his ruling with it."
"4276","4578 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, or Ishaq ibn Sa'd, then he mentioned the same with its chain. And he said: al-Furay'ah, and I do not know whether he mentioned 'Uthman's asking her and his ruling with it or not. Abu Ja'far said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barred al-Furay'ah from moving from her house in her waiting period and made that part of her mourning; we mentioned in Asma's report that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Stay three, then do as you wish, when her husband Ja'far ibn Abi Talib died. So that implies she need not mourn more than three, and all agree that is abrogated, for they left it and acted upon the reports of Zaynab bint Jahsh, 'A'ishah, Umm Salamah and Umm Habibah. What we mentioned requiring mourning through the whole waiting period was all meant for the widow. It may be that applies to the waiting period due by marriage contract, so the divorced woman likewise bears mourning in her waiting period as the widow; or it may be particular to the death waiting period. They differed: some said no mourning is due upon the divorced woman in her waiting period, others said mourning is upon her as upon the widow. We saw the divorced woman barred from moving from her house in her waiting period as the widow, a duty she may not leave as she may not leave the waiting period. Since she equals the widow in part of mourning, she equals her in all of it. So mourning is due upon the divorced woman in her waiting period, and a group of the early ones said so."
"4277","4579 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I asked Jabir: Do the divorced woman and the widow observe waiting or leave? Jabir said: No. I said: Do they wait wherever they wish? Jabir said: No."
"4278","4580 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that he said regarding the divorced woman: She does not perform i'tikaf, nor the widow, and neither leaves her house until she completes her term. So Jabir ibn 'Abdullah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the permission for his aunt to go out to harvest her palms in her waiting period as we mentioned above, then he himself said the opposite; this proves the abrogation was established with him. In Jabir's statement we mentioned is also his equating the divorced woman and the widow in that. Since they are alike in part of mourning, they are alike in all of it; mourning was before in part of the waiting period as in Asma's report, then abrogated and placed through all of it. What Jabir's aunt was ordered may have been when mourning was only in the three days of the waiting period, then abrogated. And there was narrated regarding that from the early ones what follows."
"4279","4581 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us. 4582 - And 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that 'Umar sent back women from Dhu al-Hulayfah whose husbands had died and who had gone out in their waiting periods."
"4280","4583 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban narrated to me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and Zayd ibn Thabit said regarding the widow in severe need: They did not permit her to leave her house except in the whiteness of her day to obtain food, then return to her house and sleep in it."
"4281","4584 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, Ibn Abi Layla and Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he said: The widow does not sleep outside her house."
"4282","4585 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Qusayd, from Muslim ibn al-Sa'ib, from his mother, she said: When al-Sa'ib died he left crops at Qanah, so I came to Ibn 'Umar and said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, al-Sa'ib died leaving a crop estate at Qanah and small young slaves with no means, and it is our home and dwelling, may I move to it? He said: Do not observe your waiting period except in the house in which your husband died; go to your estate by day and return to your house by night and sleep in it. So I used to do that."
"4283","4586 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, he said: I heard Umm Makhramah saying: I heard Umm Muslim ibn al-Sa'ib saying: al-Sa'ib died, and I asked Ibn 'Umar about going out, and he said: Do not leave your house except for need, and do not sleep except in it until your waiting period ends."
"4284","4587 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The irrevocably divorced woman does not move from her husband's house in her waiting period."
"4285","4588 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said regarding the widow and the woman divorced thrice: Neither moves nor sleeps except in her house."
"4286","4589 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: A woman was in her waiting period and her father fell ill, so she sent to Umm Salamah, Mother of the Believers: What do you see, my father is ill, may I go tend him? She said: Spend the night in your house at both ends of the night."
"4287","4590 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Makhramah informed me, from his father, that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad holding that the divorced woman may go out to the mosque. Bukayr said: And 'Amrah said from 'A'ishah: She goes out without sleeping away from her house."
"4288","4591 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that the daughter of Sa'id was under 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, and he divorced her irrevocably, and she moved, and 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar objected to her for that."
"4289","4592 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Humayd ibn Qays, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab used to turn back widows from al-Bayda', preventing them from Hajj."
"4290","4593 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: Neither the widow nor the divorced woman sleeps except in her house."
"4291","4594 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Halhalah, that 'Alqamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan divorced a woman of his family irrevocably then went to Iraq, and she asked Ibn al-Musayyib, al-Qasim, Salim, Kharijah and Sulayman ibn Yasar: May she leave her house? All of them said: No, let her sit in her house."
"4292","4595 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibrahim, he said: The woman divorced thrice, the khul' woman, the widow and the li'an woman do not dye, nor perfume themselves, nor wear a dyed garment, nor leave their houses. So those from the Companions and Successors from whom we narrated these reports barred the widow from travel and moving from her house in her waiting period, and permitted her to go out in the whiteness of her day provided she sleeps in her house. Some joined with her the irrevocably divorced woman in barring travel and moving, and none permitted her to go out by day as was permitted for the widow. So what we mentioned of barring both from travel in their waiting periods stands, except what the widow was permitted of going out by day from need. All this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. If one says: But 'A'ishah took her sister Umm Kulthum on a journey in her waiting period, and he mentioned regarding that what follows."
"4293","4596 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, he said: I heard 'Ata' saying: 'A'ishah performed Hajj with her sister Umm Kulthum in her waiting period."
"4294","4597 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to me, he said: I heard 'Ata' saying: 'A'ishah performed Hajj with her sister in her waiting period from Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah."
"4295","4598 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Aflah narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from 'A'ishah, that she performed Hajj with her sister Umm Kulthum in her waiting period."
"4296","4599 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from 'A'ishah, the same. It was said to him: That was only from necessity, for they were in civil strife, as made clear by what follows."
"4297","4600 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, he said: When Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah was killed on the Day of the Camel and 'A'ishah went to Makkah, 'A'ishah sent to Umm Kulthum while she was in Madinah and moved her to herself from fear of the strife for her, while she was in her waiting period. So thus we say: When there is strife feared for the waiting woman in staying, she is free to leave to whatever safe places she wishes, and success is with Allah."
"4298","Chapter: the slave woman freed while her husband is free, does she have a choice or not. 4601 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, that she said: Barirah's husband was free; when she was freed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice and she chose herself. Abu Ja'far said: Some adopted this report and gave the freed woman the choice, whether her husband was free or a slave. Others differed, saying: If her husband is a slave she has the choice, and if free she has none. They said: Barirah's husband was a slave. And they mentioned regarding that what follows."
"4299","4602 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: Barirah's husband was a slave, and had he been free the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have given her the choice."
"4300","4603 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad and Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim; 'Abd al-'Aziz said from his father; both said: al-A'mash from 'A'ishah that when Barirah was freed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice, and her husband was a slave. They said: So 'A'ishah informs that Barirah's husband was a slave, contrary to what you narrated from al-Aswad from her. Then 'A'ishah said: Had he been free the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have given her the choice. It was said to them: As for this phrase, it may be from 'A'ishah's words, and may be from 'Urwah's. The holders of this view argued to establish that Barirah's husband was a slave with what 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that Barirah's husband was a black slave named Mughith; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice and ordered her to observe a waiting period."
"4301","4604 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: When Barirah was given the choice we saw her husband following her in Madinah's streets with tears flowing upon his beard, and al-'Abbas spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his behalf to intercede with her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Your husband and the father of your child? She said: Do you command me with him, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I am only an intercessor. She said: If you are an intercessor I have no need of him, and she chose herself; he was called Mughith, a slave of Al al-Mughirah of Banu Makhzum. They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice only because her husband was a slave. The proof of the first party against them is that when reports come thus and a way is found to carry them without contradiction, we carry them so and not upon contradiction and falsehood, and their narrators remain upon truthfulness and uprightness until no escape from otherwise. Since it was said of Barirah's husband that he was a slave and that he was free, we took it that he was a slave at one time and free at another. Since slavery may be followed by freedom but freedom is not followed by slavery, we made slavery earlier and freedom later. So he was free when Barirah was given the choice, a slave before; thus the reports are reconciled. Even had all narrations agreed he was a slave, that would not negate the choice had he been free, for nothing came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I gave her the choice only because her husband is a slave; had it been so, a free husband would bar choice. Since nothing such came and it came that he gave her the choice while her husband was a slave, we asked: Does the ruling differ between free and slave? We saw the slave woman in slavery may have her master contract marriage for her to free or slave, and after freedom he may not newly contract for her to either; the master's authority is alike for slaves and freemen and its absence alike. Since, after the master's contracting a slave upon her, freedom gives her the choice to dissolve, so likewise with a freeman freedom gives her dissolution of his marriage, by analogy. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. And that was also narrated from Tawus."
"4302","4605 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The slave woman has the choice when freed, even if under a Qurashi."
"4303","4606 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said: She has the choice, meaning for slave and free. He said: And al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me the same."
"4304","Chapter: a man says to his wife: You are divorced on Laylat al-Qadr, when does divorce fall. 4607 - Muhammad ibn Humayd and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir informed us, he said: Musa ibn 'Uqbah informed me, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, while I listened, about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: It is in every Ramadan. So in this report it is in every Ramadan. Some said: This proves it may be in its start and middle as in its end. His saying in every Ramadan may bear this meaning, and may mean in every Ramadan until the Hour. Yet the basis of this report is halted; thus the firm ones narrated it from Abu Ishaq."
"4305","4608 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, the same, and he did not raise it."
"4306","4609 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, and he mentioned the same with its chain. And Abu al-Ahwas narrated this report from Abu Ishaq with other than this wording."
"4307","4610 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn 'Umar about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: It is in all of Ramadan. If this is the wording, it establishes that his saying in every Ramadan means through the whole month. And there was narrated from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite."
"4308","4611 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: Seek it in the last seven of Ramadan."
"4309","4612 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4310","4613 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Zuhri informed me, from the report of Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last seven."
"4311","4614 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4312","4615 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4313","4616 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. And there was narrated from other than Ibn 'Umar also from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"4314","4617 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl narrated to me, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, he said: I asked Abu Dharr: Did you ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Laylat al-Qadr? He said: Yes, I used to ask people about it. 'Ikrimah said: Meaning he asked fully. I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about Laylat al-Qadr; is it in Ramadan or in other than it? He said: In Ramadan. I said: And does it remain with the prophets while they live, and when they are raised it is raised? He said: Rather it is until the Hour. I said: In which Ramadan is it? He said: In the first ten or the last ten. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke and I spoke, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, in which of the twenty is it? He said: Seek it in the last ten; do not ask me about anything after it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke and I spoke, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I adjure you by my right upon you to inform me in which ten it is. He grew angry with me as never before or after, then said: Had Allah willed He would have shown it to you; seek it in the last seven; do not ask me about anything after it."
"4315","4618 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Jabir informed me that 'Abdullah ibn Unays al-Ansari asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Laylat al-Qadr when twenty-two nights had passed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek it in these last seven remaining of the month."
"4316","4619 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, that he was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Seek it tonight, that night being the twenty-third. A man said: Then this is the first of eight. He said: Rather the first of seven, for the month does not complete. So this report also establishes it is in the last seven, and he meant the twenty-third because that month was twenty-nine."
"4317","4620 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, he said: I was sitting with my father at the door when the son of 'Abdullah ibn Unays passed us, and my father said: What did you hear from your father mentioning from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Laylat al-Qadr? He said: I heard my father saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man kept by the desert, so order me a night in which I come to Madinah. He said: Come on the twenty-third night."
"4318","4621 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah, from his brother 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah, a man in 'Umar's time, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Unays sat with us in Juhaynah's gathering at the end of Ramadan, and I said to him: O Abu Yahya, did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about this blessed night? He said: Yes, we sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the end of this month, and we said: O Prophet of Allah, when do we seek this blessed night? He said: Seek it tonight, the evening of the twenty-third. A man of the people said: Then it is the first of eight. He said: It is not the first of eight, but the first of seven; what do you want with a month that does not complete?"
"4319","4622 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, that he informed him from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, he said: We were in the desert, and we said: If we bring our families it will burden us, and if we leave them they will be neglected, so they sent me, and I was the youngest, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I mentioned that to him, and he ordered us the twenty-third night."
"4320","4623 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, he said: Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: I asked Damrah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Unays about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: I heard my father informing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Seek it the twenty-third night. So he used to come down likewise."
"4321","4624 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw myself in Laylat al-Qadr as though prostrating in water and mud. A rainy night befell us, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in dawn prayer, and I saw him prostrating in water and mud, and it was the twenty-third night. As for what we narrated in this chapter from Ibn 'Umar and Abu Dharr: it orders seeking it in the last seven of Ramadan; it may mean in those seven to the exclusion of the rest, or in them while also elsewhere, except it is mostly in those seven, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered seeking there. And there was narrated from Ibn 'Umar also from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he ordered seeking it in the last ten of the month."
"4322","4625 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten of Ramadan."
"4323","4626 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: A man saw Laylat al-Qadr in sleep as though in the last ten, on the twenty-seventh or twenty-ninth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see your dreams have coincided, so seek it in the last ten in the odd. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what Ibn 'Umar narrated ordered seeking it in the last ten, as he ordered in what we narrated before from Ibn 'Umar seeking in the last seven. His ordering the last seven does not negate seeking also in the earlier part of the last ten. What was narrated from Ibn 'Umar does not prove it is in the last seven to the exclusion of the month, though the last seven may have been ordered after the last ten as in Abu Dharr's report, so it is sought in the last seven rather than the rest, and that is seeking not certainty. We wanted to know: Was anything narrated from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating that?"
"4324","4627 - Then Bakr ibn Idris had narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Uqbah ibn Hurayth narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Seek it in the last ten; if one of you is unable and weak, let him not be overcome regarding the remaining seven. So this from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shows it is likelier in the last seven than in the earlier part of the last ten. As for 'Abdullah ibn Unays: it orders seeking it the twenty-third night, and it may be sought in every Ramadan on that very night; if so it may be before the last seven, leaving what was ordered of seeking in the last seven, for the month may be full thirty and that night the first of eight remaining. What clarifies it is what we narrated above from 'Abdullah ibn Unays that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him that in a twenty-nine-day month, so that night was the first of seven not of eight, thus entering what was ordered of seeking in the last seven, all upon seeking not certainty."
"4325","4628 - And Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, he said: Ibn 'Abdullah ibn Unays narrated to me, from his father, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I am in a desert called al-Wat'ah, and, praise Allah, I lead them in prayer, so order me a night of this month in which I come down to the mosque and pray it there. He said: Come down the twenty-third night and pray it there; if you wish complete the month's end, do so, and if you wish refrain. When he prayed 'Asr he would enter the mosque and not leave except for need until he prayed dawn; when he prayed dawn his mount was at the mosque door. So in this report the twenty-third was given in seeking what was not given to the rest of the last seven."
"4326","4629 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Bilal ibn 'Abdullah ibn Unays narrated to me, from his father Bilal ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Atiyyah ibn 'Abdullah, from his father 'Abdullah ibn Unays, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: I saw it then was made to forget it, so seek it in the latter half. Then he returned and asked him, and he said: On the twenty-third passing of the month. 'Abd al-'Aziz said: My father informed me that 'Abdullah ibn Unays would enliven the sixteenth night to the twenty-third, then fall short. So in this report the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to seek it in the month's latter half, then ordered seeking it the twenty-third. So its meaning returns to what we narrated before from 'Abdullah ibn Unays. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have ordered 'Abdullah seeking it that night only for that year, due to the vision the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had seen, though in other years it may be otherwise. As for what was narrated of his vision in Bishr ibn Sa'id's report from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, there was narrated from Abu Sa'id from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite."
"4327","4630 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya narrated to us that Abu Salamah narrated to him, he said: I came to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and said: Did you hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning Laylat al-Qadr? He said: Yes, we observed i'tikaf with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the middle ten of Ramadan; on the morning of the twentieth the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us and said: Whoever went out, let him return, for I was shown the night and was made to forget it, and I saw myself prostrating in water and mud, so seek it in the last ten of Ramadan in the odd. Abu Sa'id said: We saw no wisp in the sky, then at night clouds like mountains came and we were rained upon until the mosque roof flowed, its roof then of palm stalks, until I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrating in water and mud, until I saw the mud's trace on the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nose. Abu Ja'far said: So in this report it was that year the twenty-first night. And that year may be other than the year in which in Ibn Unays's report it was the twenty-third, and that is best to carry both reports upon so they do not contradict."
"4328","4631 - And Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to inform us of Laylat al-Qadr and two men quarreled, and he said: I came out to inform you of Laylat al-Qadr, but so-and-so and so-and-so quarreled and it was raised, and perhaps it is better for you, so seek it in the ninth, seventh and fifth."
"4329","4632 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit and Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. So in this report the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it on a particular night, and after seeing it ordered seeking it thereafter in the ninth, seventh and fifth; so it may be in one year on a particular night, then in later years on another night. That supports our view on Ibn Unays's report. And there was narrated regarding that from Abu Hurayrah what follows."
"4330","4633 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown Laylat al-Qadr, then one of my household woke me and I was made to forget it, so seek it in the remaining ten."
"4331","4634 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown Laylat al-Qadr and made to forget it, so seek it in the remaining ten."
"4332","4635 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten of Ramadan. So in this report the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was made to forget the night he had been shown as Laylat al-Qadr, before that night's coming, and ordered seeking it thereafter in the month's last ten. This differs from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit's report, except it may have been in two years: in one the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw what Abu Hurayrah mentioned before the night's coming, which does not negate its being in coming years earlier in the month, and 'Ubadah's is that in that year the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was apprised of the very night then came out to inform them and it was raised, then ordered seeking it thereafter in the seventh, fifth and ninth, all upon seeking not certainty."
"4333","4636 - And Bahr ibn Nasr had narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten, nine remaining, seven remaining, five remaining. It may mean the year in which he observed i'tikaf and was shown the night then made to forget it, except he knew it was in the odd, so ordered seeking it in every odd of that ten, then the rain came and he inferred it was that year that very night. That proves nothing of its time in coming years, whether that very night or before or after. Or what Abu Nadrah narrated from Abu Sa'id from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may be all years, returning to the meaning of what we narrated above from Ibn 'Umar, except Abu Sa'id's report adds one meaning, that it is only in the odd thereof."
"4334","4637 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from 'Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, from 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last ten of Ramadan, odd. Abu Ja'far said: The discussion here is also like that on Abu Nadrah's report from Abu Sa'id."
"4335","4638 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek it with ten remaining of Ramadan. The discussion here is also like that on Abu Nadrah's report from Abu Sa'id."
"4336","4639 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek it the twenty-seventh night, meaning Laylat al-Qadr."
"4337","4640 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam informed us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4338","4641 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see your dreams have coincided that it is the seventh night of the last ten; whoever seeks it, let him seek it the seventh night of the last ten. It may be in a particular year, and may be in all years likewise, except all upon seeking not certainty. Likewise what we mentioned before from 'Abdullah ibn Unays of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordering him: it may be the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking for it that year for what he had been shown of its time then made to forget. So none of these reports tells us which very night Laylat al-Qadr is, except that in Abu Dharr's report the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him it is the first ten or in the last ten of Ramadan when asked of its time as we mentioned in his report at this chapter's start, thus negating the middle ten, establishing it in one of the two tens, either the first or the last. In this report also Abu Dharr returned asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which of the twenty it is, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered to seek it in the last ten. So we looked into what was narrated in other than these reports: Is there what indicates it is on a particular night of these two tens?"
"4339","4642 - Then Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from al-Sunabihi, from Bilal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Laylat al-Qadr is the twenty-fourth night. So in this report it is on that very night, and there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite."
"4340","4643 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn 'Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Ibn Thawban, he said: 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah narrated to me, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Laylat al-Qadr is the twenty-seventh night, and its sign is that the sun rises with no rays as though a basin."
"4341","4644 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah narrated to me, he said: Zirr ibn Hubaysh narrated to me, he said: I heard Ubayy ibn Ka'b when it reached him that Ibn Mas'ud said: Whoever stands the whole year attains Laylat al-Qadr, and Ubayy said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, it is in Ramadan; by Allah besides whom there is no god, I know which night it is; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to stand it the night of the morning of the twenty-seventh."
"4342","4645 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: 'Abdullah used to say regarding Laylat al-Qadr: Whoever stands the year attains it, and he said: May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman; by the One sworn by, he knew it is in Ramadan, and it is the twenty-seventh night. He said: When I saw him swearing without exception I said: What taught you that? He said: By the sign the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us of. So we reckoned and counted, and it was the twenty-seventh night, meaning the sun has no rays. Abu Ja'far said: So Ubayy ibn Ka'b informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is the twenty-seventh night, and negates 'Abdullah's saying whoever stands the year attains it. Yet there was narrated from 'Abdullah regarding Laylat al-Qadr that it is in Ramadan as Ubayy swore 'Abdullah knew, but contrary to the twenty-seventh."
"4343","4646 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Hujayr al-Taghlibi, from al-Aswad, from 'Abdullah, he said: Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the nineteenth night of Ramadan, whose morning is the morning of Badr, otherwise on the twenty-first or twenty-third. As for what we mentioned from 'Abdullah that it is the nineteenth, what Abu Dharr narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is in the twenty days of the month, first and last, negated it. And there was narrated from 'Abdullah also regarding that what follows."
"4344","4647 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn 'Amr ibn Ja'dah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: Which of you remembers the night of the reddish clouds? 'Abdullah said: I, by Allah, may my father and mother be for you, O Messenger of Allah, with dates in my hand with which I took suhur, screened by my saddle's back from the dawn, when the dawn rises. So in this report when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Laylat al-Qadr he told them which night it is, that it is the night of the reddish clouds, and 'Abdullah described it with the moonlight at dawn's rise, which is only at the month's end. So that also supports what Ubayy said. And in Allah's Book is what indicates Laylat al-Qadr is in Ramadan particularly: Allah said Ha Mim, by the clear Book, We sent it down in a blessed night, and therein is distinguished every wise matter; so Allah informed that the night in which every wise matter is distinguished is Laylat al-Qadr, the night in which the Qur'an was sent down, then He said the month of Ramadan in which the Qur'an was sent down. So that night is in Ramadan, and we needed to know which of its nights. What indicates it is what we narrated from Bilal from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is the twenty-fourth, and what was narrated from Ubayy ibn Ka'b from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is the twenty-seventh. And there was narrated from Mu'awiyah also from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like what was narrated from Ubayy from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4345","4648 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Mutarrif ibn 'Abdullah narrating from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Laylat al-Qadr; he said: The twenty-seventh night. This is the utmost we reached of the knowledge of which night Laylat al-Qadr is from what Allah's Book and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Sunnah indicated. As for what was narrated after from the Companions and their Successors, its meaning enters the meanings we mentioned. We needed to mention what was narrated of Laylat al-Qadr because our companions differed regarding a man's saying to his wife: You are divorced on Laylat al-Qadr, when divorce falls by it. Abu Hanifah said: If he says that before Ramadan, divorce does not fall until Ramadan passes, for Laylat al-Qadr's place among Ramadan's nights was disputed as we mentioned in this chapter, some narrating it is in the whole month and some in part of it. He said: So I do not rule divorce's falling except after the month's passing, for then I know the time in which he placed divorce has passed and divorce has fallen. He said: And if he says that in Ramadan, at its start, end or middle, divorce does not fall until the rest of that month passes and until all Ramadan of the coming year also passes. He said: For it may be in what passed of this month he is in, so divorce does not fall until all Ramadan of the coming year passes; and it may be in what remains of the month he is in, and divorce falls therein, like one who said before Ramadan: You are divorced on Laylat al-Qadr, and divorce is not ruled against him except after Ramadan's passing. He said: Since that is unclear, I do not rule divorce's falling except after my knowledge of its falling, and I know that only after the passing of the Ramadan he is in and the coming Ramadan after it. This is Abu Hanifah's view in this chapter. And Abu Yusuf once said this too, and once said: When he says that in part of Ramadan, divorce is not ruled until the like time of Ramadan of the coming year passes. He said: For then a full year is complete since he said it, and it is in every year, so our knowledge thereby is divorce's falling. Abu Ja'far said: This view in my view is nothing, for it was not said to us that every year contains Laylat al-Qadr, since that year does not contain a full Ramadan of one year; rather it was said to us it is in Ramadan of every year, thus Allah's Book indicated and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told us as we mentioned above in this chapter. Since it is so, when he says to her in part of Ramadan: You are divorced on Laylat al-Qadr, Laylat al-Qadr may be in what passed of that month. So when a year passes from then to its like of Ramadan of the coming year, there is no Laylat al-Qadr in it. So Abu Yusuf's described view is corrupted by what we mentioned, and upon this order Abu Hanifah's view stands. And Abu Yusuf once said: When he says it in part of Ramadan, divorce does not fall until the twenty-seventh night passes. He went in that, as we see, and Allah knows best, to that what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of its being on a particular night of Ramadan is Bilal's report and Ubayy ibn Ka'b's. So when the twenty-seventh passes it is known Laylat al-Qadr occurred and divorce's falling is ruled; and it was said that, so it is not knowledge of its being, likewise divorce's falling was not ruled. And this view is witnessed by the reports we narrated in this chapter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4346","Chapter: divorce of the coerced. 4649 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i informed us, from 'Ata', from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has overlooked for me from my nation mistake, forgetfulness and what they were coerced into. Abu Ja'far said: Some held that when a man is coerced into divorce, marriage, oath, manumission or the like until he does it coerced, all of that is void, for it entered what Allah overlooked for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his nation, and they argued with this report. Others differed, saying: Rather what he swore in coercion of oaths binds him, and his divorce, manumission, marriage and return to his divorced wife if he returned to her take effect. They interpreted in this report other than the first party's meaning, saying: That is only in polytheism, for the people were new to disbelief in a land of disbelief, and when the polytheists overcame them they coerced them to affirm disbelief and they affirmed with their tongues; they did that with 'Ammar ibn Yasir and others of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Companions, and regarding them was revealed except one coerced while his heart is secure in faith. And perhaps from inattention they spoke what their pre-Islam habit ran upon, and perhaps by mistake they spoke it too, so Allah overlooked it for them, for they did not choose it nor intend it. And Abu Yusuf also went to this interpretation; al-Kaysani narrated it to us from his father. The report bears this meaning and bears what the first party said; since it bears that, we needed to uncover its meanings to indicate one of the two interpretations to divert this report's meaning to. We found mistake is what a man meant other than, and did without intending or willing it, and inattention is what he intended and did intending it while inattentive to the meaning barring that act. When a man forgets that this woman is his wife and intends her and divorces her, all agree his divorce operates and do not void it for his inattention, and that inattention does not enter the excused inattention. Since the excused inattention contains none of the divorce, oaths and manumission we mentioned, so likewise the excused coercion contains none of that either. So the view of those who entered divorce, manumission and oaths therein is corrupted. And the holders of the first view also argued for their saying with what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4347","4650 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, that he heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab upon the pulpit saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Actions are only by intentions, and each man has only what he intended; so whoever's migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration is to Allah and His Messenger, and whoever's migration was to a worldly gain or to a woman to marry, his migration is to what he migrated to."
"4348","4651 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned the same with its chain. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said actions are by intentions, it is established that no act of divorce, manumission or other takes effect without intention; the others' proof is that this speech was not meant for the opponent's meaning, but for acts by which reward is due. Do you not see him saying: Actions are by intentions, and each man has only what he intended, meaning of reward, then he said: So whoever's migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration is to Allah and His Messenger, and whoever's migration was to a worldly gain or to a woman to marry, his migration is to what he migrated to. So that is only an answer to a question the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked of what the migrant has in his act, that is in his migration, and he said: Actions are only by intentions, until the speech in the report, and that has nothing to do with coercion upon divorce, manumission, return and oaths. So this report also ceases to be a proof for the first-mentioned party against the second. And among what the second party argued for their mentioned view is what follows."
"4349","4652 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Jumay', he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us, he said: Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Nothing kept me from witnessing Badr except that my father and I went out and the Quraysh disbelievers seized us, and said: You mean Muhammad. We said: We mean only Madinah. They took from us Allah's covenant and pact that we return to Madinah and not fight with him, so we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: Turn away, fulfilling their pacts for them, and we seek Allah's help against them."
"4350","4653 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to me, from al-Walid, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Hudhayfah, he said: My father Husayl and I went out meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned similar. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barred them from attending Badr due to the overpowering polytheists' oath upon them, it is established that an oath upon willingness and coercion is alike, and likewise divorce and manumission. And this is best done with reports when the meanings of some are stood upon, to carry the rest upon what does not oppose that meaning whenever possible, so they do not contradict. So by what we mentioned it is established that Ibn 'Abbas's report is in polytheism and Hudhayfah's in divorce, oaths and the like. As for its ruling by reason: a man's coerced act is of one of two faces: either the coerced one who does it coerced is in the ruling of one who did not do it, so nothing is due, or in the ruling of one who did it, so what is due upon a willing doer is due. We examined and saw no disagreement that when a husband coerces his wife while she fasts in Ramadan or in Hajj and has intercourse with her, her Hajj is voided and likewise her fast, and coercion was not considered to distinguish it from willingness, nor was the woman placed as one who did nothing; rather she was placed as one who did an act upon which ruling is due, and only sin was lifted from her therein. And likewise had a man coerced a man to intercourse with a woman compelled to it, the dower in reason is upon the intercourse-doer not the coercer, and the doer does not recover it from the coercer, for the coercer did not have intercourse, so a dower is due for his intercourse, and what is due in that intercourse is upon the doer not another. Since in these things the coerced is ruled with the doer's ruling, obliging monies upon him as upon the willing doer, so likewise the coerced divorcer, manumitter and returner is ruled with the doer's ruling and all his acts bind. If one says: Why did you not allow his sale and hire? It is said to him: We saw sales and hires revoked by defects, by the option of inspection and by stipulated option, while marriage is not so, nor divorce, return nor manumission. What may be undone by stipulated option and by the causes in its basis of unseen inspection and revocation by defects is undone by coercion, and what must not be undone after established is not undone by coercion or otherwise. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. And we saw the Sunnah bring the like of this."
"4351","4654 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Habib ibn Ardak narrated to us that he heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah saying: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Three matters, their seriousness is seriousness and their jest is seriousness: marriage, divorce, and taking back (the wife)."
"4352","4655 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib and Asad narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Habib ibn Ardak, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Mahak, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4353","4656 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Habib ibn Ardak, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Mahak, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, Three, their seriousness is seriousness and their jest is seriousness, he prevented marriage from being nullified after its occurrence, and likewise divorce and taking back. And we did not see sales treated upon that meaning; rather they were treated upon its opposite: whoever sold jestingly, his sale is void, and whoever leased jestingly, his lease is void. That, in our view - and Allah knows best - is only because sales and leases are among things annulled by the causes we mentioned, so they are annulled by jest as they are annulled by that. And the other matters - divorce, manumission, and taking back - are not nullified by any of that, so they were made not rejected by jest. Likewise also by reasoning: whatever is annulled by the causes we mentioned is annulled by coercion, and whatever is not annulled by those causes is not annulled by coercion. And that has been narrated from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"4354","4657 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Allaf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Sawaa narrated to us, he said: Abu Sinan narrated to us, he said: I heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz saying: The divorce of the intoxicated and the coerced is valid."
"4355","Chapter 9: A man denying the pregnancy of his wife as being from him. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a man denies the pregnancy of his wife as being from him, the judge performs lian between her and him on account of that pregnancy, attaches it to its mother, and separates the woman from her husband. They argued with a hadith narrated by Abdah ibn Sulayman from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed lian on account of pregnancy. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, held this view once, and it is not the well-known view from him. Others disagreed with them; they said: There is no lian on account of pregnancy, because it is possible that it is not a pregnancy, for what appears of the woman from which pregnancy is surmised does not establish pregnancy with certainty; it is only surmise, and denial of the surmised does not necessitate lian. And among the proof for them against the people of the first view is that the hadith they used against them is an abridged hadith; the one who narrated it abridged it and erred in it. Its origin is only that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed lian between them while she was pregnant; that, in our view, is lian on account of accusation, not lian on account of denying pregnancy, so the one who narrated it imagined that it was lian on account of pregnancy and abridged the hadith as we mentioned. And the origin of the hadith in that is what: 4658 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: While we were one evening in the mosque, a man said: If one of us sees a man with his wife, if he kills him you will kill him, if he speaks you will flog him, and if he stays silent he stays silent in rage! I shall surely ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he asked him and said: O Messenger of Allah, if one of us sees a man with his wife, if he kills him you will kill him, if he speaks you will flog him, and if he stays silent he stays silent in rage! O Allah, judge. Then the verse of lian was revealed. Abdullah said: That man was the first to be tested with it."
"4356","4659 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hakim ibn Sayf narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: A man stood in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday night and said: What do you think if a man finds a man with his wife! Then he mentioned similar, and added in it: Abdullah said: So he was tested with it; he was a man of the Ansar who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and performed lian with his wife. When his wife began to invoke curses, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Mah! So she invoked them. When she turned away, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps she will bring him black and curly-haired! So she brought him black and curly-haired."
"4357","4660 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from al-Amash - then he mentioned with his isnad the same. So this is the origin of the hadith of Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning lian, and it is lian on account of an accusation made by that man against his wife while she was pregnant, not on account of her pregnancy. And others besides Ibn Masud, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, also narrated it in that manner."
"4358","4661 - Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, he said: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed lian between al-Ajlani and his wife, and she was pregnant. Her husband said: By Allah, I have not approached her since we afarna. And al-afr is watering the date-palms after leaving them without watering, after the wells, by two months. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make clear. They claimed that the womans husband was thin in the forearms and shanks, reddish-blond in hair, and that she had been accused with Ibn al-Sahma. He said: So she gave birth to a black curly-haired boy, very curly, thick in the forearms, fleshy in the shanks. Al-Qasim said: Ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had said: O Abu Abbas, is she the woman about whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I stoned without proof I would have stoned her? Ibn Abbas said: No, but that was a woman who had openly committed immorality in Islam."
"4359","4662 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Qasim, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - similar."
"4360","4663 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: My father narrated to me that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to him from Ibn Abbas - the same, except that he did not mention the question of Abdullah ibn Shaddad, to the end of this hadith."
"4361","4664 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have had no contact with my wife since we afarna the date-palms, and I found a man with my wife. And her husband was lean, thin-limbed, lank-haired, while the one she was accused with tended to blackness, curly and very curly-haired (severely curly or beautifully so). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make clear. Then he performed lian between them, and she brought him resembling the one she was accused with."
"4362","4665 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Makhlad ibn Husayn, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, that Hilal ibn Umayyah accused Sharik ibn Sahma concerning his wife, and raised that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Bring four witnesses, otherwise a hadd (flogging) on your back. He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful! He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying to him: Four, otherwise a hadd on your back. He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful - saying that repeatedly - and Allah will surely reveal to you what will clear my back of flogging. Then the verse of lian was revealed: And those who accuse their wives and have no witnesses except themselves. He said: So Hilal was called and bore four testimonies by Allah that he was among the truthful, and the fifth that the curse of Allah be upon him if he were among the liars. He said: Then the woman was called and bore four testimonies by Allah that he was among the liars; when it came to the fifth, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stop her, for it is decisive. He said: So she hesitated until we had no doubt she would confess, then she said: I will not disgrace my people for the rest of the day. So she went through with the oath. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch: if she brings him white, lank-haired, with flowing eyes, then he is Hilal ibn Umayyahs, and if she brings him dark-eyed, curly-haired, thin-shanked, then he is Sharik ibn Sahmas. He said: So she brought him dark-eyed, curly-haired, thin-shanked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for what preceded in the Book of Allah Almighty, I and she would have had a matter. He said: Qadi al-aynayn means long in the hair of the eyes, not wide-open-eyed."
"4363","4666 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Anas ibn Malik, that Hilal ibn Umayyah accused his wife with Sharik ibn Sahma. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch her: if she brings him white, lank-haired, with flowing eyes, then he is Hilal ibn Umayyahs, and if she brings him dark-eyed, curly-haired, thin-shanked, then he is Sharik ibn Sahmas. So she brought him dark-eyed, curly-haired, thin-shanked."
"4364","4667 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (Ha.) 4668 - And Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us; they said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, that Uwaymir came to Asim ibn Adi and said: What do you think of a man who finds a man with his wife and kills him - will you kill him for it? Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me, O Asim. So Asim came and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the question, and he disapproved of it. Uwaymir said: By Allah, I shall surely come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Allah has revealed Quran concerning you. So he called them both, they stepped forward and invoked curses upon each other. Then he said: I have lied against her, O Messenger of Allah, if I keep her! So he parted from her, though the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not order him to part from her; thus the sunnah continued for the two invoking curses. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch: if she brings him red, short, like a waharah (small lizard), then I see him only as having lied against her; and if she brings him large-eyed, wide-eyed, with large buttocks, then I consider him only as having told the truth against her. He said: So she brought him in the disliked manner. So it is established by what we mentioned that there is no proof in any of that for one who makes lian obligatory on account of pregnancy. If one says: In the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), If she brings him such then he is her husbands, and if she brings him such then he is so-and-sos, there is proof that the pregnancy is what is intended by the accusation and lian - our answer to him is that had lian been on account of pregnancy, it would have been negated from the husband, attached to him or not, whether it resembled him or not. Do you not see that had she given birth before he accused her, and he denied her child though it was the most resembling of people to him, lian would be performed between them, they would be separated, the child would be attached to its mother and would not be attached to the one invoking lian due to resemblance to him. Since resemblance does not establish lineage nor does its absence negate lineage, and in the hadith we mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she brings him such then he is for the one who invoked curses against her - that proves lian did not negate him; for had it negated him, his resemblance to him would not be proof he is from him, nor the remoteness of his resemblance proof he is from another. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the bedouin who asked him, saying: My wife gave birth to a black boy: What Yunus narrated to us - Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that a bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My wife gave birth to a black boy and I deny him. He said to him: Do you have camels? He said: Yes. He said: What are their colours? He said: Red. He said: Is there any grey among them? He said: There are grey ones among them. He said: How do you think that came to them? He said: O Messenger of Allah, a strain drew it out. He said: Perhaps this one is a strain that drew him out."
"4365","4669 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik and Ibn Abi Dhib and Sufyan informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same. So since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit him to deny him due to the remoteness of his resemblance to him, and resemblance is no proof of anything, it is established that the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigning the child of the woman subjected to lian to her husband if she brings him resembling him is proof that lian had not negated him from him. So the corruption of what those who hold lian on account of pregnancy argued is established by what we mentioned. And in that is another proof: in the hadith of Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Watch her: if she brings him such, I see him only as having lied against her; and if she brings him such, I see him only as having told the truth against her - so that statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon conjecture, not certainty, which also proves that no ruling at all proceeded from him concerning the pregnancy. So the corruption of the view of one who goes to lian on account of pregnancy is established. We only argued with it for one who went to its opposite at the beginning of this chapter, among those who refuse lian on account of pregnancy, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah and Muhammad and the well-known view of Abu Yusuf."
"4366","Chapter 10: A man denying the child of his wife when it is born - does he perform lian on account of it or not? 4670 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us. (Ha.) 4671 - And Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Yaqub, from al-Hasan ibn Sad - Rabi said in his hadith: the freedman of al-Hasan ibn Ali - from Rabah, he said: I came to Uthman ibn Affan and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that the child belongs to the bed."
"4367","4672 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone."
"4368","4673 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4369","4674 - Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4370","4675 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, he heard Umar saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed the child for the bed. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a man denies the child of his wife, it is not negated by him and he does not perform lian on account of it. They argued with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, and said: The bed establishes the childs right to firm lineage from the husband and the wife, so they cannot remove him from it by lian or otherwise. Others disagreed with them; they said: Rather he performs lian on account of it, his lineage is negated and he is attached to his mother, when he had not acknowledged him and there was nothing from him whose ruling is that of acknowledgment, and that was not prolonged. They argued with what."
"4371","4676 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated between the two invoking curses and attached the child to its mother. They said: This is a sunnah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we know nothing opposing it nor abrogating it. So we knew by it that the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), The child belongs to the bed, does not negate that lian on account of it is obligatory when denied, since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that. His Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, after him agreed upon what they ruled concerning the inheritance of the son of the woman subjected to lian: they made him fatherless, made him from his mothers people, and removed him from the people of the one subjected to lian. Then their followers after them agreed upon that. Then people remained upon that until this opponent to them deviated. The view, in our opinion, is upon what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, after him, and their followers after them did, as we mentioned, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allahs mercy be upon them all."
"4372","Book 9: The Book of Manumission. Chapter 1: A slave owned between two men, one of them manumits him. 4677 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever manumits a share of his in a slave guarantees to his partners their portions."
"4373","4678 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever manumits a slave between him and his partners, its value is assessed upon him and it is freed."
"4374","4679 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever manumits a portion of his of a slave or bondwoman, what remains is borne upon him in his wealth, until all of him is freed entirely. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a slave is between two men and one of them manumits his portion, he guarantees the value of his partners portion, whether solvent or insolvent. They said: Manumission by the partner has been made an offense against his partners portion, for which guarantee of its value in his wealth is due; and whoever offends against a mans wealth, solvent or insolvent, guarantee of what he destroyed by his offense is due, and his ruling does not differ whether solvent or insolvent in the obligation of guarantee upon him. They said: Likewise, when guarantee of his partners portions value became due upon the partner for his manumission when solvent, guarantee of that is also due upon him when insolvent. Others disagreed with them; they said: Guarantee of his partners portions value for his manumission is due upon him only if solvent. They said: This hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the guarantee mentioned in it is upon the solvent specifically, not the insolvent; that has been clarified from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in other than these reports. What was narrated from him in that is what:"
"4375","4680 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever manumits a share of his in a slave, and he has wealth reaching the price of the slave, the value of the slave is assessed upon him, his partners are given their portions, and the slave is freed upon him; otherwise, there is freed upon him what was freed."
"4376","4681 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever manumits a share of his in a slave, and the one manumitting his portion has what reaches his price, then he is entirely freed."
"4377","4682 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah and Abdullah Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever manumits a share of his in a slave, upon him is his entire freeing if he has wealth reaching his price; if he has no wealth, he is assessed a just value upon the manumitter, and there is freed by it what was freed."
"4378","4683 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever manumits a share of his in a slave, all of him is freed; if the one who freed him has wealth reaching his price, upon him is his entire freeing."
"4379","4684 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, he said: Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to give fatwa concerning a slave or bondwoman owned between partners, when one of them manumits his portion of him: freeing becomes obligatory upon the one who freed if he has wealth reaching his price; he is assessed in his wealth a just value, pays his partners their portions, and frees the slave; Abdullah ibn Umar reports that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4380","4685 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a slave is between two and one of them manumits his portion, if solvent, it is assessed upon him at the highest value, then he is freed. Sufyan said: And Amr ibn Dinar sometimes said: a just value, with neither undervaluation nor excess. So by authentication of these reports it is established that what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only concerning the solvent specifically. So we wanted to examine the ruling of the insolvents manumission, how is it? Some said: The statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), otherwise there is freed of him what was freed, proves what remains of the slave is not touched by manumission; he remains a slave of the one who did not manumit as before. Others disagreed; they said: Rather the slave labours for half his value for the one who did not manumit. Among their proof is that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated, and added something clarifying the ruling of what remains of the slave after the manumitters portion."
"4381","4686 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever manumits a portion or share of his in a slave, upon him in his wealth is his complete freeing; if he has no wealth, the slave labours without hardship upon him."
"4382","4687 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Qatadah - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4383","4688 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Qatadah - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4384","4689 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Qatadah - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4385","4690 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4386","4691 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah and Yahya ibn Subayh, from Qatadah - then he mentioned with his isnad the same. So this hadith contains what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, and in it is the obligation of labour upon the slave when his manumitter is insolvent. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what:"
"4387","4692 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, that a man manumitted a portion of his in a slave, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed all of him upon him and said: Allah has no partner."
"4388","4693 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us - then he mentioned with his isnad the same. So the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah has no partner, proves that when part of the slave becomes due to Allah by manumission, no ownership for another remains upon its rest; thus it is established that manumission by the solvent and the insolvent both clear the slave of bondage. So this hadith also agrees with the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and the hadith of Abu Hurayrah adds over it and over the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the obligation of labour for the partner who did not manumit when the manumitter is insolvent. So authentication of these reports necessitates acting upon that: guarantee upon the solvent manumitter for his partner who did not manumit, and no guarantee upon the insolvent manumitter; rather the slave labours for that for the partner who did not manumit. This is the view of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, and by it we take. As for Abu Hanifah, may Allah be pleased with him, he used to say: If the manumitter is solvent, the partner has the choice: if he wishes he manumits as he manumitted and wala is between them in halves; if he wishes he makes the slave labour for half the value, and when he pays it he is freed and wala is between them in halves; if he wishes he holds the manumitter liable for half the value, and when he pays it he is freed, and the one held liable returns upon the slave for it and makes him labour for it, and his wala is for the manumitter. If the manumitter is insolvent, the partner has the choice: if he wishes he manumits, and if he wishes he makes the slave labour for half his value; whichever he does, wala is between them in halves. He argued with what:"
"4389","4694 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: We had a boy who had witnessed al-Qadisiyyah and distinguished himself in it; he was between me and my mother and my brother al-Aswad. They wanted to free him, and I was then small. Al-Aswad mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, and he said: Free you; when Abd al-Rahman reaches maturity, if he desires what you desired he frees, otherwise he holds you liable. So in this hadith, Abd al-Rahman after maturity may manumit his portion of the slave already touched by the manumission of his mother and brother before that. Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Since he may manumit without compensation, he may take the slave by payment of the value of what remains for him in him until he is freed by payment of that to him. And since the one who did not manumit may manumit his portion of the slave, the manumitting partner held liable returns to this one held liable from this slave the like of what the one he held liable had; so he must make the slave labour for the value of what belonged to his companion in him and in what his companion might make him labour for. This is the school of Abu Hanifah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, in this chapter. The first view, held by Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, is the sounder of the two in our view, for agreeing with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah knows best."
"4390","Chapter 2: A man owning a relative unmarriageable to him - is he freed upon him or not? 4695 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No child repays his father except that he finds him enslaved and buys him and frees him."
"4391","4696 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Sufyan - he is al-Thawri. (Ha.) 4697 - And Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4392","4698 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Suhayl - then he mentioned with his isnad the same. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that whoever owns his father is not freed upon him until he frees him. Others disagreed; they said: He is freed upon him by his owning him. Among their proof is that this statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bears what they said, and bears: so he buys him and frees him by his buying - this is correct in speech, and it is the worthiest interpretation of this hadith, so that it agrees with other than it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. For:"
"4393","4699 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Umayr ibn al-Nahhas narrated to us, he said: Damrah narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns an unmarriageable relative is free."
"4394","4700 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj and Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns an unmarriageable relative is free."
"4395","4701 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us. (Ha.) 4702 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us - then he mentioned with his isnad."
"4396","4703 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns an unmarriageable relative is free. So authentication of these two hadiths of Samurah necessitates that the relative mentioned in them is the unmarriageable relative, and that the relative mentioned in them is the mahram among relatives; their combined meaning is like what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Whoever owns an unmarriageable relative is free. It has reached me that Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani used to narrate from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns a relative among mahram is free. That supports what we mentioned. And there has been narrated from those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among his Companions and their followers, may Allah be pleased with them, what agrees with this also."
"4397","4704 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, he said: Whoever owns an unmarriageable relative is free."
"4398","4705 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Mustawrid, that a man married his brothers son to his bondwoman, and she bore children; he wanted to enslave her children. So his brothers son came to Abdullah ibn Masud and said: My uncle married me to his slave-girl, and she bore me children, and he wants to enslave my children! Abdullah said: He lies; that is not his."
"4399","4706 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: When a man owns his paternal aunt or maternal aunt or brother or sister, they are freed, even if he does not free them."
"4400","4707 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us - Abu Jafar said, I think: from Hajjaj, from Ata and al-Shabi - the same. He said: And Ibrahim said: None is freed except the father and the child. So when we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned, and what we narrated from his Companions and their followers, may Allah be pleased with them, agreed with it, and we knew no disagreement from their likes in that, it became obligatory to take what was narrated from them and leave their disagreement. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allahs mercy be upon them all."
"4401","Chapter 3: The mukatab - when is he freed? 4708 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The mukatab is compensated for the portion he paid as blood-money of a freeman, and what remains as blood-money of a slave."
"4402","4709 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas."
"4403","4710 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed concerning a mukatab killed, with the blood-money of a freeman according to what was freed of him. Ibn Abbas said: And the hadd of a slave is applied upon the mukatab."
"4404","4711 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mukatab is compensated according to what he paid as blood-money of a freeman, and according to what remains enslaved of him as blood-money of a slave. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that the mukatab is freed of him according to what he paid, his ruling in it being that of a freeman, and his ruling in what he did not pay being that of a slave. They argued with this hadith. Others disagreed; they said: The mukatab is not freed except by payment of all the kitabah. They argued with what."
"4405","4712 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sulaym, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mukatab is a slave as long as a dirham of his kitabah remains upon him. So these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed; we looked at what was narrated from his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, in that."
"4406","4713 - So Ali ibn Shaybah: narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, from Mabad al-Juhani, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The mukatab is a slave as long as a dirham remains upon him."
"4407","4714 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Samurah, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the mukatab pays half, he is a creditor (i.e. treated as free in liability)."
"4408","4715 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Samurah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: O people, you contract kitabah with mukatabs; whichever of them pays half, there is no return for him to bondage. So this contradicts what we narrated before it from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4409","4716 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Imran ibn Bashir, from Salim Sabalan, that he said to Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do you not feel shy of me? She said: What is with you? He said: I contracted kitabah. She said: You are a slave as long as anything remains upon you."
"4410","4717 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah and Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: I sought permission from Aishah; she said: How much remains upon you of your kitabah? I said: Ten uqiyyahs. She said: Enter; for you are a slave as long as anything remains upon you."
"4411","4718 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Amr ibn Maymun informed us - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4412","4719 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah said: When the mukatab pays a third or a quarter, he is a creditor (treated as free in liability)."
"4413","4720 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah said: When the mukatab pays the value of his neck, he is a creditor."
"4414","4721 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: Abdullah and Shurayh used to say concerning the mukatab: when he pays a third, he is a creditor."
"4415","4722 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi informed me, from Abu Mashar, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, that Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The mukatab is a slave as long as anything of his kitabah remains upon him."
"4416","4723 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd and Malik informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The mukatab is a slave as long as anything of his kitabah remains upon him."
"4417","4724 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: The mukatab is a slave as long as anything of his kitabah remains upon him. And Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: Their conditions are valid among them. So since they differed in that as we mentioned, and all agreed the mukatab is not freed by the kitabah contract but is freed by a second state - some said that state is payment of all the kitabah, some said it is payment of part of the kitabah, and some said he is freed of him according to what he paid of the kitabah wealth - it is established that its ruling has left the ruling of one freed for wealth; for one freed for wealth is freed by the statement before paying anything, while the mukatab is not so, by their agreement upon what we mentioned. So since it is established the mukatab does not deserve freeing by the kitabah contract but deserves it by a second state, we examined that and all things not due by contracts but due by another state after them - how is their ruling? We saw a man sells a man a slave for a thousand dirhams: the buyer is not entitled to take the slave by the contract itself until he pays all the price, and he has no taking of part of the slave by paying part of the price. Likewise things withheld for others, like a pledge withheld for debt: all agreed that had the pledgor paid the pledgee part of the debt and wanted to take the pledge or part of it according to what he paid of the debt, he has not that except by paying all the debt. So this was the ruling of things owned for things when their withholding is due: they are withheld until all what was made compensation for them is taken. So when the mukatab left being in the ruling of one freed for wealth who is freed by the contract not by a second state, and it is established he is in the ruling of one withheld for payment of something, it is established his ruling in kitabah and in his masters withholding him is as the ruling of the sold in the sellers withholding it. Just as the buyer is unable to take it except after payment of all the price, so likewise the mukatab is unable to take anything of his neck out of the masters ownership except by payment of all the kitabah. So it is established by what we mentioned the view of those who said: nothing of the mukatab is freed except by payment of all the kitabah; and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allahs mercy be upon them all."
"4418","Chapter 4: A bondwoman her master has intercourse with, then he dies, she having brought a child in his lifetime - is he his son and is she thereby umm walad or not? 4725 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas entrusted to his brother - i.e. willed to him - Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, that the son of the slave-girl of Zamah is from me, so take him to you. When it was the year of the Conquest, Sad took him and said: My brothers son; he had entrusted him to me. Abd ibn Zamah stood to him and said: My brother, son of my fathers slave-girl, born upon his bed. So they brought the dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, my brothers son; he had entrusted him to me. Abd ibn Zamah said: My brother, son of my fathers slave-girl, born upon my fathers bed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Sawdah bint Zamah: Veil yourself from him; due to what he saw in him of resemblance to Utbah. So she complied, and he did not see her until he met Allah Almighty. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a master has intercourse with a bondwoman, every child she brings after that is attached to him, whether he claims him or not. They argued with this hadith; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah, then said: The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attached him to Zamah, not for his sons claim - for a sons claim of lineage for another from his father is not accepted - but because his mother was a bed for Zamah by his intercourse with her. They also argued with what."
"4419","4726 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, said: What is with men who have intercourse with their slave-girls then practice coitus interruptus with them! No slave-girl shall come to me whose master acknowledges he has been with her except that I attach her child to him; so practice coitus interruptus or leave it."
"4420","4727 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Umar said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying - then he mentioned the same."
"4421","4728 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: What is with men who have intercourse with their slave-girls then let them go out! No slave-girl shall come to me whose master acknowledges he has been with her except that I attach her child to him; so send them out afterwards or keep them."
"4422","4729 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever has intercourse with a bondwoman then neglects her and sends her out, then she gives birth - the child is his and the neglect is upon him. Nafi said: So this is the judgment of Umar ibn al-Khattab and the view of Ibn Umar. Others disagreed with them; they said: Whatever child this bondwoman brings is not attached to her master except that he acknowledges him; if he dies before acknowledging him, he is not attached to him. Among their proof concerning the first hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said to Abd ibn Zamah: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah - and did not say: He is your brother. So it is possible he meant by His is yours, i.e. he is owned by you, by the owners right of possession over him, and he did not rule anything concerning his lineage. The proof of that is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sawdah bint Zamah to veil from him. Had the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made him the son of Zamah, he would not have veiled Zamahs daughter from him; for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not order cutting kinship but would order joining it, and among joining it is visiting - so how could he order her, having made him her brother, to veil from him! This is not permissible for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And how is that permissible for him when he orders Aishah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with her, to admit her foster uncle to her, then veils Sawdah from one he made her brother and her fathers son! Rather its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is he did not rule in him anything besides possession, by which he made him for Abd ibn Zamah and the rest of Zamahs heirs, not Sad. If one says: What is the meaning of his statement joined to this: The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone? It is said to him: That is instruction from him to Sad; i.e. you claim for your brother, while your brother had no bed - lineage is established from him only had he a bed; when he has no bed, he is an adulterer, and for the adulterer is the stone. What has clarified and uncovered this meaning is what:"
"4423","4730 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Zamah had a slave-girl he used to have intercourse with, and another man was suspected of visiting her. Zamah died while she was pregnant, and she gave birth to a boy resembling the man suspected of her. Sawdah mentioned him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: As for inheritance, it is his; as for you, veil yourself from him, for he is not a brother to you. So in this hadith, Zamah used to have intercourse with that bondwoman, yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Sawdah: He is not a brother to you - meaning the son of the one had intercourse with. So this proves the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not decree his lineage upon Zamah by anything, and that Zamahs intercourse was not, in his view, necessitating that what that one had intercourse with brings of a child is from him. If one says: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for inheritance, it is his - this proves his decree of his lineage. It is said to him: That does not prove what you mentioned; for Abd ibn Zamah had claimed him and alleged he is his fathers son - for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed in her hadith we mentioned from her at the beginning of this chapter that Abd ibn Zamah said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Sad ibn Abi Waqqas disputed him: My brother; son of my fathers slave-girl, born upon my fathers bed. So Sawdah may have said the like, and they are Zamahs heirs; they acknowledged for him inheritance from what Zamah left. That was permissible against them in the wealth that would have been theirs had they not acknowledged what they acknowledged - but no establishment of lineage follows by which a ruling is due that he be left to look at Sawdah. If one says: Her veiling from him was only for what he saw of his resemblance to Utbah, as in the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. It is said to him: This cannot be so; for existence of resemblance does not establish lineage, nor does its absence negate lineage. Do you not see the man who said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that his wife gave birth to a black boy? He said: What do you see, whence did that come to her? He said: From a strain that drew him out. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps this is from a strain that drew him out! We have mentioned this hadith with its isnad in (the chapter of lian). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit him to deny him due to remoteness of his resemblance to him, nor did he prevent him from admitting him to his daughters and household; rather he struck for him a similitude informing him that resemblance does not necessitate establishment of lineages and its absence does not necessitate negation of lineages. So likewise the son of Zamahs slave-girl: had Zamahs intercourse with his mother necessitated establishment of his lineage from him, remoteness of his resemblance to him would have no meaning, and his lineage from him would be established, entering upon his daughters as others of his sons enter upon them. As for what they argued from Umar and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that of what we narrated from them - Abdullah ibn Abbas and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them, opposed them in that."
"4424","4731 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ibn Abbas used to visit a slave-girl of his; she became pregnant, and he said: He is not from me; I visited her in a manner I did not intend offspring by."
"4425","4732 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, that his father used to practice coitus interruptus with a Persian slave-girl; she became pregnant, and he denied him and said: I did not intend your child; I only sought your company! So he flogged her, freed her, and freed the child."
"4426","4733 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit - the same, except that he did not say: So he freed her, and freed her child."
"4427","4734 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A slave-girl of Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, gave birth, and he said: He is not from me; I used to practice coitus interruptus with her. So this Zayd ibn Thabit and Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, opposed Umar and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that. So their views are balanced, and reflection became obligatory to extract a correct view from the two. We saw that when a man acknowledges this is his child from his wife then denies him after that, he is not negated. Likewise had he claimed her pregnancy is from him then she brings a child of that pregnancy, he has no denying him after that by lian or otherwise; for his lineage is established from him. This is the ruling of what claim has fallen upon, which its claimant may not deny. And we saw had he acknowledged he had intercourse with his wife, then she brings a child and he denies him, the ruling is lian is performed between them, the child leaves the husbands lineage and is attached to his mother. So his acknowledgment of intercourse with his wife did not establish lineage of what is born of him, nor was it in the ruling of what is attached to him which he may not deny. Since this was the ruling of wives, the ruling of bondwomen is worthier to be so. If a man acknowledges the child of his bondwoman as from him, or acknowledges while she is pregnant that what is in her womb is from him, it is attached to him and is never negated from him after that. If he acknowledges he had intercourse with her, that is not in the ruling of his acknowledgment of her child as from him; rather it is otherwise: he may deny him, and his ruling, even acknowledging intercourse with his bondwoman, is as his ruling had he not acknowledged intercourse with her - by analogy with what we described of free women. All this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allahs mercy be upon them all."
"4428","Book 10: The Book of Oaths and Vows. Chapter 1: The amount given to each poor person of food in expiations. 4735 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan! He said to him: Free a neck. He said: I do not have it, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Then fast two consecutive months. He said: I cannot! He said: Then feed sixty poor. He said: I do not have it, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a basket containing about fifteen sa of dates, and said: Take them and give charity with them. He said: Upon one more needy than me and my household?! He said: Then eat them you and your household, fast a day in its place, and seek Allahs forgiveness. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: A group went to the view that feeding in oath expiations is a mudd for each poor person; for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the man in the hadith we mentioned to feed sixty poor fifteen sa - so what reaches each of them is a mudd each. They said: A group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went in oath expiations to what we said. They mentioned in that what."
"4429","4736 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that Abu Hazim narrated to him, from Abu Jafar, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to say: In oath expiations, feeding ten poor, each poor a white mudd."
"4430","4737 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas - the same."
"4431","4738 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that when he expiated his oath and fed ten poor with the smaller mudd, he considered that sufficient in his view."
"4432","4739 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that he used to say: Whoever swears an oath and emphasizes it then breaks it, upon him is freeing a neck or clothing ten poor; whoever swears an oath and does not emphasize it then breaks it, upon him is feeding ten poor - for each poor a mudd of wheat."
"4433","4740 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: A mudd of wheat for each poor suffices in oath expiation."
"4434","4741 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Khalil ibn Murrah informed me that Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to him - then he mentioned with his isnad the same. Others disagreed with them; they said: In feeding in oath expiation, only two mudds each for each poor suffices; and a full sa of dates suffices, and likewise of barley. Among their proof against the people of the first view is it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he knew the mans need, gave him what he gave of dates to help him in what was due upon him - not that it is all what was due upon him; like a man complaining to a man of his weak condition and debt upon him, and he says to him: Take these ten dirhams and pay your debt with them - not that they settle all his debt, but that they settle an amount of his debt. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the amount due of food in an expiation among expiations - that due for shaving the head in ihram due to harm: he made that two mudds of wheat for each poor."
"4435","4742 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Maqil saying: I sat with Kab ibn Ujrah in the mosque and asked him about this verse: Then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. He said: Concerning me it was revealed; I was carried to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with lice falling upon my face. He said: I did not think hardship reached you as this, and reached you what I see! So it was revealed concerning me specifically and for you generally: he ordered me to shave my head, offer my sacrifice, fast three days, or feed six poor - for each poor half a sa of wheat."
"4436","4743 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ibn al-Asbahani, from Abdullah ibn Maqil, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same, except that he said: And feed a faraq among six poor."
"4437","4744 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amir al-Shabi, he said: Kab ibn Ujrah narrated to me - the same, except that he said: each poor half a sa of dates."
"4438","4745 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, from Abu Layla, from Kab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same, and he did not mention dates."
"4439","4746 - Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us. (Ha.)"
"4440","4747 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us; both said: from Ayyub, from Mujahid - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4441","4748 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4442","4749 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4443","4750 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from Humayd ibn Qays, from Mujahid - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4444","4751 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Mujahid - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4445","4752 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Yahya ibn Jadah, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4446","4753 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same, and added: And he knew I have nothing with which to sacrifice."
"4447","4754 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik al-Jazari, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same, except that he did not mention the addition in it over what is in the hadiths before it. So what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him of feeding in these reports - despite their frequency - is half a sa of wheat for each poor; and they agreed upon acting with that in the expiation of shaving the head. And there has come from him concerning feeding the poor in zihar, of dates, what is mentioned after this."
"4448","4755 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Farwah narrated to us, from Abu al-Maghra narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Mamar ibn Abdullah, from Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam, Khawlah daughter of Malik ibn Thalabah, brother of Ubadah ibn al-Samit, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) helped her husband when he did zihar from her, with an irq (basket) of dates, and she helped him with another faraq; that is sixty sa. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give charity with it. And said: Fear Allah and return to your husband! So by reasoning upon what we mentioned, feeding each poor in all expiations is likewise: of wheat half a sa, and of dates a sa. And there has been narrated in that from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4449","4756 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Yasar ibn Numayr, he said: Umar said to me: I swear not to give some peoples, then it appears to me to give them; so when you see me do that, feed for me ten poor - each poor a sa of dates."
"4450","4757 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Wail, from Yasar ibn Numayr, from Umar - the same, except that he said: Ten poor; for each poor half a sa of wheat or a sa of dates."
"4451","4758 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I heard Abu Wail, from Yasar - then he mentioned with his isnad the same, and added: Or a sa of dates, or a sa of barley."
"4452","4759 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Yasar - the same."
"4453","4760 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Yasar - the same."
"4454","4761 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid and Ali ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, from Ali concerning oath expiations - then he mentioned similar to what was narrated from Umar."
"4455","4762 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Salih narrated to us, from Muslim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas concerning oath expiation, he said: Half a sa of wheat. This contradicts what we narrated from Ibn Abbas in the preceding section. So Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, made feeding in oath expiations of wheat two mudds each for each poor, and of barley and dates a sa each; so likewise we say. Likewise every feeding in an expiation or otherwise, this is its amount, upon what is agreed from the lesser expiation. That is also strengthened by what we clarified in the book of fitr charity concerning its amount and what we mentioned therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions after him. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4456","Chapter 2: A man swearing not to speak to a man for a month - how many days is that month? 4763 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Sad, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is thus and thus and thus. And he decreased a finger in the third."
"4457","4764 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ismail al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Yafur, he said: We discussed the month with Abu al-Duha; some of us said twenty-nine, some said thirty. Abu al-Duha said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us, he said: One morning the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were weeping, with each ones family with her! Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, came and ascended to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in his chamber; he greeted him and none answered him, then he greeted and none answered him. When he saw that he left. Bilal called him, and he entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Have you divorced your wives? He said: No, but I have sworn abstention from them for a month. So he remained twenty-nine nights then descended and entered upon his wives."
"4458","4766 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Jabalah ibn Suhaym narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is thus and thus and thus. And he folded his thumb in the third."
"4459","4767 - Bakr narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us, he said: I heard Said ibn Amr saying: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar mentioning from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"4460","4768 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Alqamah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine; when you see it, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it. We have also mentioned in this reports in what preceded of this book of ours."
"4461","4769 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Hakam al-Sulami narrating from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swore abstention from his wives for a month; Gabriel came to him and said: O Muhammad, the month is twenty-nine."
"4462","4770 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, that he heard Abdullah ibn Umar saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The month is twenty-nine."
"4463","4771 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sayfi informed me that Ikrimah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him that Umm Salamah informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swore not to enter upon some of his family for a month; when twenty-nine days passed he came to them in the morning - or evening. It was said to him: You swore, O Prophet of Allah, not to enter upon them for a month! He said: The month is twenty-nine days."
"4464","4772 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) boycotted his wives for a month; he would be in the upper room and they in the lower. He descended to them in twenty-nine. A man said: You remained twenty-nine nights! He said: The month is thus and thus - with the fingers of his hands - and thus; and he folded his thumb in the third."
"4465","4773 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir - then he mentioned the same."
"4466","4774 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swore abstention from his wives and stayed in a mashrubah (upper chamber) twenty-nine, then descended. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you swore a month! He said: The month is twenty-nine. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: A group went to the view that when a man swears not to speak to a man for a month and speaks to him after twenty-nine days, he does not break his oath. They argued with these reports. Others disagreed; they said: If he swore with the sighting of the crescent, it is upon that month, whether thirty days or twenty-nine days; if he swore in part of a month, his oath is upon thirty days. They argued with the hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine; when you see it, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, complete thirty days. Do you not see he obligated upon them - when obscured - thirty, and made it upon completion until they see that crescent? Likewise he did in Shaban: he ordered fasting after the crescent of Ramadan is seen; when obscured for them, they did not fast, and Shaban was upon thirty except interrupted by sighting the crescent. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that other than what is in the first reports."
"4467","4775 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swore to boycott us for a month; he entered upon us at twenty-nine. We said: O Messenger of Allah, you swore not to speak to us for a month, yet you came at twenty-nine! He said: The month does not complete. So he informed he did that only due to the deficiency of the month; this proves he had sworn upon them with the beginning of the crescent. So likewise we say, and there has been narrated in this what is clearer than this."
"4468","4776 - Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: Their statement that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine - no, by Allah, he did not say so; I - by Allah - know best what he said in that: he only said when he boycotted us: I shall boycott you for a month. Then he came after twenty-nine nights passed. I said: O Prophet of Allah, you swore a month, yet you were absent from us twenty-nine nights! He said: This month of ours was twenty-nine nights. So by that it is established his oath was with the sighting of the crescent. And there has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, something of this."
"4469","4777 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Simak Abu Zumayl, he said: Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me - then he mentioned the ila of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his wives, that he descended at twenty-nine and said: The month may be twenty-nine. And there has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in that what."
"4470","4778 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine and is thirty; when you see it, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if obscured for you, complete the number. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that it is twenty-nine only by sighting the crescent before thirty. So these reports prove what I uncovered of what we mentioned. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And that has also been narrated from al-Hasan."
"4471","4779 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who vowed to fast a month, he said: If he begins with the sighting of the crescent, he fasts by its sighting and breaks by its sighting; if he begins in part of the month, he fasts thirty days; and Allah Almighty knows best."
"4472","Chapter 3: A man obligating upon himself to pray in a place, then praying in another. 4780 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Habib al-Muallim, from Ata, from Jabir, that a man said on the day of the Conquest: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I vowed - if Allah grants you conquest of Makkah - to pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Pray here. He repeated it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twice or thrice. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then as you wish! Abu Jafar said: In this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the one who vowed to pray in Bayt al-Maqdis to pray in another than it. Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said: Whoever makes upon himself for Allah to pray in a place, then prays in another, that suffices him. They argued with this hadith. Except that Abu Yusuf said in his dictation: Whoever vows to pray in Bayt al-Maqdis, then prays in the Sacred Mosque or in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that suffices him; for he prayed in a place where prayer is superior to prayer in the place he obligated prayer in upon himself. Whoever vows to pray in the Sacred Mosque, then prays in Bayt al-Maqdis, that does not suffice him; for he prayed in a place whose prayer has not the merit prayer has in that place he obligated prayer in upon himself. He argued with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4473","4781 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Aziz al-Zubaydi, from Amr ibn al-Hakam, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in what is besides it, except the Sacred Mosque."
"4474","4782 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki and Shuja narrated to us. (Ha.) 4783 - And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Makki narrated to us; they said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Mudrik, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4475","4784 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Musa al-Juhani, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4476","4785 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, saying: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Mabad ibn Abbas narrated to me, from Maymunah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4477","4786 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth informed me, he said: Nafi narrated to me - then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4478","4787 - al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4479","4788 - Musa said: And Abu Abdullah narrated to me this hadith from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4480","4789 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Sahm ibn Minjab, from Qazaah, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4481","4790 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4482","4791 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Abu Salamah narrating from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4483","4792 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Aflah ibn Humayd narrated to us. (Ha.) 4793 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. (Ha.) 4794 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us; they said: Aflah narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hazm narrated to me, from Salman al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4484","4795 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Rabah and Ubaydullah ibn Abi Abdullah, from Abu Abdullah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4485","4796 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Salman al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4486","4797 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Salman narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4487","4798 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hilal narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4488","4799 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, he said: I asked Abu Salih: Did you hear Abu Hurayrah mention the merit of prayer in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No, but Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz narrated to me that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the same. Abu Jafar said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferred prayer in his mosque over prayer in others by a thousand prayers, other than the Sacred Mosque. So it is possible there is no merit of prayer in the Sacred Mosque over prayer in his mosque, or prayer in one of them is superior to prayer in the other."
"4489","4800 - So we examined that, and Ahmad ibn Dawud: narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Habib al-Muallim, from Ata, from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other mosques except the Sacred Mosque; and a prayer in that is better than a hundred prayers in this."
"4490","4801 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Sulayman ibn Atiq narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr upon the minbar saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab - then he mentioned the same and did not raise it. Sufyan said: So they consider prayer in the Sacred Mosque better than a hundred thousand prayers in other mosques except in the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he only preferred it over it by a hundred prayers."
"4491","4802 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in what is besides it except the Sacred Mosque; and a prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in what is besides it. He said: Since the merit of prayer in some of these mosques over others is what was mentioned in these reports, one who obligated upon himself prayer in any of them may only pray it where he obligated or in what is superior to it among places. And among the proof for Abu Hanifah and Muhammad against the people of this view is that the meaning of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in what is besides it, is only upon obligatory prayers, not supererogatory - do you not see his statement in the hadith of Abd ibn Sad: That I pray in my house is dearer to me than that I pray in the mosque. And his statement in the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit: The best prayer of a man is in his house except the prescribed. That was when he wanted to lead them in Ramadan in supererogatory prayer. We have mentioned that in other than this place of these reports. So when that was narrated as we mentioned, authentication of the reports necessitates that prayer in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which has merit over prayer in houses is the prayer opposite to this prayer, namely the prescribed. So the corruption of what Abu Yusuf argued is established, and it is established that whoever obligated upon himself prayer in a place then prayed it in another, it suffices him. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we saw when a man says, Upon me for Allah to pray two rakahs in the Sacred Mosque - the prayer he obligated is devotion wherever it is, so it is obligatory upon him. Then we wanted to examine the place he obligated upon himself to pray them in: is it obligatory upon him as that prayer is obligatory upon him or not? We saw had he said, Upon me for Allah to remain in the Sacred Mosque an hour - that would not be obligatory upon him, even though that remaining, had he done it, is devotion. So remaining - though devotion - is not obligatory by a mans obligating it upon himself. Since what we mentioned is so, one who obligated for Allah upon himself prayer in the Sacred Mosque - prayer is obligatory upon him, but remaining in the Sacred Mosque is not obligatory upon him. This is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, may Allahs mercy be upon them both, and Allah knows best."
"4492","Chapter 4: A man obligating upon himself walking to the House of Allah. 4803 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Hiql ibn Ziyad narrated to me, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Yaman narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said, that Humayd al-Tawil informed him that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man being supported between his two sons. He asked about him; they said: He vowed to walk. He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is surely independent of this ones tormenting himself; and he ordered him to ride (i.e. due to his inability to walk)."
"4493","4804 - al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the same."
"4494","4805 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Humayd, from Thabit, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4495","4806 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Mansur narrated to us, from Dukhayn al-Hajri, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: My sister vowed to walk to the Kabah barefoot, unveiled. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon her and said: What is with this one? They said: She vowed to walk to the Kabah barefoot, unveiled. He said: Order her to ride and to veil. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: A group went to these reports and said: Whoever vows to perform hajj walking is ordered to ride, with nothing else upon him besides that. Others disagreed; they said: He rides, as came in this hadith; if by his statement, Upon me for Allah, he intended the meaning of an oath, upon him with that is expiation of an oath - for the meaning of, Upon me for Allah, may be in the meaning of, By Allah; for a vow has the meaning of an oath. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that in a vow is expiation of an oath."
"4496","Among what was narrated on that subject is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Tamimi, from his father, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in anger, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"4497","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4498","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Hanzali narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4499","Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abbadah ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4500","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us. And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, they both said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Hanzali informed us, from his father, from a man, from Imran, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4501","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Arqam, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir who used to live in al-Yamamah, that he narrated to him that he heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman reporting, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in disobedience, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"4502","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Kab ibn Alqamah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah al-Mahdi, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The expiation of a vow is the expiation of an oath."
"4503","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Salim narrating, from Ismail ibn Rafi, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Uqbah ibn Amir, he said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever vows a vow without naming it, its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"4504","They also mentioned on that subject what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Huyayy ibn Abd Allah al-Maafiri informed me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, that his sister vowed to walk to the Kabah barefoot and without a head covering, so Uqbah mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Command your sister to ride, to cover her head, and to fast three days."
"4505","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, that he heard Abu Said al-Ruayni, from Abd Allah ibn Malik, from Uqbah ibn Amir, the same as it."
"4506","Al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Abu Said al-Yahsabi, from Abd Allah ibn Malik, from Uqbah ibn Amir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. They said: Those three days were only an expiation for her oath by which she had sworn, by her saying: Allah has upon me that I perform Hajj walking."
"4507","What indicates that is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Sharik, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my sister vowed to perform Hajj walking. He said: Allah has no need of your sister's hardship; let her perform Hajj riding and expiate for her oath. Others also opposed them and said: Rather we command this one who vowed to perform Hajj walking to ride and expiate his oath, if he intended an oath, and along with that we command him to offer a sacrificial animal."
"4508","Among the proof for them in that is that Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Uqbah ibn Amir came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that his sister vowed to walk to the Kabah barefoot with her hair uncovered. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Command her to ride, to cover her head, and to offer a sacrificial animal."
"4509","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Matar al-Warraq narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: My sister vowed to walk to the Kabah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon her and said: What is the matter with this woman? They said: She vowed to walk to the Kabah, so he said: Allah is indeed free of need of her walking; command her to ride and to offer a camel as sacrifice. In this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to offer a sacrifice on account of her riding instead of walking. The authentication of all these reports together requires that the ruling for one who vowed to perform Hajj walking is that he rides if he wishes that, offers a sacrificial animal for abandoning the walking, and expiates for his oath for breaking it. Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad used to say this. As for the angle of rational consideration in that, a group said: Walking is not among what a vow makes obligatory, because it involves bodily hardship, and the walker while walking is not in the sanctity of ihram, so they did not make walking obligatory upon him nor a substitute for walking. We examined that and saw that Hajj includes circumambulation of the House, standing at Arafah and at Muzdalifah. Part of the circumambulation is what a man does while in ihram, namely the circumambulation of visiting, and part is what he does after exiting ihram, namely the farewell circumambulation. All of that is among the rites of Hajj which one may intend to do walking, and whoever does it riding falls short, and blood is imposed upon him, when he did it without an excuse. If he did it due to an excuse, people differ about that. Some said: Nothing is upon him, and among those who said that are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them, and some said: Upon him is blood, and among those who said that is al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him."
"4510","Chapter: A man vows a vow while a polytheist then embraces Islam. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic period to observe itikaf in the Sacred Mosque, so he said: Fulfill your vow."
"4511","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, I think from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed a vow in the pre-Islamic period, and now Allah has brought Islam, so he said: Fulfill your vow."
"4512","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, that Ayyub narrated to him, that Nafi narrated to him, that Abd Allah ibn Umar narrated to him, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at al-Jiranah, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic period to observe itikaf for a day in the Sacred Mosque. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and observe itikaf for a day. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: A group went to the view that when a man imposes upon himself in his state of polytheism itikaf or charity or anything that Muslims impose upon themselves for Allah, then embraces Islam, that it is obligatory upon him, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: Nothing of that is obligatory upon him, and they argued with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4513","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Ayli, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."
"4514","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4515","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4516","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Talhah, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4517","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Aban, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."
"4518","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4519","Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Kab al-Halabi narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Ibn Harmalah, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A vow is only what seeks thereby the Face of Allah. They said: Since vows are only obligatory when they are among what draws one near to Allah Almighty, and are not obligatory when they are disobedience to Allah, and a disbeliever when he says: Allah has upon me fasting, or says: Allah has upon me itikaf, if he did that he would not be drawing near to Allah thereby, since at the time he imposed it he only intended thereby his lord whom he worships besides Allah, and that is disobedience. So that enters under the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no vow in disobedience. It is also possible that the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umar: Fulfill your vow, is not by way of that being obligatory upon him, but that he had been willing at the time he vowed it to do it, though it was in disobedience to Allah Almighty, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to do it now as obedience to Allah Almighty. So what he commanded him was different from what he had imposed upon himself. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"4520","Book of Prescribed Punishments. Chapter of the punishment of the virgin for adultery. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hittan ibn Abd Allah al-Raqashi, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin, and the previously married with the previously married; the virgin is flogged and exiled, and the previously married is flogged and stoned."
"4521","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Fadl ibn Dalham, from al-Hasan, from Qabisah ibn Hurayth, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbaq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin, one hundred lashes and exile for a year, and the previously married with the previously married, one hundred lashes and stoning."
"4522","Yunus and Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Hurayrah, Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani and Shibl, they said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then a man stood up to him and said: I adjure you by Allah, judge between us only by the Book of Allah Almighty. His opponent, who was more understanding than him, said: He spoke the truth; judge between us by the Book of Allah, and permit me. He said: Speak. He said: My son was a hired worker for this man, and he committed adultery with his wife, so I ransomed him from him with one hundred sheep and a servant, then I asked men of knowledge, and they informed me that upon my son is one hundred lashes and exile for a year, and upon this man's wife is stoning. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I shall judge between you both by the Book of Allah; the hundred sheep and the servant are returned to you, and upon your son is one hundred lashes and exile for a year, and go, O Unays, to this man's wife, and if she confesses, stone her. So he went to her in the morning and she confessed, so he stoned her."
"4523","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, they said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned similar to it. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a virgin commits adultery, upon him is one hundred lashes and exile for a year together, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: The punishment of the virgin when he commits adultery is one hundred lashes, with no exile along with flogging, unless the ruler sees fit to exile him due to the shamelessness that came from him, exiling him wherever he wishes, just as shameless persons and non-adulterers are exiled."
"4524","They argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a slave woman when she commits adultery without being married. He said: When she commits adultery, flog her, then if she commits adultery, flog her, then if she commits adultery, flog her, then sell her even for a rope. Malik said: Ibn Shihab said: I do not know whether after the third or the fourth."
"4525","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah informed me, that Shibl ibn Khalid informed him, that Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Awsi informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The young slave woman when she commits adultery, the same as it. Except that he said regarding the third or fourth: the sale. And Zayd ibn Khalid, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him the same as that. Abu Jafar said: This is a mistake; this Shibl is Ibn Khulayd al-Muzani."
"4526","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah, that is Ibn al-Walid, narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, that Shibl ibn Khulayd al-Muzani informed him, that Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Awsi informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the young slave woman commits adultery, flog her, then if she commits adultery, flog her, then if she commits adultery, flog her, then if she commits adultery, sell her even for a rope. And al-dafir means the rope."
"4527","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Makhul, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the slave woman of one of you commits adultery, let him flog her the prescribed punishment and not scold her. He said that three times, then said at the third or fourth: Then sell her even for a rope."
"4528","Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, that his father informed him, from Said al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard him say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the same as it."
"4529","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4530","Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Uways informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, who was a Companion, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the slave woman commits adultery, flog her, then when she commits adultery, flog her, then when she commits adultery, flog her, then sell her even for a rope."
"4531","Ali narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Abu Uways, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, the same as it."
"4532","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Umarah ibn Abi Farwah, that Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to him, that Urwah narrated to him, that Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman narrated to him, that Aishah narrated to her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same as it."
"4533","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Ala al-Thalabi, from Abu Jamilah, from Ali, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed of a slave woman of theirs who had committed immorality, so he sent me to her and said: Go and carry out the punishment upon her. So I went and found her not yet dried of her blood from childbirth, so I returned to him, and he said to me: Have you finished? I said: I found her not yet dried of her blood. He said: When she is dried of her blood, flog her. Ali said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Carry out the prescribed punishments upon those whom your right hands possess. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding the slave woman when she commits adultery that she be flogged, and did not command exile along with flogging, and Allah Almighty said: Upon them is half of what is upon married free women of punishment, we know thereby that what is due upon slave women when they commit adultery is half of what is due upon free women when they commit adultery. Then since it is established that there is no exile upon the slave woman when she commits adultery, so likewise there is no exile upon the free unmarried woman when she commits adultery. We have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book of ours that he forbade a woman to travel three days except with a mahram, so that is also proof that a woman does not travel three days for the punishment of adultery without a mahram, and in that is the nullification of exile for women in adultery; and when it is negated that exile is due upon unmarried women in adultery, it is likewise negated for men. The Prophet warding it off from slave women in what we mentioned was a warding off from free women, and in his warding it off from free women is proof of his warding it off from free men. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4534","It is said to him: There has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding exile in other than adultery what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man killed his slave deliberately, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged him one hundred, exiled him a year, erased, I think, his share from the Muslims, and commanded him to free a slave. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did here, exiling the killer a year, was not in our view nor in yours proof that it is an obligatory prescribed punishment that must not be abandoned; it was only for shameless misconduct, not as a prescribed punishment. So why do you deny that what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he commanded of exiling the adulterer may likewise be for shameless misconduct, not as an obligatory prescribed punishment like the obligation of flogging and stoning."
"4535","Chapter: What is the punishment of the married adulterer? Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that a man committed adultery, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him and he was flogged, then he was informed that he had been married, so he commanded regarding him and he was stoned. Abu Jafar said: A group went to this and said: Such is the punishment of the married person when he commits adultery, flogging and stoning together. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather his punishment is stoning without flogging. They said: It is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only stoned him when he was informed he was married because the flogging he had flogged him was not part of his punishment at all, since his punishment was stoning without flogging; and it is possible that he stoned him because that stoning is his punishment along with flogging."
"4536","The people of the first saying also argued for their saying with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hittan ibn Abd Allah al-Raqashi, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin is flogged and exiled, and the previously married with the previously married is flogged and stoned."
"4537","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Zadhdhan informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: Hittan ibn Abd Allah al-Raqashi narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin, one hundred lashes and exile for a year, and the previously married with the previously married, one hundred lashes and stoning. They said: By this we say, we hold that the married person is flogged then stoned after that, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. Among the proof for the others against them in that is what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his commanding Unays al-Aslami to stone the woman whom he commanded him to go to in the morning and stone if she confessed, and he did not command him to flog her. I have mentioned that with its chain in the first chapter, and in that hadith also is that the one who stood up to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I asked men of knowledge and they informed me that upon this man's wife is stoning, without mentioning flogging along with it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not object to that. This indicates that all of what was upon her of punishment for the adultery that came from her was stoning without flogging. That is also strengthened by what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he did with Maiz, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4538","Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Simak, from Jabir ibn Samurah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned Maiz and did not mention flogging. In what we mentioned of that is what indicates that the punishment of the married person is stoning without flogging. If one says: Why was not what includes stoning and flogging more worthy than what includes stoning alone? It is said to him: Because of an indication that showed the abrogation of flogging along with stoning, namely that we saw the basis of what was upon the adulterer before distinguishing between his ruling when married and his ruling when unmarried was what Allah Almighty described in His Book by His saying: As for those of your women who commit immorality, call four witnesses from among you against them, and if they testify, confine them to houses until death takes them or Allah makes for them a way. So this was the punishment of the adulteress, to be confined in houses until she dies or Allah makes for them a way. Then it was abrogated by his saying: Take from me, Allah has made for them a way. Then he mentioned what we have mentioned in the hadith of Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and that was the way which Allah Almighty said: Or Allah makes for them a way. So Allah made that way as He explained upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ordaining therein flogging and stoning for the previously married and flogging and exile for the unmarried. We know that saying was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the revelation of this verse, and that the obligation of stoning upon the adulterer did not precede the revelation of the verse, since his punishment was as Allah Almighty described in His Book of confinement in houses. After that came what abrogated flogging with stoning for the married person, as shown by the stoning of Maiz and others without flogging, so the established ruling is stoning alone. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad."
"4539","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: A woman from Hamdan called Shurahah came to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I committed adultery. He turned her away until she testified against herself four testimonies, then he commanded regarding her and she was flogged, then he commanded regarding her and she was stoned."
"4540","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4541","Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Radrad ibn Asad, he said: I witnessed Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, flog Shurahah then stone her."
"4542","Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Muslim al-Awar, from Habbah al-Urani, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Shurahah came to him and confessed before him that she committed adultery. Ali said to her: Perhaps you were coerced against yourself. She said: I came willingly, not coerced. He said: He delayed her until she gave birth and weaned her child, then he flogged her the prescribed punishment by her confession, then buried her in al-Rahbah, that is the wide open space, up to her shoulders, then he cast at her first of the people, then said: Cast. Then he said: I flogged her by the Book of Allah Almighty, and stoned her by the Sunnah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4543","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah, from al-Shabi, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, flogged Shurahah on Thursday and stoned her on Friday, and said: I flogged her by the Book of Allah Almighty, and stoned her by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It is said to him: Even if this was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, as we mentioned, others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have narrated regarding that contrary to what was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4544","Among that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me, that Abu Waqid al-Laythi then al-Ashjai informed him, who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: While we were with Umar at his arrival in Syria at al-Jabiyah, a man came to him and said: O Commander of the Faithful, my wife committed adultery with my slave boy, and she here confesses that. He sent me among a group to her to ask her about that. I came to her and she was a young girl of recent age. I said: O Allah, free her mouth today for whatever You will. I asked her and informed her of what her husband said. She said: He spoke the truth. We conveyed that to Umar, so he commanded that she be stoned."
"4545","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Waqid al-Laythi, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, while he was in Syria, was come to by a man who mentioned to him that he found a man with his wife. Umar ibn al-Khattab sent Abu Waqid al-Laythi to his wife to ask her about that. He came to her with women around her, mentioned to her what her husband said to Umar ibn al-Khattab, informed her that she is not taken by his word, and kept suggesting to her similar things so that she would retract, but she refused to retract and remained firm upon confession. Umar commanded regarding her and she was stoned. This is Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who did not flog her before stoning her. This contradicts what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, did with Shurahah, flogging her before stoning her. This of the two acts is more worthy in our view, for what we have mentioned in this chapter."
"4546","Chapter: The confession of adultery by which the punishment becomes due, what is it? Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that when a man confesses adultery once, the punishment of adultery is carried out upon him. They argued with what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this book of his saying to Unays, may Allah be pleased with him: Go in the morning, O Unays, to this man's wife, and if she confesses, stone her. They said: In this is proof that confession of adultery once makes the punishment due. Others opposed them and said: The punishment of adultery is not due upon one who confesses adultery until he confesses it against himself four times. They said: There is no proof in what you mentioned of the hadith of Unays for what you described, for it is possible that Unays already knew what confession makes the punishment of adultery due upon the confessor, by what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taught them regarding Maiz and others, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed him with this address after knowing that he knew what confession makes the punishment due. Other reports besides this one have come clarifying what the confession of adultery that makes the punishment due upon the confessor is. Among that is what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Abu Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned Maiz away four times."
"4547","Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Taifi, from Abd Allah ibn al-Miqdam, from Ibn Shaddad, from Abu Dharr, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, then a man came and confessed before him to adultery, so he turned him away four times, then he dismounted and commanded us, so we dug for him a pit not very deep, then he commanded regarding him and he was stoned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departed low-spirited and grieved. We traveled until we stopped at a stopping place, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abu Dharr, did you not see your companion, he was forgiven and admitted to Paradise."
"4548","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim al-Zibriqan and Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4549","Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Maiz: Is what reached me about you true? He said: What reached you about me? He said: It reached me that you came to the slave girl of the family of so-and-so. So he confessed against himself four times, and he commanded regarding him and he was stoned."
"4550","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4551","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, that a man from Aslam came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the mosque, called him and told him that he committed adultery. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from him, so he moved to the side he had turned toward and told him that he committed adultery, testifying against himself four times. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: Is there madness in you? He said: No. He said: Have you been married? He said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him that he be stoned at the prayer place. When the stones pained him he dashed off until he was caught at al-Harrah and killed there by stoning."
"4552","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: A hairy, short, muscular man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and confessed to him adultery. He turned away from him, then he came to him from the other side of his face, and he turned away from him, I do not know whether twice or thrice, then he commanded regarding him and he was stoned. He said: I mentioned that to Said ibn Jubayr, and he said: He turned him away four times."
"4553","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it. Except that he said: He turned him away twice. Someone said: In this is that he was punished after confessing fewer than four times. It is said to him: In this hadith is a defect."
"4554","That is because Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Maiz ibn Malik came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and confessed twice, so he said: Take him away, then bring him back. So he confessed twice until he confessed four times. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take him and stone him. In this hadith is that he confessed twice, then they took him away, then brought him back, and he confessed twice. It is possible that Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, witnessed the last two times and did not witness what came from him before that, while Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, witnessed the whole confession, as did those who agreed with him that it was four times."
"4555","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hadhdhad, from Abu Hurayrah, that Maiz ibn Malik committed adultery, then came to Hazzal and confessed to him that he committed adultery. Hazzal said to him: Go to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him before a Quranic verse comes down about you. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I committed adultery. He turned away from him until he said that four times, then he commanded regarding him and he was stoned."
"4556","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah and Said ibn al-Musayyib informed me, that Abu Hurayrah said: A man from Aslam came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the mosque and called him, telling him that he committed adultery. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from him, so he moved to the side he had turned toward and told him that he committed adultery, testifying against himself four times. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: Is there madness in you? He said: No. He said: Have you been married? He said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him that he be stoned at the prayer place."
"4557","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn al-Muhajir al-Ghanawi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from his father, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then a man called Maiz ibn Malik came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, I have committed adultery and I want you to purify me. He said to him: Go back. The next day he came again and confessed before him to adultery, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go back. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to his people and asked them about him, saying: What do you say about Maiz ibn Malik? Do you see any harm in him, or do you disapprove anything of his intellect? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we see no harm in him and disapprove nothing of his intellect. Then he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a third time and confessed before him to adultery, saying: O Messenger of Allah, purify me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to his people and asked them about him, and they said to him as they said the first time: We see no harm in him and disapprove nothing of his intellect. Then he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a fourth time and confessed before him to adultery, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him: a pit was dug for him, he was placed in it up to his chest, then he commanded the people to stone him. Buraydah said: We used to talk among ourselves, the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Maiz ibn Malik, had he stayed in his dwelling after confessing three times, he would not have sought him out; he only stoned him at the fourth. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not stone him for confessing once, twice or thrice, that indicates the punishment was not due upon him until he confessed four times. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad."
"4558","Chapter: A man commits adultery with his wife's slave girl. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbaq, like the one narrating as a Companion, that a man committed adultery with his wife's slave girl, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he coerced her, she is free and upon him is one like her; and if she obeyed him willingly, she belongs to him and upon him is one like her."
"4559","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: I asked al-Hasan about a man who falls upon his wife's slave girl. He said: Qabisah ibn Hurayth al-Ansari narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbaq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same as it. He added: And he did not carry out a prescribed punishment upon him. Abu Jafar said: A group went to this and said: This is the ruling for one who commits adultery with his wife's slave girl, as in this hadith of Salamah. They said: Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, acted upon that after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4560","They mentioned in that what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb informed us, from Shubah, from Mansur, from Uqbah ibn Jayyan, that a man came to Abd Allah ibn Masud and said: I committed adultery. He said: What did you do? He said: I fell upon my wife's slave girl. Abd Allah ibn Masud said: Allah is Most Great; if you coerced her, free her, and if she obeyed you willingly, free her and upon you is one like her. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather we hold upon him is stoning if married and flogging if unmarried."
"4561","Among what they held in that of reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Hushaym ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Habib ibn Salim, that a man fell upon his wife's slave girl, so his wife came to al-Numan ibn Bashir and informed him. He said: Indeed I have with me regarding that established report taken from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If you permitted him, I flog him one hundred; and if you did not permit him, I stone him."
"4562","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah was asked about a man who had intercourse with his wife's slave girl, so he narrated to us from Habib ibn Yasaf, from Habib ibn Salim, that she was brought before al-Numan ibn Bashir, and he said: I shall judge therein by the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If she permitted it to him, I flog him one hundred; and if she did not permit it to him, I stone him. In this hadith is contrary to what is in the first hadith, for therein is that if she did not permit him, he is stoned. As for his saying: If you permitted him, we flog him one hundred, that hundred in our view is discretionary punishment, as though he warded off the prescribed punishment from him due to his intercourse under doubt, and disciplined him for engaging in what is not lawful for him. If one says: Is discretionary punishment with one hundred permissible? It is said to him: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disciplined with one hundred in a hadith we have mentioned from him regarding a man who killed his slave deliberately, in the chapter of the punishment of the virgin in this book. What al-Numan mentioned in our view abrogates what Salamah ibn al-Muhabbaq narrated. That is because the ruling in early Islam imposed penalties for acts upon wealth, and imposed penalties upon bodies for consuming wealth. Among that is what we mentioned in the chapter of the prohibition of charity for Banu Hashim, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one who withholds zakat: We shall take it from him and half his wealth along with it as punishment for what he did."
"4563","Among that is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, from Ibn Thawr, from Mamar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Ikrimah, I think, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding the concealed stray camel: Its compensation and its like along with it."
"4564","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Hisham ibn Said informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that a man from Muzaynah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think about sheep of the mountain pasture? He said: There is no cutting in anything of grazing livestock except what the pen shelters, and its price reaches the price of a shield, then therein is cutting of the hand; and what does not reach the price of a shield, therein is compensation of twice its like and exemplary lashes. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think about hanging fruit? He said: It and its like with it, and exemplary punishment, and there is no cutting in anything of hanging fruit except what the threshing floor shelters; whatever is taken from the threshing floor and its price reaches the price of a shield, therein is cutting, and whatever does not reach the price of a shield, therein is compensation of twice its like and exemplary lashes. Penalties continued in what is mentioned in these reports as mentioned therein, until that was abrogated by the prohibition of usury, so the matter returned to that none is taken from one who took something except the like of what he took, and penalties are not due upon wealth for violating sanctities that are not wealth. The hadith of Salamah in our view was in the first period, when the ruling upon one who committed adultery with his wife's slave girl, coercing her, was upon him that she be freed as punishment for his act, and he compensates one like her to his wife. If she obeyed him willingly, he was made to give a slave girl, an adulteress, and made to give in her place a pure slave girl, and she was not freed due to her willing obedience to him. He distinguished between when she was willing and when she was coerced. Then that was abrogated, and matters were returned to that none is punished for violating a sanctity in which he did not take wealth by being made to pay wealth, and the ruling became the prescribed punishment or discretionary punishment in the body."
"4565","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib used to say: No man who falls upon his wife's slave girl is brought to me except I stone him."
"4566","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Hamzah, from Amr al-Aslami, from his father, that Umar sent him as a collector upon Sad ibn Hudhaym. Hamzah came with wealth to collect it. There was a man saying to his wife: Pay the charity of your master's wealth, while the woman said to him: Rather you pay the charity of your son's wealth. Hamzah asked about their affair and their words, and was informed that the man is the husband of that woman, that he fell upon a slave girl of hers and she bore a child, and his wife freed her. They said: This wealth belongs to his son from her slave girl. Hamzah said: I shall stone you with your own stones. It was said to him: May Allah set you right, his affair was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, flogged him one hundred and did not see stoning upon him. Hamzah took a guarantor for the man until he came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him about what was mentioned of Umar flogging him and not seeing stoning upon him. Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, confirmed them in that of their word, and said: What warded off stoning from him is that I excused him due to ignorance of the pre-Islamic period. This is Hamzah ibn Amr, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who held that upon one who commits adultery with his wife's slave girl is stoning, and Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, did not object to what he saw of that when he took surety for the man until the command of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, comes regarding carrying out the punishment upon him. That agrees with what was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and what al-Numan narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Also in the hadith of Hamzah is Umar flogging that man one hundred lashes as discretionary punishment in the presence of Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), without objection, proving consensus upon warding off the prescribed punishment by doubt of permission and imposing discretionary punishment."
"4567","Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: The affair of the man who came to Ibn Masud, whose wife's slave girl he had fallen upon and he saw no prescribed punishment upon him, was mentioned to Ali. So Ali said: Had the companion of Ibn Umm Abd come to me, I would have crushed his head with stones; Ibn Umm Abd did not know what happened after him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was informed that Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, held to a matter that once was, then was abrogated after it, and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, did not know of that. Alqamah also opposed Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, in that, inclining to the saying of those who opposed him, though he was the most knowledgeable of his companions, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4568","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, that he was asked about a man who came to his wife's slave girl. He said: I do not care whether you came to her or to the slave girl of the wife of Awsajah. This is Alqamah, may Allah have mercy on him, the most eminent of Abd Allah's companions, may Allah be pleased with him, and the most knowledgeable of them, who abandoned the saying of Abd Allah in that, despite the eminence of Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, with him, and went to other than it. That in our view is due to the abrogation of what Abd Allah went to in that being established with him. So likewise we say: Whoever commits adultery with his wife's slave girl is punished, unless he claims a doubt, such as saying: I thought she was lawful to me, or the wife permitted her to him, in which case the prescribed punishment is warded off from him, he is disciplined, and the dower is due upon him. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"4569","Chapter: One who marries his father's wife or a female relative unmarriageable to him and consummates with her. Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara, he said: I met my maternal uncle with the banner with him. I said: Where are you going? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to a man who married his father's wife after him, to strike his neck, or to kill him."
"4570","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf, that is Ibn Munazil, and Abu Said al-Ashajj narrated to us, they said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ashath, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara, he said: My maternal uncle Abu Burdah ibn Niyar al-Aslami passed by me with the standard, and he mentioned the same as it, except that he said: I bring him with his head."
"4571","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yaqub al-Talqani narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated it to us, he said: al-Ashath informed us, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: al-Harith ibn Amr passed by me with a standard which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had tied for him. I said: To what thing has he sent you? He said: To a man who married his father's wife, to strike his neck."
"4572","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf, that is Ibn Munazil, narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ashath, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4573","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu al-Jahm, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: Some camels of mine went astray, so I went out seeking them. The cavalry came, and when the people of the water saw the cavalry they gathered to me. They came to a tent among those tents and brought out a man from it and struck his neck. They said: This is a man who consummated with his father's wife, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to him and killed him. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that one who marries a female relative unmarriageable to him, knowing her prohibition upon him, and consummates with her, his ruling is the ruling of the adulterer, and the punishment of adultery is carried out upon him, stoning or flogging, and they argued with these reports. Among those who said this saying are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others opposed them and said: The punishment of adultery is not due in this, but severe discretionary punishment and chastisement is due. Among those who said that are Abu Hanifah and Sufyan al-Thawri, may Allah have mercy on them both. Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah with that."
"4574","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan saying regarding a man who marries a female relative unmarriageable to him and consummates with her. He said: No prescribed punishment upon him. Among the proof against those who argued against them with what we mentioned is that in those reports the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded killing, with no mention of stoning nor mention of carrying out the prescribed punishment. All unanimously agree that the doer of that is not subject to killing; rather upon him in the saying of those who make the punishment due upon him is stoning if married. Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command the messenger with stoning but commanded him with killing, it is established thereby that killing is not a punishment for adultery, but for a different meaning: that the marrying one did what he did thereof upon deeming it lawful, as they used to do in the pre-Islamic period, thus becoming an apostate, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that there be done with him what is done with the apostate. So Abu Hanifah and Sufyan, may Allah have mercy on them both, used to say regarding this marrying one when he acted therein upon deeming it lawful that he is killed. Since there is nothing in this hadith negating what Abu Hanifah and Sufyan say, there is no proof therein against them, for their opponent is not more worthy of interpretation than they are. In that hadith also is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tied a banner for Abu Burdah, and banners were only tied for one commanded to fight, while one dispatched to carry out the punishment of adultery is not commanded to fight. In the hadith also is that he sent him to a man who married his father's wife, with no mention that he consummated with her. Since this chastisement, namely killing, was intended against the marrying one for his marrying, it indicates apostasy, not adultery."
"4575","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Yazid ibn al-Bara, from his father, he said: He met his maternal uncle with a banner with him, and said to him: Where are you going? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to a man who married his father's wife, to kill him and take his wealth."
"4576","There has also been narrated similar to that from other than al-Bara, what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud, Fahd and Muhammad ibn al-Ward narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Manazil al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Abi Karimah, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his grandfather Muawiyah to a man who consummated with his father's wife, to strike his neck and take a fifth of his wealth. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these two hadiths commanded taking the wealth of the marrying one and taking a fifth of it, that indicates the marrying one was by his marrying an apostate combatant, so he was to be killed for his apostasy, and his wealth was like the wealth of belligerents. For the apostate who did not fight, all agree regarding taking his wealth upon contrary to taking a fifth. A group, namely Abu Hanifah and his companions, may Allah have mercy on them, and those who say their saying, said: His wealth is for his Muslim heirs. Their opponents said: All his wealth is spoils with no fifth therein, since no cavalry or mounts were urged upon it. In the Prophet taking a fifth of the wealth of the marrying one we mentioned is proof that apostasy and combat both came from him. Thereby is negated that there is proof in that hadith against Abu Hanifah and Sufyan, may Allah have mercy on them both. If one says: We saw that marriage is a marriage that does not stand, so since it does not stand it should be in the ruling of what never contracted, making the one who has intercourse thereby like one who has intercourse without marriage, so he is punished. It is said to him: If that is so, why was your question to us mentioning marriage; you should have said: A man committed adultery with a female relative unmarriageable to him. If you said that, our answer to you would be to say: Upon him is the prescribed punishment only without doubt."
"4577","That is because Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib and Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Tulayhah married during her waiting period, so she was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he beat her blows with a whip and beat her husband, separated between them, and said: Any woman who marries during her waiting period is separated between her and her husband whom she married, then she completes the remainder of her waiting period from the first, then observes waiting from the other; and if the other consummated with her, he never marries her ever; and if he did not consummate with her, she observes waiting from the first, and the other is a suitor among suitors."
"4578","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4579","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that a man married a woman during her waiting period, so he was raised to Umar, who beat her less than the prescribed punishment, assigned her the dower, separated between them, and said: They never come together ever. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If they repent and make amends, I place them among suitors. Do you not see that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, beat the woman and the husband marrying during the waiting period with a whip; it is impossible that he beat them while they were ignorant of the prohibition of what they did, for he was more knowledgeable of Allah Almighty than to punish one against whom proof was not established. Since he beat them, that indicates proof of prohibition had been established against them before they acted, yet he, may Allah be pleased with him, did not carry out the prescribed punishment upon them, in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who followed him upon that and did not oppose him therein. This is sound proof that a marriage contract, even if it does not stand, entails the ruling of marriage in the obligation of dower by subsequent consummation, waiting period from it, establishment of lineage, and what we mentioned; and what entails what we mentioned of that, it is impossible that a prescribed punishment is due therein, for what makes the punishment due is adultery, and adultery does not entail establishment of lineage, dower or waiting period. If one says: What you mentioned of intercourse with an unmarriageable relative upon the marriage you described, even if not adultery, is graver than adultery, so more worthy that what is due in adultery is due therein. It is said to him: By this saying you have removed it from being adultery, claiming it is graver than adultery; and what is like adultery or greater than adultery is not thereby made subject to the punishment of adultery without a text imposing it."
"4580","Chapter: The punishment of wine. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from al-Danaj, from Hudayn ibn al-Mundhir al-Raqashi Abu Sasan, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged forty for wine, Abu Bakr forty, and Umar completed it eighty, and each is a Sunnah."
"4581","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Danaj narrated to us, he said: Hudayn ibn al-Mundhir al-Raqashi narrated to us, he said: I witnessed Uthman ibn Affan when al-Walid ibn Uqbah was brought, having led the people of Kufah in dawn prayer with four units, saying: Shall I add for you. Humran and another man testified against him. One of them testified that he saw him drinking it, and the other testified that he saw him vomiting it. Uthman said: He did not vomit it until he drank it. Uthman said to Ali: Carry out the punishment upon him. Ali said to his son al-Hasan: Carry out the punishment upon him. Al-Hasan said: Entrust its heat to one who undertook its cool. Ali said to Abd Allah ibn Jafar: Carry out the punishment upon him. He took the whip and began flogging him while Ali counted until he reached forty, then said to him: Stop. Then he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged forty, Abu Bakr flogged forty, and Umar flogged eighty, and each is a Sunnah, and this is more beloved to me. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that the punishment due upon the drinker of wine is this forty, arguing with these reports. Others opposed them, claimed corruption of this hadith, and denied that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said any of that, since there has been narrated from him what contradicts and rebuts it."
"4582","Namely what Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Umayr ibn Said al-Nakhai, he said: Ali said: Whoever drinks wine and we flog him and he dies, we pay his blood money, for it is something we did."
"4583","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Husayn, from Umayr ibn Said, from Ali, he said: I never carried out a prescribed punishment upon anyone and he died therein and I felt anything in myself except wine, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not institute anything therein. This is Ali, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, informing that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not instituted a prescribed punishment in drinking wine. Then the narration from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in the hadith of the drinker of wine is also contrary to what is in the first hadith of his preferring forty over eighty."
"4584","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, he said: Ali was brought al-Najashi having drunk wine in Ramadan, so he struck him eighty, then ordered him to prison, then brought him out the next day and struck him twenty, then said: I only flogged you these twenty for your breaking fast in Ramadan and your daring against Allah."
"4585","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Musab, from his father, that a man drank wine in Ramadan, then he mentioned similar to it."
"4586","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, that Ibn Shihab narrated to him, that Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to him, that a man from Kalb, the name of an Arab tribe, called Wabarah, informed him that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq used to flog forty for drink, and Umar used to flog forty therein. He said: Khalid ibn al-Walid sent me to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so I came to him and said: O Commander of the Faithful, Khalid sent me to you. He said: For what? I said: People have grown accustomed to punishment and plunged into wine, so what do you think in that? Umar said to those around him: What do you think? Ali ibn Abi Talib said: We think, O Commander of the Faithful, eighty lashes. Umar accepted that. Khalid was the first to flog eighty, then Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged people after him."
"4587","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it, except that he said: I came to Umar and found with him Ali, Talhah and al-Zubayr, or Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, reclining in the mosque, and he mentioned the same as in the hadith of Yunus. Except that he added in the speech of Ali that he said: When he becomes intoxicated he raves, when he raves he slanders, and upon the slanderer is eighty. His companions followed him, then he mentioned the hadith. Do you not see that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when asked about that, struck examples of punishments how they are, then derived therefrom a punishment by his opinion, making it like the punishment of the slanderer. Had he possessed therein anything fixed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it would have sufficed him from that; and had his companions, may Allah be pleased with them, also possessed therein anything from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they would have objected to him taking it by derivation and striking examples. What we mentioned from him and from them indicates they possessed nothing therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So how can there be accepted after this from Ali, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, what contradicts this?"
"4588","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl informed us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, he said: A group of the people of Syria drank wine while Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan was over them that day, saying: It is lawful, interpreting: No blame is upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they tasted. The verse. He wrote regarding them to Umar. Umar wrote: Send them to me before they corrupt those with you. When they came to Umar he consulted people about them. They said: O Commander of the Faithful, we see they lied against Allah and legislated in their religion what Allah did not permit, so strike their necks, while Ali was silent. He said: What do you say, O Abu al-Hasan? He said: I see that you seek their repentance; if they repent you strike them eighty each for drinking wine, and if they do not repent you strike their necks, for they lied against Allah and legislated in their religion what Allah did not permit. So he sought their repentance, they repented, and he struck them eighty each. In this hadith is that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked him about their punishment, answered him that it is eighty and did not say: If you wish make it forty, and if you wish make it eighty. This negates what is in the hadith of al-Danaj of what is mentioned therein from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding forty, and of his own preference after that. It has been narrated that the whip with which al-Walid was struck had two ends, so the stroke was two strokes."
"4589","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Ali, that Ali flogged al-Walid forty with a whip having two ends."
"4590","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to me, from Urwah, that Ali flogged al-Walid ibn Uqbah with a whip having two tails, forty lashes for wine. He said: That was in the time of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. In this hadith is that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, struck him eighty, since each lash of those lashes was two lashes. It is thus impossible that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, would say: Forty is more beloved to me than eighty, then flog himself eighty. This is also proof of the corruption of the hadith of al-Danaj. Others have narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, contrary to all that."
"4591","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abd Allah narrated to us. And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud and Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he flogged a man for wine eighty. Except that Salih said in his hadith: He flogged a man from Banu Harith ibn al-Khazraj. This in our view is also corrupt, not established from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, for what Said narrated from him of his saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died without instituting a punishment in wine, and that they made it after him eighty by the analogy we mentioned from him in this chapter. It is not permissible in our view, and Allah knows best, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he would need to derive the punishment of wine therefrom while possessing therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is in this hadith. Successive reports have come that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not aim in the punishment of the drinker at a known number of strikes, so much so that in some of what was narrated from him the negation of that is clear, like what we narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died without instituting a punishment therein."
"4592","Among what was narrated in that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar, he said: As though I now look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he is among the lodgings seeking the lodging of Khalid ibn al-Walid on the day of Hunayn. While he was so, a man who had drunk wine was brought, so he said to the people: Strike him. Some of them struck him with sandals, some struck him with a stick, and some struck him with a mitakhah, meaning a moist palm stalk. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took dust from the ground and threw it in his face."
"4593","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn going through the people, that is entering among them, asking about the dwelling of Khalid ibn al-Walid. A drunkard was brought, so he commanded those with him and they struck him with what was in their hands. Then he threw dust upon him, that is cast dust upon him with his hand. Then Abu Bakr was brought a drunkard, so he aimed at what had been of their striking before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and struck him forty. Do you not see that Abu Bakr only struck after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forty by approximation from him of the striking of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that was, since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not fixed them therein upon anything clear."
"4594","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah ibn al-Tayyah narrated to us, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said, he said: I shall not drink jar-nabidh after a drunkard was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not drink wine; I only drank date-and-raisin nabidh in a gourd. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him: he was shoved with hands and struck with sandals."
"4595","Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, he narrated to him, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a drinker, so he said: Strike him. Some of them struck him with his hand, his garment and his sandal."
"4596","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4597","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim and al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a drinker on the day of Hunayn. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people: Stand to him. The people stood and struck him with their sandals."
"4598","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn al-Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, he said: al-Nuayman was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while drunk. He said: It grieved the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with severe grief. He said: He commanded those in the house to strike him. He said: They struck him with sandals and palm stalks. Uqbah said: I was among those who struck him."
"4599","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it. Except that he said: With al-Numan, or: Ibn al-Numan."
"4600","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it. What we mentioned indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not fix them in the punishment of wine upon a known striking, as he fixed them in the punishment of adultery for the unmarried and in the punishment of slander. If one says: It has been narrated from Abu Said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck in wine with two sandals forty forty. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, made for each sandal a whip."
"4601","It is said to him: You spoke truly; Muhammad ibn Bahr, that is Ibn Matar, narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi informed us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq or Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as that. There is nothing in this hadith either indicating that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed by that striking at eighty. It is possible that he aimed at an unknown striking, so the people struck, and their striking in its total was eighty. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, aimed at that when he wanted to fix people therein upon something known, making in place of each sandal a whip."
"4602","Proof of that also is that Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged for wine with palm stalks and sandals, and Abu Bakr flogged forty. When Umar assumed authority he called the people and said: What do you think about the punishment of wine? Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: I see that you make it like the taking of punishments, making therein eighty. Had Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, known that what is in the hadith of Abu Said we mentioned was a fixing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the people upon the punishment of wine as eighty, he would not have needed therein consultation. He only consulted so that they derive a known time therein, not exceeding to more than it nor decreasing to less than it."
"4603","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, they both said: From Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who drank wine, so he commanded regarding him and he was struck with two palm stalks about forty. Then Abu Bakr did the same as that. When it was Umar, he consulted the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: O Commander of the Faithful, the lightest of punishments is eighty. So he did that. It is established by what we mentioned that fixing the punishment of wine upon known flogging was only in the time of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and that what they fixed upon thereof was eighty, with none of them opposing them therein. None should abandon that and say contrary to it, for the consensus of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is proof when free of delusion and error, like their transmission of hadith free of delusion and error. Just as their transmission which they all transmitted is proof, none may oppose it, so likewise their opinion which they all held is proof, none may oppose it."
"4604","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabiah, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, that Umar prayed over a funeral, and when he left he took the hand of a son of his, then turned to the people and said: O people, I smelled from this one the odor of drink, and I shall ask about him; if he was drunk we shall flog him. Al-Saib said: I saw Umar flog his son after that the punishment eighty."
"4605","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: al-Saib narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it. This was in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with no objector among them objecting to him, indicating their following him. There has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding fixing the punishment of wine as eighty a hadith, if it is established."
"4606","Namely what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sakhr al-Afriqi, from Jamil ibn Kurayb, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks a sip of wine, flog him eighty. This is what we found therein of fixing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the punishment of wine, namely eighty. If that is established, eighty is established thereby; and if it is not established, there is established from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we have mentioned in this chapter of their consensus upon eighty and their deriving it from the lightest of punishments. That is of their consensus after what had been contrary to it, like their consensus upon forbidding sale of slave-mothers of children, and the takbirs of funerals, though contrary had been. Just as they should not be opposed in abandoning sale of slave-mothers of children, so likewise they should not be opposed in their fixing eighty in the punishment of wine. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"4607","Chapter: One who becomes intoxicated four times, what is his punishment? Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Asim, from Dhakwan Abu Salih, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: If they drink wine, flog them, then if they drink, flog them, then if they drink at the fourth, kill them."
"4608","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Mabad al-Qass, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah al-Jadali, from Muawiyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4609","Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Khalid informed us, from al-Hasan, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. He said: Abd Allah ibn Amr said: Bring me a man upon whom I carry out the punishment three times; if I do not kill him, I am a liar."
"4610","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hadbah narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. He did not mention the saying of Abd Allah ibn Amr."
"4611","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Amr al-Zahrani narrated to us."
"4612","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they both said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4613","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Jarir, from Jarir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4614","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hubayrah narrated to us, that Abu Sulayman, freedman of Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him that Abu al-Ramda al-Balawi informed him that a man among them drank wine, so they brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he struck him, then he drank a second time and they brought him and he struck him, then he drank and they brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I do not know whether he said at the third or the fourth, so he commanded regarding him: he was placed upon the cart, then his neck was struck. Abu Jafar said: A group went to these reports, followed them, and claimed that whoever drinks wine four times his punishment is killing. Others opposed them and said: His punishment at the fourth is like his punishment at the first."
"4615","They argued against them in that with what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us."
"4616","And with what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, they both said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, that Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, thus Ibn Marzuq said in his hadith."
"4617","Yazid said in his hadith: From Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, he said: We were with Uthman while he was besieged, and he said: For what do you kill me? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The blood of a Muslim man is not lawful except by one of three: a life for a life, the married adulterer, and one who abandons his religion, leaving the community."
"4618","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from al-Amash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4619","Ali ibn Shaybah and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban informed us, from al-Amash, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4620","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4621","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us. And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah narrated to us. And Abu Umayyah also narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq said in his hadith, he said: Sulayman al-Amash narrated to us, and Ubayd Allah said in his hadith: From al-Amash, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4622","Sulayman said: I narrated it to Ibrahim, and he said: al-Aswad narrated to me, from Aishah, the same as it."
"4623","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Ghalib, he said: al-Ashtar entered upon Aishah, and she said: Did you intend to kill my sister's son? He said: He was keen to kill me and I was keen to kill him. She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and she mentioned the same as it. These reports we mentioned oppose the first reports, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in these reports that blood be lawful except by one of the three qualities mentioned therein. Yet it is possible that these reports we mentioned abrogate the first reports, so we examined that, whether we find anything of reports indicating it."
"4624","For Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Sharik, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine, flog him, then if he returns, flog him, then if he returns, flog him, then if he returns, flog him. He said: Flogging is established and killing is warded off."
"4625","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to him, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding the drinker of wine: If he drinks wine, flog him, thrice, then said at the fourth: Kill him. He was brought three times with a man who drank wine and he flogged him, then he was brought a fourth time and he flogged him, lifting killing from the people."
"4626","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb al-Kabi, that he narrated to him, that it reached him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same as it exactly. It is established by what we mentioned that killing for drinking wine at the fourth is abrogated. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. Then we returned to consideration therein to know what it is. We saw the chastisements due for violating sanctities differ. Among them is the punishment of adultery, namely flogging without marriage; whoever commits adultery unmarried and is punished, then commits adultery a second time, his punishment is likewise, and likewise his punishment at the fourth does not change from his punishment the first time. Whoever steals what cutting is due in, his punishment is cutting the hand, then if he steals a second time his punishment is cutting the foot, then if he steals a third time, his ruling is differed upon among people. Among them are those who say: His hand is cut, and among them are those who say: It is not cut. These are the rights of Allah due in less than lives. As for the limits of Allah due in lives, namely killing in apostasy and stoning in adultery when the adulterer is married: whoever commits adultery among those married is stoned, without waiting for him to commit adultery four times, and whoever apostatizes from Islam is killed, without waiting for him to apostatize four times. As for the rights of men, among them also is what is due in less than life, among that the punishment of slander; whoever slanders times, his ruling in what is due upon him for each time thereof is one ruling, unchanging, and what is due for slandering him at the fourth time does not differ from what is due upon him for slandering him the first time. Punishments do not change by repetition, so likewise the punishment of wine does not change at the fourth from what it was at the first. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad."
"4627","Chapter: The amount in which the thief is cut. Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah al-Umari narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut for a shield worth three dirhams."
"4628","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4629","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"4630","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"4631","Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who stole a shield worth three dirhams, so he cut him. Abu Jafar said: What was in these reports is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut for a shield worth three dirhams, with nothing therein that he does not cut for less than that."
"4632","We examined that, and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Salih Abu Waqid narrated to us, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thief is not cut except in the price of a shield. We know thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he cut for the shield, fixed them upon that there is no cutting for what is worth less than the worth of a shield. A group went to the view that the thief is cut in this amount which Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, estimated in the price of the shield, namely three dirhams, and is not cut for less than that, arguing with what they narrated thereof from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Others opposed them and said: The thief is not cut except in what equals ten dirhams and above."
"4633","They argued in that with what Ibn Abi Dawud and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The worth of the shield for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut was ten dirhams."
"4634","Ibn Abi Dawud and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, the same as it."
"4635","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Hisham informed me, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid and Ata, from Ayman al-Habashi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The least in which the thief is cut is the price of a shield. He said: It was valued that day a dinar."
"4636","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ata, from Ayman son of Umm Ayman, from Umm Ayman, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand of the thief is not cut except in a hajafah, and it was valued that day, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a dinar or ten dirhams. Since there was disagreement in the worth of the shield for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut, precaution was taken therein, so there is no cutting except in what is agreed that therein is fulfillment of the worth of the shield which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made an amount below which there is no cutting, namely ten dirhams. Others went to the view that there is no cutting except in a quarter dinar and above."
"4637","They argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut in a quarter dinar and above. It is said to them: This is also no proof against those who went to the view that there is no cutting except in ten dirhams, for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, only reported what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut in. It is possible that is because she valued what he cut in, and its worth with her was a quarter dinar, so she made that the amount in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut."
"4638","They also argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah and Amrah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand of the thief is cut in a quarter dinar and above. They said: This is information from Aishah, may Allah Almighty be pleased with her, from the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), indicating that what we mentioned from her in the first hadith of the Prophet cutting in a quarter dinar and above, she only took that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he fixed her upon as in this hadith, not by her valuation of what he cut in. It is said to them: This is as you mentioned, had there been no disagreement therein from her. Ibn Uyaynah narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah, from Aishah, what we mentioned in the preceding section, which was information from her about the act of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not about his saying. Yunus ibn Yazid in your view does not approximate Ibn Uyaynah, so how do you argue with what he narrated and abandon what Ibn Uyaynah narrated? They said: This hadith has also been narrated from another angle, from Amrah, from Aishah, as Yunus ibn Yazid narrated it."
"4639","They mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Amrah, from Aishah, that she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The hand of the thief is not cut except in a quarter dinar and above. It is said to them: How do you argue with this, while you claim that Makhramah did not hear a letter from his father, that what is narrated from him is disconnected, and you do not argue with the disconnected?"
"4640","Among what they mention negating thereby the hearing of Makhramah from his father is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from his maternal uncle Musa ibn Salamah, he said: I asked Makhramah ibn Bukayr: Did you hear anything from your father? He said: No. They said: This hadith has also been narrated from Amrah, as Yunus ibn Yazid narrated it from al-Zuhri from her, by Yahya ibn Said also."
"4641","4959 - They cited regarding that what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Saeed narrated to us, from Amra, from Aisha, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand of the thief is cut off for a quarter dinar and above. It was said to them: This hadith was narrated from Yahya by one more reliable than Aban, who stopped it at Aisha and did not raise it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4642","4960 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Amra bint Abd al-Rahman, that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: Long time never passed over me - i.e. time never grew long over me - nor did I forget: cutting is for a quarter dinar and above."
"4643","4961 - Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, from Sufyan, saying: Four narrated from Amra, from Aisha, without raising it: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, and Zurayq ibn Hakim al-Ayli, and Yahya and Abd Rabbihi, the two sons of Saeed; and al-Zuhri was the most preserving of them all. Except that in the hadith of Yahya there is what indicates raising it: I neither forgot nor did long time pass over me: cutting is for a quarter dinar and above."
"4644","4962 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Yahya ibn Saeed, saying: Amra narrated to me that she heard Aisha saying: Cutting is for a quarter dinar and above. So the basis of the hadith of Yahya from Amra is what we mentioned, reported from him by the people of memorization and precision, Malik and Ibn Uyayna, not as Aban ibn Yazid narrated it. Thus the hadith of Yahya ibn Saeed from Amra from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, returns to herself alone, either as her own valuation of what was disputed regarding its fixing, with nothing established from her from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for what Ibn Uyayna inferred, that the hadith of Aisha from Yahya ibn Saeed from Amra from her is raised, by her saying: Long time never passed over me, nor did I forget - then that, in our view, proves nothing of what was mentioned; it may mean: long time never passed over me nor did I forget what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut for, of that whose value in her view was a quarter dinar while its value in the view of others was more than that. Then the meaning of this hadith of hers returns to the meaning of what we already narrated from her, of her mention of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut for, and of her valuation of it as a quarter dinar. If they say: Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated from Amra from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the same as Aban ibn Yazid narrated from Amra from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her."
"4645","4963 - They mentioned regarding that what Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, saying: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Amra bint Abd al-Rahman, from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The hand of the thief is not cut off except for a quarter dinar and above."
"4646","4964 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Amir narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4647","4965 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: al-Layth narrated to me, saying: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4648","4966 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: Hushaym informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Amra, from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. It was said to them: This was narrated as you mentioned, but by your own principles you cannot use this hadith to counter what al-Zuhri narrated, nor what Yahya and Abd Rabbihi, the two sons of Saeed, narrated; for Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm does not possess the precision nor the memorization that any one of these has, nor does Ibn al-Had, who narrated this hadith from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad; and Muhammad ibn Ishaq, in your view, does not possess the precision in narration and memorization that those who narrated the hadith of al-Zuhri and of Yahya and Abd Rabbihi, the two sons of Saeed, possess. Moreover, Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad was also contradicted in what he narrated from Amra by his son Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr."
"4649","4967 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amra, who said: Aisha said: Cutting is for a quarter dinar and above. He was also contradicted in that by Ruzayq ibn Hakim, who narrated it from Amra the same as Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, and Yahya, and Abd Rabbihi narrated it from her. So he said: If this matter is taken by the multitude of narrators, then those who narrated the hadith of Amra from her contrary to what Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad narrated from her are greater in number. And if it is taken by precision and memorization among narrators, then those who narrated the hadith of Amra from her, Yahya and Abd Rabbihi, possess a precision in narration and accuracy that Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad does not possess. If they say: Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and others narrated from Amra the same as Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad narrated from her."
"4650","4968 - They mentioned regarding that what Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Rabia, from al-Ala ibn al-Aswad ibn Jariya, and Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, and Kathir ibn Khunays, that they disputed about cutting, so they entered upon Amra to ask her. She said: Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting except for a quarter dinar. It was said to them: As for Abu Salama, we know of no hearing of Jafar ibn Rabia from him, nor do we know that he ever met him at all; so how is it lawful for you to argue with the like of this against your opponent, and to counter with it what those we mentioned narrated from Amra?"
"4651","4969 - And if they also argue regarding that with the hadith of al-Zuhri, then Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: al-Zuhri narrated to us, saying: Amra bint Abd al-Rahman informed me that she heard Aisha saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thief is cut for a quarter dinar and above."
"4652","4970 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amra, from Aisha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thief, when he steals a quarter dinar, is cut."
"4653","4971 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, saying: Asad narrated to us, saying: Ibrahim ibn Saad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amra, from Aisha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand is cut for a quarter dinar and above. It was said to them: We already narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri in this chapter, in the hadith of Ibn Uyayna, with a different wording whose meaning contradicts this meaning, namely: the hand was cut in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a quarter dinar and above. So when the hadith of al-Zuhri became confused as we mentioned, and reports from others from Amra differed as we described, all of that falls away, and no proof is established by any of it, since part of it negates part. And we returned to Allah Almighty saying in His Book: And the male thief and the female thief, cut off their hands as recompense for what they earned, as exemplary punishment from Allah. They agreed that Allah Almighty did not mean by that every thief, and that He meant thereby only specific thieves for a known amount of wealth. So nothing enters into what they agreed that Allah Almighty meant except what they agreed that Allah Almighty meant. They agreed that Allah Almighty meant the thief of ten dirhams, and they differed about the thief of less than that. One group said: he is among those Allah Almighty meant. Another group said: he is not among them. So it was not lawful for us, given their disagreement, to testify against Allah Almighty that He meant what they did not agree He meant. And it was lawful for us to testify, regarding what they agreed Allah meant, that He meant it. So we placed the thief of ten dirhams and above inside the verse and cut him by it, and we placed the thief of less than ten outside the verse and did not cut him. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all; and it was narrated from Ibn Masud, and Ata, and Amr ibn Shuayb."
"4654","4972 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from al-Masudi, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abdullah ibn Masud said: The hand is not cut except for a dinar or ten dirhams."
"4655","4973 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, saying: The saying of Ata followed the saying of Amr ibn Shuayb: The hand is not cut for less than ten dirhams. And praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"4656","Chapter 9: Confession of the theft that necessitates cutting. 4974 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Awn, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, saying: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfa, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayra, who said: A thief was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said: O Messenger of Allah, this man stole. He said: I do not think he stole. The thief said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Take him away and cut him, then cauterize him, then bring him to me. He said: So he was taken, cut, then cauterized, then brought; and he said: Repent to Allah Almighty. He said: I repent to Allah. He said: May Allah accept your repentance."
"4657","4975 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Khusayfa, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4658","4976 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfa, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4659","4977 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating that Yazid ibn Khusayfa informed him that he heard Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"4660","4978 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, saying: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Habib narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Thalaba al-Ansari, from his father, that Amr ibn Samura ibn Habib ibn Abd Nahs came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I stole a camel belonging to such-and-such tribe. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to them, and they said: We lost a camel of ours. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered regarding him, and his hand was cut. Thalaba said: I was looking at him when his hand was cut while he was saying: Praise belongs to Allah who purified me from what would have caused my body to enter the Fire. Abu Jafar said: A group held that when a man confesses theft once, he is cut, and they argued for that with this hadith. Among those who held that were Abu Hanifa and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them. Others opposed them, among them Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and they said: He is not cut until he confesses twice."
"4661","4979 - They argued for that with what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, saying: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj and Muhammad ibn Awn al-Ziyadi narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, saying: Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha informed me, from Abu al-Mundhir, freedman of Abu Dharr, from Abu Umayya al-Makhzumi, that a thief was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who confessed a confession, with no goods found with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I do not think you stole. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated it to him two or three times. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he ordered regarding him and he was cut. Then he was brought, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Say: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him. He said: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him. Then he said: O Allah, accept his repentance. So in this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not cut him for confessing once until he confessed a second time. This is more worthy than the first hadith, because it adds to what is in the first. It is possible that one of them abrogated the other. Since that was possible, we returned to reflection, and we found the Sunna established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one who confesses adultery, that he turned him away four times, and that he did not stone him for confessing once; and that was removed from the ruling of confession regarding rights of people, in which confession once is accepted, and the ruling of confession in that was referred to the ruling of testimony against him. Just as testimony against him was not accepted except from four, likewise confession of it was made to require flogging only by his confessing four times. Thus it is established that the ruling of confession of theft is likewise referred to the ruling of testimony against it. Just as testimony for it is not valid except from two, likewise confession of it is not accepted except twice. We saw them all, when they narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one who confessed adultery, when he fled, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not leave him alone. That, in their view, meant his retraction is accepted; and they applied that in all the hadd punishments of Allah Almighty, making one who confessed them then retracted before it, his retraction accepted, without restricting adultery by that apart from other hadds of Allah. Likewise, when confession in adultery was made acceptable only by the number of witnesses accepted for it, it is established that confession of other hadds of Allah is not accepted except by the number of witnesses accepted for them. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, objected against Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, saying: If he is not cut for theft until its thief confesses it twice, then when he confessed the first time, what he confessed became a debt upon him, and cutting would no longer be due after that, since the thief is not cut for what became due upon him as a debt by his taking it. Part of our argument for Abu Yusuf, may Allah's mercy be upon him, was that if that bound Abu Yusuf in theft, the like would bind Muhammad in adultery also, since the adulterer in their saying is not punished with hadd for that in which a dower became due upon him, just as the thief is not cut for what became due upon him as a debt. If this reasoning with which Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, argued against Abu Yusuf, invalidated the saying of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, regarding confession of theft, Muhammad would be bound by the like regarding confession of adultery. That is because when he confessed adultery once, no hadd became due upon him, yet he confessed an intercourse for which he is not punished by that confession, so a dower became due upon him; then he should not be punished for an intercourse in which a dower became due upon him. Since Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on him, was not bound by that as proof regarding confession of adultery, likewise Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, is not bound by that as proof regarding confession of theft. And Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, turned away one who confessed theft before him twice."
"4662","4980 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, that a man confessed before him to theft twice, and he said: You have borne witness against yourself twice. He said: So he ordered regarding him and he was cut, and he hung it on his neck. Do you not see that Ali, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, referred the ruling of confession of theft to the ruling of testimony for it in the number of witnesses; likewise confession of all the hadds of Allah is not accepted except by the number of witnesses accepted for them."
"4663","Chapter 10: A man borrows jewelry and does not return it - is there cutting for that or not? Abu Jafar said: It was narrated from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, that a woman used to borrow jewelry and not return it. He said: She was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and was cut. 4981 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, saying: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, who said: A Makhzumi woman used to borrow goods and deny them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her hand cut. Her family came to Usama ibn Zayd and spoke to him, and Usama spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usama, I see you speaking to me about one of the hadds of Allah Almighty. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to preach and said: Those before you were destroyed only because when the noble among them stole they left him, and when the weak among them stole they cut him. By Him in whose hand is my soul, if Fatima bint Muhammad were, I would cut her hand. So he cut the hand of the Makhzumi woman. Abu Jafar said: A group held that whoever borrows something and denies it must be cut for it, and in their view he was thereby in the meaning of a thief, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others opposed them and said: He is not cut but guarantees it. Part of the proof for them was that Mamar narrated this hadith as they mentioned, but others narrated it and added in it: That woman who used to borrow jewelry and not return it stole, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut her for her theft."
"4664","4982 - Among what was narrated regarding that is what Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Urwa ibn al-Zubayr informed him, from Aisha, that a woman stole in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the year of the Conquest, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her cut. Usama ibn Zayd spoke to him about her, and the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color - i.e. changed from anger - and he said: Do you intercede regarding one of the hadds of Allah Almighty? Usama said to him: Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah. When evening came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah as He deserves, then said: To proceed, those before you were destroyed only because when the noble among them stole they left him, and when the weak among them stole they established the hadd upon him. By Him in whose hand is my soul, if Fatima bint Muhammad stole, I would cut her hand. Then he ordered regarding that woman who stole, and her hand was cut."
"4665","4983 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa, from Aisha, that Quraysh were concerned about the affair of the Makhzumi woman who stole, and they said: Who dares speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about her? They said: Who dares but Usama? Then he mentioned the like of its meaning. So it is established by this hadith that the cutting was for other than the borrowed denied property."
"4666","4984 - There was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what negates cutting in betrayal, what Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting upon the traitor, nor upon the snatcher, nor upon the plunderer."
"4667","4985 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Makki ibn Ibrahim al-Balkhi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4668","4986 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, saying: al-Mughira ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Since the traitor incurs no cutting, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between him and the thief, and the Sunna firmly established the affair of the thief upon whom cutting is due, that he is one who steals a known amount of wealth from safekeeping, and the borrower took the borrowed wealth from non-safekeeping, it is established that there is no cutting upon him in that for lack of safekeeping. And what we mentioned, by which we validated the meanings of these reports, is the saying of Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"4669","Chapter 11: Theft of fruits and kathar. 4987 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, that a slave stole a young palm shoot from a man's garden and planted it in his master's garden. The owner of the shoot went seeking his shoot and found it, and complained against the slave before Marwan ibn al-Hakam. So he imprisoned the slave and intended to cut his hand. The slave's master went to Rafi ibn Khadij and informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar. The man said: Marwan ibn al-Hakam took my servant and intends to cut his hand, and I would like you to walk with me to him and inform him of what you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say. So Rafi walked with him until he came to Marwan and said: You took a slave of this man? He said: Yes. He said: What will you do with him? He said: I intended to cut his hand. Rafi said to him: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar. So Marwan ordered regarding the slave and he was released."
"4670","4988 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, that a slave stole a young palm shoot from a man's garden and brought it and planted it in another place. He was brought to Marwan, who intended to cut him, so Rafi ibn Khadij testified that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathar. Abu Jafar said: A group held that there is no cutting for any fruits nor kathar, whether taken from its owner's garden or his house after he picked and stored it in it. They said: There is also no cutting for palm fronds nor its wood; for Rafi did not ask about the value of the fronds in the stolen shoot, nor about the value of its trunk, and he averted cutting from the thief in that, by the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no cutting for kathar, which is jummar (palm heart). Thus it is established that there is no cutting for jummar, nor for its fronds, wood, and fruits. Among those who said that was Abu Hanifa, may Allah's mercy be upon him. Others opposed them and said: What Rafi reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor kathar, refers to fruits and kathar taken from the garden, which is not safekeeping for what is in it. As for what was stored of that, its ruling is the ruling of other wealth, and cutting is due upon whoever steals of that the amount in which cutting is due. They argued for that with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this book, in other than this chapter, when he was asked about hanging fruits, and he said: There is no cutting in it, except what the threshing floor sheltered and reached the price of a shield - in it is cutting; and what did not reach the price of a shield, in it is a fine of its like and disciplinary lashes."
"4671","4989 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: al-Wahbi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that also. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished among stolen fruits between what the threshing floor sheltered and what it did not shelter while on its trees; and he placed cutting in what the threshing floor sheltered, and fine and exemplary punishment in what it did not shelter. So reconciling this hadith and what Rafi narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor kathar, is to make what Rafi narrated refer to what was in gardens whose contents were not stored, matching what is in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr, of what exceeds what is in the hadith of Rafi; so in that there is cutting, and no cutting in other than that. These two reports thus agree and do not conflict. This is the saying of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him."
"4672","Book 12: Offences. Chapter 1: What is due in intentional murder and intentional wounds. 4990 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, saying: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me. 4991 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Abu Dawud narrated to us, saying: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, saying: Abu Salama narrated to us, saying: Abu Hurayra narrated to me, saying: When Allah gave His Messenger victory over Mecca, Hudhayl killed a man of Banu Layth for a slain one of theirs in pre-Islamic times. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and preached, saying in his sermon: Whoever has a slain one, he is given the better of two views: either he kills, or blood-money is paid for him. The wording is Muhammad ibn Abdullah's. Abu Bakra said in his hadith: Khuzaa killed a man of Banu Layth. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is mention of what is due for the soul specifically. It was also narrated from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of that."
"4673","4992 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Musaddad narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Saeed narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, saying: Saeed al-Maqburi narrated to me, saying: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Kabi saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his sermon on the day of the conquest of Mecca: Verily you, O tribe of Khuzaa, killed this slain one of Hudhayl, and I shall pay his blood-money; whoever has a slain one after my saying, his family is between two choices: between taking the blood-money, and between killing. It was also narrated from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai by another route, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), regarding less than the soul, the like of that also."
"4674","4993 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Fudayl, from Sufyan ibn Abi al-Awja, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is afflicted with blood or with khabl - meaning by khabl wounds - his guardian has the choice among one of three: between pardoning, or retaliating, or taking the blood-money; if he comes with a fourth, take him by his hand; if he accepts one of them then transgresses after that, for him is the Fire, abiding therein forever."
"4675","4994 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: Abbad narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, saying: al-Harith ibn Fudayl informed me, from Sufyan ibn Abi al-Arja, from Abu Shurayh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. So in this hadith is that the ruling of intentional wounds is, in what is due for each of them, retaliation and blood-money. Abu Jafar said: A group held that when a man is killed intentionally, his guardian has the choice between pardoning, or taking blood-money, or retaliating, whether the killer agrees or not; and they argued for that with these reports. Others opposed them and said: He may not take blood-money except with the killer's consent. Part of the proof for them was that His saying: or takes the blood-money, may be upon what the holders of the first saying stated, and it may mean he takes the blood-money if he is given it, as one says to a man: Take for your debt, if you wish dirhams, if you wish dinars, if you wish goods - without meaning he takes that whether the debtor agrees or hates it, but meaning it is permitted to him if he is given it. If one asks: What need had they to mention this? It is said to him: Because of what was narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them."
"4676","4995 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Retaliation was among the Children of Israel, with no blood-money among them. So Allah Almighty said to this community: Retaliation is prescribed for you in murders, the free for the free, up to His saying: But whoever is pardoned anything by his brother - and pardoning is accepting blood-money in intentional killing: That is alleviation from your Lord, from what was prescribed upon those before you. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them, informed that the Children of Israel had no blood-money, i.e. it was forbidden for them to take it or to seek a substitute for blood, or to leave it until they shed it, and that was among what was prescribed upon them. So Allah Almighty lightened for this community and abrogated that ruling by His saying: But whoever is pardoned anything by his brother, then following with kindness and payment to him with excellence - meaning when payment becomes due. We shall explain what was said regarding that in its place in this chapter, Allah Almighty willing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also explained that to them in this manner, saying: Whoever has a guardian killed, he has the choice between retaliating, or pardoning, or taking the blood-money which was permitted to this community, granting them its taking when they are given it. This is a construction this hadith bears. No one, when a hadith like this bears two equal constructions, may incline it to one without the other except by evidence from elsewhere indicating its meaning is as he inclined it. So we looked into that: do we find anything indicating any of that? The holders of the first saying said: Allah Almighty said: But whoever is pardoned anything by his brother, then following with kindness and payment to him with excellence, that is alleviation from your Lord and mercy - the verse. So Allah Almighty informed in this verse that the guardian may pardon or follow the killer with excellence; they inferred thereby that the guardian, when he pardons, may take blood-money from the killer even if he did not stipulate that in his pardoning him. It was said to them: There is no proof in this for what you mentioned; it bears constructions, one what you described. It also bears: But whoever is pardoned anything by his brother, upon the manner we stated, with the killer's consent that he pardons him for what is taken from him. It also bears that it concerns blood shared among a group, where one of them pardons, so the rest follow the killer for their shares of blood-money with kindness, and he pays that to them with excellence. These are interpretations scholars placed upon this verse; so there is no proof in it for some against others except by other evidence in another verse agreed upon in interpretation, or a Sunna, or consensus. In the hadith of Abu Shurayh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He has the choice between pardoning, or killing, or taking blood-money - making his pardoning other than his taking blood-money. Thus it is established that when he pardons, he has no blood-money; and when he has no blood-money upon pardoning blood, it is established that what became due to him was blood, and that his taking the blood-money permitted to him is by way of taking it as substitute for killing. Substitutes for things we never found due except by consent of both the one on whom it is due and the one to whom it is due. Once that is established in killing, what we mentioned is established and our opponent's claim falls. Since the holders of the first saying had nothing proving their saying, we considered: do the others have a report proving what they said?"
"4677","4996 - Abu Bakra and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, saying: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik ibn al-Nadr, that his aunt al-Rubayyi slapped a slave girl and broke her front tooth. They sought pardon from them and they refused, and compensation and they refused, except retaliation. They disputed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered retaliation. Anas ibn al-Nadr said: O Messenger of Allah, shall the front tooth of al-Rubayyi be broken? No, by Him who sent you with truth, her front tooth shall not be broken. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, the Book of Allah is retaliation. The people were satisfied and pardoned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among Allah's servants are those who, if they swear by Allah, He fulfills it - some adding upon others. Since the ruling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged against al-Rubayyi for the one whose tooth was removed was retaliation, without giving her choice between retaliation and taking blood-money, and Anas ibn al-Nadr was stirred when he refused that, saying: O Anas, the Book of Allah is retaliation - then the people pardoned, and he did not judge blood-money for them. Thus it is established that what is due by Allah Almighty's Book and His Messenger's Sunna in intentional injury is retaliation; for if the injured one had choice between retaliation and pardoning for what the offender pays, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have given her choice and informed her what she might choose. Do you not see that if a judge is approached by a man regarding something in which one of two things is due to him, and his right is established before him, he does not judge for him one of the two things apart from the other, but judges that he choose what he likes of this and that; if he exceeds that he falls short of understanding the ruling - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the wisest of judges. Since he judged retaliation and informed it is Allah Almighty's Book, it is established that in the like thereof is retaliation and nothing else. Since this hadith is established as we mentioned, the hadith of Abu Shurayh and Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with them, must be inclined to it, making the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein: He has the choice between pardoning, or retaliating, or taking blood-money, refer to the offender's consent to pay blood-money, so the meanings of these two hadiths agree with the meaning of the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. If one says: Reflection supports what the holders of the first saying said, namely people must preserve their lives; so when the one entitled to shed blood says: I am content taking blood-money and leaving bloodshed - the killer must preserve his life, and when that becomes due upon him it is taken from his wealth though he hates it. The proof against him is that people preserving their lives, as mentioned, is by blood-money, by more than blood-money, and by all they own. We saw them agree that if the guardian said to the killer: I am content to take this house of yours on condition I do not kill you - then what is due upon the killer between him and Allah is to surrender it to him and spare his own blood; yet if he refuses he is not compelled to it, by their agreement on that, nor is it taken from him by force and paid to the guardian. Likewise blood-money when the guardian seeks it: the killer must between him and his Lord preserve his life by it, yet refusing he is not compelled nor taken by force. Then we returned to the holders of the first saying in their saying: the guardian may take blood-money though the offender hates it. We say to them: That is one of three possibilities: either what he has upon the killer is retaliation and blood-money together, so pardoning retaliation he may take blood-money; or what became due is retaliation specifically, with right to take blood-money as substitute; or what became due is one of two things, retaliation or blood-money, choosing what he wills - it is one of these three, no more. If you say both together, that is corrupt, for Allah Almighty never required of one who did an act more than he did; He Almighty said: We prescribed for them therein: a soul for a soul, an eye for an eye, a nose for a nose, an ear for an ear, a tooth for a tooth, and wounds in retaliation. Had it been so he would be killed and blood-money taken; since after his killing no blood-money is taken, what became due differs from your claim. If you say retaliation became due but he may take blood-money as substitute, we find no right of a man to take its substitute without consent of who owes it - so this too falls. If you say one of two, retaliation or blood-money, taking whichever he likes, neither due apart from the other - then his pardoning one specifically should be invalid, for his right was not the pardoned thing specifically to void it; he might only choose it to become his right, or choose the other to become his right. Pardoning one before choosing it specifically, before it became due to him specifically, his pardon is void. Do you not see that if a man's father were intentionally wounded and he pardoned his father's wounder, then his father died of those wounds with no heir but him - his pardon is void, for he pardoned before the pardoned became due to him. Since pardoning before the killer chooses retaliation or blood-money is valid, it is established retaliation had become due to him specifically before his pardoning it; otherwise he could not void it by pardoning, as the son's pardoning his father's blood before it became due was invalid. In establishing what we mentioned and negating these aspects is proof that what is due upon the intentional killer or wounder is retaliation and nothing else of blood-money or otherwise - unless he, if alive, or his heir if dead, settles with who owes it upon something; settlement valid upon what they settle of blood-money or otherwise. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"4678","Chapter 2: A man kills a man - how is he killed? 4997 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Abu Dawud narrated to us, saying: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, that a Jew crushed a boy's head between two stones, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his head crushed between two stones. Abu Jafar said: A group adopted this hadith and said: Every killer is killed by what he killed with. Others opposed them and said: Whoever incurs retaliation is killed only by the sword. They said: This hadith you narrated may mean the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw that killer deserved killing for Allah, since he killed for wealth - as clarified in part of the hadith."
"4679","4998 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, saying: Ibrahim ibn Saad narrated to us, from Shuba, from Hisham ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: A Jew attacked a slave girl in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), taking silver ornaments she wore, and crushed her head. Her family brought her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while she was at her last breath, having lost speech. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Who killed you? So-and-so? - for other than her killer - and she motioned with her head: No. He named another man other than her killer, and she motioned with her head: No. He said: So-and-so, naming her killer, and she motioned: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered regarding him and his head was crushed between two stones. If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made that Jew's blood due to Allah Almighty, as the blood of a highway robber is due to Allah Almighty, then he might be killed as he wished, by sword or otherwise - mutilation then being lawful, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with the Uranis."
"4680","4999 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas, who said: Eight men of Ukl came, found Medina unhealthy, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent them to camels of his and they drank their milk. When they recovered they apostatized from Islam, killed the camel herdsman, and drove off the camels. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent in pursuit; they were seized, and he cut their hands and feet, branded their eyes, and left them until they died."
"4681","5000 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"4682","5001 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, saying: Qabisa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger - he said: They were of Ukl; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut their hands and feet and branded their eyes."
"4683","5002 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Mansur narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas."
"4684","5003 - Salih narrated to us, saying: Saeed narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut their hands and feet, branded their eyes, and left them until they died."
"4685","5004 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, saying: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, saying: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Qurra, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: A group from an Arab tribe came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), embraced Islam and pledged him. He said: Brain fever - namely barsam - struck; they said: O Messenger of Allah, this pain has struck; if you permit us to go out to the camels and stay among them? He said: Yes, go out and stay among them. He said: They went out, killed one of the two herdsmen, and took the camels. He said: The other came forth and said: They killed my companion and took the camels. He said: With him were youths of the Ansar, near twenty. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the youths to them, with a tracker, who traced their tracks; they were brought, and he cut their hands and feet and branded their eyes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with the Uranis what he did, since shedding their blood became lawful to him; he might kill them as he liked, even if that was mutilation, for mutilation was then lawful, then abrogated thereafter, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it, so none might do it. It is possible he did with the Jew what he did for that reason, then that was abrogated after the abrogation of mutilation. It is also possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not see what was due upon the Jew as due to Allah Almighty, but saw it due to the girl's guardians and killed him for them. It is possible he killed him as he did because that was what was due upon him. It is possible what was due upon him was shedding blood by whatever means the guardian wished to shed it with, and they chose crushing, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that for them. These are constructions this hadith bears, with no proof indicating the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended some to the exclusion of others. It was also narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he killed that Jew differently from how he had killed the girl."
"4686","5005 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abu Yala Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, saying: Abu Safwan Abdullah ibn Saeed ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us - Ibn Abi Dawud said: he was trustworthy - raising it from Ibn Jurayj, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas, that a Jewish man crushed a slave girl's head for her jewelry, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him stoned until killed. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed that Jew by stoning, for his killing the girl as mentioned in this report and in what we present of reports, namely his crushing her head; stoning may strike the head and other than the head. So he killed him by other than what he had killed the girl with. That proves what he did was lawful that day, then abrogated by the abrogation of mutilation."
"4687","5006 - Among what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on abrogating mutilation is what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, saying: Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ikrima, who said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mujaththama - and mujaththama is a sheep shot with arrows until killed."
"4688","5007 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us. 5008 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us, saying: Shuba informed us, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Saeed ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not take anything with a soul as a target."
"4689","5009 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, saying: Shuba informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4690","5010 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, saying: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal and Simak, from Ikrima - one of them said: from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the same."
"4691","5011 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, saying: Shuba narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4692","5012 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, from al-Amash, saying: al-Minhal ibn Amr narrated to me, from Saeed ibn Jubayr - or Mujahid - who said: Ibn Umar passed by a hen set up to be shot at, and Ibn Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding mutilation of animals."
"4693","5013 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: My uncle - namely Ibn Wahb - narrated to me, saying: Amr ibn al-Harith and Ibn Lahia narrated to me that Bukayr ibn Abdullah narrated to them, from his father, from Ibn Tila, that he said: We raided with Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid, and four enemy non-Arabs were brought; Abd al-Rahman ordered them killed by confinement with arrows. That reached Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding killing by confinement. By Him in whose hand is my soul, were it a hen I would not confine it. That reached Abd al-Rahman, so he freed four slaves."
"4694","5014 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: al-Wahbi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Bukayr, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4695","5015 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, saying: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from his father, from Ubayd ibn Yala, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade confining an animal. Abu Ayyub said: Were it a hen I would not confine it."
"4696","5016 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: Hushaym informed us, from Mansur, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to preach to us, commanding charity and forbidding mutilation."
"4697","5017 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, saying: Samura ibn Jundub narrated to us, saying: Rarely did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preach a sermon to us without commanding charity in it and forbidding mutilation in it."
"4698","5018 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: al-Hasan narrated to us, saying: Samura said: Rarely did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand preaching among us without commanding charity and forbidding mutilation."
"4699","5019 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Wahb narrated to us, saying: Shuba narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Yazid, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade confining animals."
"4700","5020 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, saying: al-Qasim - meaning Ibn Malik - narrated to us, from Maslama ibn Nawfal al-Thaqafi, saying: al-Mughira ibn Safiyya narrated to us, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutilation."
"4701","5021 - Ibn Abi Imran and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, saying: Ghundar narrated to us, from Shuba, from Mughira, from Shibak, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayra, from Alqama, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The best of people in killing are the people of faith."
"4702","5022 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughira, from Ibrahim - without mentioning Shibak - from Hunayy, from Alqama, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Thus the abrogation of mutilation is established by these reports, after it was lawful as we narrated in the hadith of the Uranis. If one says: What we dispute about retaliation does not enter into this, for Allah Almighty said: If you punish, punish with the like of what you were punished with. It is said to him: This verse is not intended for this meaning; it was intended for what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported by Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with them."
"4703","5023 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, saying: Qays narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, who said: When Hamza was killed and mutilated, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I prevail over them I shall mutilate seventy men of them. So Allah Almighty revealed: If you punish, punish with the like of what you were punished with, but if you are patient it is better for the patient. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather we shall be patient."
"4704","5024 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us. 5025 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, saying: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, saying: Salih al-Murri narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over Hamza when he was martyred and saw a sight more painful to his heart than he ever saw. He said: May Allah have mercy on you; you were one who joined kinship, active in good deeds; were it not for the grief of those after you, I would be pleased to leave you until you are gathered from scattered multitudes. By Allah, I shall mutilate seventy of them in your place. Then Gabriel, peace be upon him, descended upon him, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was still standing, with the endings of Surat al-Nahl: If you punish, punish with the like of what you were punished with, but if you are patient it is better for the patient, to the end of the sura. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was patient and expiated his oath. This verse was revealed only in this sense, not in the sense you mentioned. It was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no retaliation except by the sword."
"4705","5026 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, saying: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Jabir, from Abu Azib, from al-Numan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no retaliation except by the sword. This hadith proves retaliation for every slain one is only by the sword. There also came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what further proves what we mentioned."
"4706","5027 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, saying: Asad narrated to us, saying: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Unaysa, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that wounds were brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he ordered them to wait a year with them."
"4707","5028 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, saying: Mahdi ibn Jafar narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Anbasa ibn Saeed, from al-Shabi, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: No retaliation is taken for a wound until it heals. Had the offender be treated as he did, as the holders of the first saying stated, waiting would have no meaning, for the cutter's hand must be cut whether the victim healed or died. Since waiting is established, to see what the offence results in, it is established that what requires retaliation is what the offence results in, nothing else. If one impugns Yahya ibn Abi Unaysa and rejects our arguing by his hadith, then Ali ibn al-Madini mentioned from Yahya ibn Saeed that he is dearer to him in the hadith of al-Zuhri than Muhammad ibn Ishaq."
"4708","5029 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Ashath, from Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah prescribed excellence upon everything; when you kill, kill excellently, and when you slaughter, slaughter excellently; let each of you sharpen his blade and comfort his slaughtered animal. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered people to kill excellently and to comfort what Allah permitted them to slaughter of livestock; what He permitted them to kill of mankind deserves that even more. If one says: One does not wait for wounds to heal, opposing the reports we mentioned - his ignorance suffices, in opposing all scholars before him. Moreover, we invalidate his saying by reflection: we saw that if a man cut a man's hand by mistake and it healed, blood-money of the hand is due; if he died of it, blood-money of the soul is due, with nothing due for the hand, what was due for the hand merging into what is due for the soul. The offender becomes as one who killed, not as one who cut; the hand has no ruling except while the soul remains, and none when the soul is lost. By reflection it should likewise be when he cut his hand intentionally: if he heals, the ruling is for the hand, in it retaliation; if he dies of it, the ruling is for the soul, in it retaliation, not in the hand - by analogy with the ruling of mistake. One who says the offender is killed as he killed must also say: if he shot him with an arrow and killed him, the shooter is set up and shot until killed - yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade confining a living soul, so none should be confined, given the Prophet's prohibition, but killed a killing free of anything prohibited. Do you not see that if a man sodomized a man and killed him thereby, the guardian may not do to the killer as he did, but must kill him, for sodomizing him is forbidden to him. Likewise confining him as described is forbidden, but he may kill him as one whose blood became lawful is killed, by apostasy or otherwise. This is reflection, and the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all. Except Abu Hanifa, may Allah be pleased with him, did not require retaliation for one killed by stone; we shall explain his saying and its proof in the chapter of quasi-intentional killing, Allah willing."
"4709","Chapter 3: Quasi-intentional killing in which there is no retaliation - what is it? 5030 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Qasim ibn Rabia ibn Jawshan, from Uqba ibn Aws al-Sadusi, from a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached on the day of the conquest of Mecca, saying in his sermon: Verily the slain of mistaken-intent with whip, stick and stone incurs aggravated blood-money: one hundred camels, forty of them pregnant with their young in their bellies. Abu Jafar said: A group adopted this hadith, saying: No retaliation upon one who kills a man with stick or stone. Among them Abu Hanifa, may Allah be pleased with him. Others opposed them, among them Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them, saying: If the wood is such as kills, its killer incurs retaliation - that is intentional. If its like does not kill, in it is blood-money - that is quasi-intentional. They said: What the holders of the first saying argued against us, the Prophet's saying: Verily the slain of mistaken-intent with whip, stick and stone incurs one hundred camels, proves nothing they claim; for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have meant a stick whose like does not kill, like a whip whose like does not kill. If he meant that, it is what we said; if he did not mean that but meant what you claim, then we abandoned the hadith and opposed it. Yet we never affirmed opposing this hadith, since we say: some sticks, when killing with them, incur no retaliation. Our construction of this hadith is worthier than theirs, for ours does not contradict the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), requiring retaliation against the Jew who crushed the girl's head with stone; theirs contradicts and negates it. Construing hadiths to agree is worthier than construing them to conflict. If one says: You said this hadith of Anas is abrogated in the first chapter, how do you affirm acting by it here? It is said: We did not say this hadith of Anas is abrogated in the sense you mentioned; the obligation of retaliation and killing by stone is established in the hadith of Anas. We said only that retaliation by stone may be abrogated, per the proof mentioned. So the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, on obligating retaliation is with us unabrogated. As for the manner of due retaliation, it may be abrogated as we explained in the preceding chapter. Part of the proof of those who said killing by stone incurs no retaliation, deflecting the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, is that what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) required of killing therein may have been a right of Allah Almighty, making the Jew like a highway robber upon whom a hadd of Allah Almighty is due. If so, a highway robber who kills with stone or stick incurs killing even in the view of one who claims no retaliation upon one who kills with stick - a group of speculators held this. Abu Hanifa, may Allah be pleased with him, said of strangling: In it is blood-money, and he is killed only if he does it repeatedly, when it becomes a hadd of Allah Almighty. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have killed the Jew, per the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, because killing became due upon him for Allah Almighty as upon a highway robber. If so, Abu Hanifa, may Allah be pleased with him, says: whoever robs the road and kills with stick or stone, or does that in town, his ruling is that of a highway robber; likewise the strangler who did it repeatedly is killed. By analogy upon his saying, one who did it once should incur killing, as a hadd of Allah Almighty, as when he does it repeatedly; for we saw hadds incurred by violating sanctity once, with no more due for violating it again than was due at first. By reflection the strangling offender should likewise be, his ruling first time as last time - this is reflection in this chapter. With what we established, the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, is no proof against one who says: Whoever kills a man with stone incurs no retaliation."
"4710","5031 - Part of Abu Hanifa's proof, may Allah be pleased with him, for this saying was also what Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyab and Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: Two women of Hudhayl fought; one struck the other with a stone, killing her and what was in her womb. They disputed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who judged blood-money of her fetus a slave or slave girl, and judged the woman's blood-money upon her aqila, bequeathing her to her son and those with them. Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha al-Hudhali said: O Messenger of Allah, how do I pay for one who neither drank nor ate nor spoke nor cried? The like is void. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is but among the brethren of soothsayers, for his rhymed speech."
"4711","5032 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: al-Firyabi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ubayd ibn Nadla al-Khuzai, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, that two women, one struck the other with a tent-pole and killed her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged blood-money upon the killer's paternal kin, and judged what was in her womb a ghurra - and ghurra is a slave or slave girl. The Bedouin said: Do I pay for one who neither tasted nor drank nor cried nor wailed? The like is void. He said: Rhymed speech like the rhyming of Bedouins."
"4712","5033 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, saying: Zaida informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ubayd ibn Nadla, from al-Mughira, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. They said: These reports inform that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not kill the woman who killed with stone nor with tent-pole - and a tent-pole's like kills. That proves no retaliation upon one who kills with wood, even if its like kills. Part of the proof of their opponents was: Hamal narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to this."
"4713","He mentioned what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, saying: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, adjured the people - i.e. asked and swore upon them - for the Messenger of Allah's judgment on the fetus. Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabigha stood and said: I was between two women; one struck the other with a mistah and killed her and her fetus. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged a ghurra for the fetus and that she be killed in her place."
"4714","5034 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Hisham ibn Sulayman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, the same - except he did not mention his saying: and that she be killed in her place. So this Hamal ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he killed the woman for the one she killed with a mistah. Thus he contradicted Abu Hurayra and al-Mughira, may Allah be pleased with them, in what they narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his judging blood-money therein. The reports thus balance out. Since they balanced and differed, reflection became due, to extract a sound saying from the two; we considered. We found the agreed basis: whoever kills a man with iron intentionally incurs retaliation, sinful therein, with no expiation upon him in most scholars' view. If he kills him by mistake, blood-money is upon his aqila, expiation upon him, no sin upon him - expiation due where sin is lifted, lifted where sin is due. We saw quasi-intentional killing agreed to incur blood-money and expiation, differing on its nature: some said it is a man killing a man deliberately with other than a weapon. Others said it is killing with what is not seen to kill - as intentionally striking with a whip or what its like does not kill, and he dies of it; this is quasi-intentional in their view. If he repeated striking with the whip until its like would kill, that is intentional, incurring retaliation. All who placed quasi-intentional killing upon either kind required expiation in it. We saw expiation, upon what both parties agreed, due where no sin is due, negated where sin is - and the killer with stone, stick or the like that kills bears the sin of a soul, being before his Lord as one who killed with iron; while one who kills with a whip whose like does not kill bears not the sin of killing but the sin of striking - the sin of killing lifted from him since he did not intend it, the sin of striking recorded since he intended it. By reflection, quasi-intentional killing, agreed to incur expiation for the soul, is what bears no sin: killing with what its like does not kill, intending striking without intending loss of life, yet loss of life results. Thus the saying of these holders is established - the saying of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them. It was also narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4715","5035 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, saying: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, saying: Zayd ibn Jubayr al-Jushami narrated to me, from Jarwa ibn Humayl, from his father, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: One of you deliberately strikes his brother like a meat-eater - al-Hajjaj said: meaning the stick - then says: No retaliation upon me. No one who does that shall be brought to me but I shall retaliate him. It was also narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, contrary to that."
"4716","5036 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, saying: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, who said: Quasi-intentional killing is with stick and heavy stone, with no retaliation in them. Allah knows best what is correct."
"4717","5037 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, saying: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar - adding: He said: He asked him and he confessed what she claimed, so his head was crushed between two stones."
"4718","5038 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, saying: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, that a Jew crushed a slave girl's head between two stones. She was asked: Who did this to you? So-and-so? So-and-so? Until they mentioned the Jew; he was brought and confessed, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered and his head was crushed between two stones. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed him only for his confessing what was claimed against him, not by the girl's accusation. That is also clarified by what they agreed upon. Do you not see that if a man claimed against a man a claim, killing or otherwise, and the defendant was asked and nodded his head: Yes - that would not be a confession imposing a right. If the defendant's nodding is no confession imposing a right, the claimant's nodding is even less likely to establish a right."
"4719","5039 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If people were given by their claims, men would claim the blood of men and their wealth; but the oath is upon the defendant. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barred anyone being given blood or wealth by his claim, granting the claimant nothing by his claim except the oath. This is the ruling of this chapter by validating the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by reflection: they agreed that if a man claimed in his dying state that a man owes him dirhams, then died, that is not accepted from him, being as in his claim in health. By reflection his claiming blood in that state is likewise as his claiming it in health. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"4720","5040 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us, saying: Nafi ibn Umar informed us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, who said: I was governor for Ibn al-Zubayr over Taif, and I wrote to Ibn Abbas about two women in a house stitching silk of theirs; one struck her companion's hand with an awl and wounded her; she went out bleeding, with sharp tools in the room. She said: She struck me; the other denied it. I wrote to Ibn Abbas about that, and he wrote to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged the oath upon the defendant; were people given by their claims, men would claim the blood of men and their wealth. So call her and recite this verse to her: Those who purchase with Allah's covenant and their oaths a small price - the verse. I recited the verse to her and she confessed. Nafi said: I think he said: That reached Ibn Abbas and pleased him. Do you not see that Ibn Abbas, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them, referred her ruling therein to the ruling of all what people claim against one another. Allah knows best."
"4721","Chapter 6: A believer intentionally killing a disbeliever. 5041 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, saying: Sufyan informed us. 5042 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, saying: Asad narrated to us, saying: Asbat narrated to us, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from al-Shabi, from Abu Juhayfa, who said: I asked Ali: Have you anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides the Quran? He said: By Him who split the grain and created the soul, we have nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides the Quran, and what is in this sheet. I said: What is in this sheet? He said: Blood-money, freeing the captive, and that no Muslim is killed for a disbeliever. Abu Jafar said: A group held that when a Muslim kills a disbeliever deliberately, he is not killed for him, arguing with this hadith. Others opposed them, saying: Rather he is killed for him. Part of their proof was that this speech Abu Juhayfa reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was not isolated; had it been isolated it might bear what they said, but it was joined to other than it."
"4722","5043 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Musaddad narrated to us, saying: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Aruba, saying: Qatada narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: I and al-Ashtar went to Ali and said: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenant to you a covenant he did not covenant to people generally? He said: No, except what is in this book of mine. He produced a book from his sword sheath; in it: The believers, their blood is equal, the lowest seeks protection by their covenant, they are one hand against others; no believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant during his covenant; whoever innovates an event, it is upon himself; whoever innovates an event or shelters an innovator, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all people. This is the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in full; what negates killing the believer for the disbeliever is his saying: No believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant during his covenant. It is impossible its meaning is as the holders of the first saying construed; for had it been as they mentioned, it would be solecism - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is farthest from that - and it would be: no believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant during his covenant. Since its wording is not so, but: nor one under covenant during his covenant, we know thereby that the one under covenant is meant by retaliation. It becomes as his saying: No believer nor one under covenant during his covenant is killed for a disbeliever. We know the one under covenant is a disbeliever; that proves the disbeliever whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barred the believer being killed for in this hadith is the disbeliever with no covenant. This is undisputed among believers: the believer is not killed for the hostile disbeliever, and the covenanted disbeliever granted dhimma is also not killed for him. We find much like this in the Quran; Allah Almighty said: Those who despair of menstruation among your women, if you doubt, their waiting period is three months, and those who have not menstruated. Its meaning: Those who despair of menstruation and those who have not menstruated, if you doubt, their waiting period is three months - advancing and delaying. Likewise his saying: No believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant during his covenant - its intent, Allah knows best: No believer nor one under covenant during his covenant is killed for a disbeliever - advancing and delaying. The disbeliever barred from the believer being killed for him is the non-covenanted disbeliever. If one says: His saying: nor one under covenant during his covenant means: No believer is killed for a disbeliever - speech ends - then he said: nor one under covenant during his covenant - fresh speech, i.e. the covenanted one is not killed during his covenant. Part of our proof against him was that this hadith concerned bloods shed for one another, for he said: The Muslims are one hand against others, their blood is equal, the lowest seeks protection by their covenant - then said: No believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor one under covenant during his covenant. He ran the speech upon bloods taken in retaliation, not upon sanctity of blood by covenant; so the hadith is construed upon that - this is one aspect. Another proof: this hadith was narrated only from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we know of it narrated from no other by a sound route; so he was most knowledgeable of its interpretation. His interpreting it, when it bore with you these two meanings you mentioned, proves its true meaning is what he interpreted it upon."
"4723","5044 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: al-Layth narrated to me, saying: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: Saeed ibn al-Musayyab informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq said - when Umar was killed: I passed by Abu Lulua with Hurmuzan. When we surprised them they sprang up, and from between them fell a dagger with two heads, its grip in the middle. He said: I said: See, perhaps it is the dagger with which he killed Umar; they looked and it was the dagger Abd al-Rahman described. Ubaydullah ibn Umar set out upon hearing that from Abd al-Rahman, with sword, until he summoned Hurmuzan; when he came out to him he said: Come, look at a horse of mine - then lagged behind him; when he walked before him he struck him with the sword. Finding the sword's touch he said: There is no god but Allah. Ubaydullah said: I summoned Hufayna, a Christian of the Christians of Hira; when he came out to me I struck him with the sword between his eyes. Then Ubaydullah went and killed Abu Lulua's small daughter who claimed Islam. When Uthman was made caliph he summoned the Emigrants and Helpers, saying: Advise me regarding killing this man who rent in religion what he rent. The Emigrants united upon one word, ordering severity against him, urging Uthman to kill him - Ali among them. The great mass of people were with Ubaydullah, saying of Hufayna and Hurmuzan: May Allah cast them far. Therein was disagreement. Then Amr ibn al-As said: O Commander of the Faithful, Allah has excused you from this affair after you were pledged; it was only before you had authority over people. So he turned away from Ubaydullah. People dispersed upon Amr ibn al-As's sermon, and he paid blood-money for the two men and the girl. So in this hadith Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him, killed Hufayna while an idolater, and struck Hurmuzan while a disbeliever - his Islam after that. The Emigrants, Allah's pleasure upon them, advised Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, to kill Ubaydullah - Ali among them. It is impossible the Prophet's saying: No believer is killed for a disbeliever, means other than the hostile one, then the Emigrants, Ali among them, advise Uthman to kill Ubaydullah for a covenanted disbeliever; rather its meaning is as we mentioned: intending the disbeliever with no dhimma. If one says: In this hadith Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him, killed Abu Lulua's small daughter who claimed Islam, so they may have deemed his blood lawful for her, not for Hufayna and Hurmuzan. It is said to him: In this hadith is what proves he meant killing him for Hufayna and Hurmuzan - their saying: May Allah cast them far. It is impossible Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, meant killing him for other than them, while people said to him: May Allah cast them far - without his saying to them: I did not mean killing him for these two, but for the girl. Rather he meant killing him for them and the girl. Do you not see him saying: disagreement increased therein. That proves Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, meant killing him for those he killed - among them Hurmuzan and Hufayna. Thus it is established by what we mentioned, validating this hadith's meaning upon the first as we described, negating any proof therein barring the Muslim being killed for the dhimmi. That is also matched by its soundness in what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though interrupted."
"4724","5045 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Amir narrated to us, saying: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a Muslim man who had killed a covenanted man of the dhimma people; he ordered him and his neck was struck, saying: I am foremost to fulfill his covenant."
"4725","5046 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd al-Madani, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Reflection with us also supports that: we saw the hostile one's blood and wealth lawful; when he becomes a dhimmi, his blood and wealth become inviolable as the Muslim's blood and wealth. Then we saw whoever steals of the dhimmi's wealth what requires cutting, is cut as for the Muslim's wealth. Since punishments for violating wealth made inviolable by dhimma are as for wealth made inviolable by Islam, by reflection the punishment for blood made inviolable by dhimma should be as for that made inviolable by Islam. If one says: We saw punishments for violating wealth distinguished from those for violating blood - we saw a slave stealing his master's wealth is not cut, yet killing his master is killed - so why deny distinguishing what is due for violating the dhimmi's wealth and blood? It is said: What you mentioned strengthens our view, for you mentioned they agreed a slave is not cut for his master's wealth, yet is killed for his master and his master's slaves. What you described lightened wealth and emphasized blood, requiring punishment for blood where not required for wealth. With blood's emphasis and wealth's lightness established, and the dhimmi's wealth incurring upon the Muslim violator the same punishment as the Muslim's wealth, his blood is worthier to incur for violating its sanctity what is incurred for violating the Muslim's blood. They agreed that if a dhimmi killed a dhimmi then the killer embraced Islam, he is killed for the dhimmi he killed in his disbelief, his Islam not voiding it. Since later Islam upon killing does not void killing in disbelief, and hadds are complete one of them, never found in a state not due at start - do you not see if a man killed a man, the slain an apostate, nothing is due; and if he wounded him while Muslim then he apostatized - Allah forbid - and died, he is not killed. His apostasy preceding the offence and occurring upon it equally avert killing. Likewise by reflection the killer before and after his offence is alike. Since his Islam after his offence before being killed for it does not avert retaliation, likewise his prior Islam for his offence does not avert retaliation. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all. And..."
"4726","5047 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Wahb narrated to us, saying: Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabra, who said: A Muslim man killed a man of the monks; his brother went to Umar, and Umar wrote that he be killed. They kept saying: Kill, O Jubayr - and he would say: Until anger comes. He said: Then Umar wrote that blood-money be paid and he not be killed. So this Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, also held a Muslim is killed for a disbeliever, writing so to his governor in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with none of them rejecting it. This, in our view, is their concurrence with him therein. His later writing: He is not killed, may have been because he disliked permitting his blood given his hesitation over killing him, making that a doubt barring killing, granting what is granted in intentional killing entered by doubt - namely blood-money. The Medinans said: If a Muslim kills a dhimmi by treachery for his wealth, he is killed for him. Since this, in their view, is outside the Prophet's saying: No Muslim is killed for a disbeliever - though the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excepted none of the disbelievers - then as they might exclude from disbelievers one whose wealth was sought, their opponents might likewise exclude one whose dhimma is established."
"4727","Chapter 7: Qasama - is it upon the dwellers of the house where the slain is found, or upon its owner? 5048 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Saeed, who heard Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathma - who said: Abdullah ibn Sahl was found slain in a well of Khaybar's wells. His brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl and his uncles Huwayyisa and Muhayyisa, sons of Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Abd al-Rahman went to speak. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The elder, the elder. One of his two uncles spoke - either Huwayyisa or Muhayyisa - the elder of them spoke. He said: O Messenger of Allah, we found Abdullah ibn Sahl slain in a well of Khaybar's wells, mentioning the Jews' enmity toward them. He said: Will the Jews exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths that they did not kill him? He said: I said: How can we accept their oaths when they are idolaters? He said: Then fifty of you swear they killed him. They said: How can we swear upon what we did not see? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from himself."
"4728","5049 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that he informed him that Abdullah ibn Sahl al-Ansari and Muhayyisa ibn Masud went out to Khaybar and parted for their needs; Abdullah ibn Sahl was killed, reaching Muhayyisa. He came with his brother Huwayyisa and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Abd al-Rahman went to speak given his place as his brother. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the elder speak, let the elder speak. Huwayyisa and Muhayyisa spoke, mentioning Abdullah ibn Sahl's affair. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Will you swear fifty oaths, deserving thereby the blood of your killer or companion? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we neither witnessed nor attended. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will the Jews exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths? They said: O Messenger of Allah, how can we accept the oaths of disbelieving folk? Malik said: Yahya ibn Saeed said: Bushayr claimed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from himself."
"4729","5050 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Ubayd al-Tai narrated to us, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that an Ansari man called Sahl ibn Abi Hathma informed him that men of his people went to Khaybar and dispersed therein; they found one of them slain. They said to those among whom they found him: You killed our companion. They said: By Allah, we did not kill, nor know a killer. They went to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Prophet of Allah, we went to Khaybar and found one of us slain. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The elder, the elder. He said to them: Will you bring proof against who killed? They said: We have no proof. He said: Will they swear to you? They said: We do not accept the oaths of Jews. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked his blood voided, so he paid it with one hundred camels of charity."
"4730","5051 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Layla ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathma, that men of his people's elders informed him that Abdullah ibn Sahl and Muhayyisa went out to Khaybar from hardship that befell them. Muhayyisa came reporting Abdullah ibn Sahl killed, cast in a cleft or spring. He came to the Jews saying: By Allah, you killed him. They said: By Allah, we did not kill him. He proceeded to his people, mentioning that, then came with his brother Huwayyisa - elder than him - and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl. Muhayyisa, who had been at Khaybar, went to speak. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Muhayyisa: Let the elder speak, let the elder speak - meaning age. Huwayyisa spoke first, then Muhayyisa spoke. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Either they pay blood-money for your companion, or they announce war. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to them about that; they wrote: By Allah, we did not kill him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Huwayyisa, Muhayyisa and Abd al-Rahman: Will you swear and deserve your companion's blood? They said: No. He said: Will the Jews swear to you? They said: They are not Muslims. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from himself, sending them one hundred she-camels until brought into their house. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said: We know Khaybar belonged to the Muslims, for they conquered it, the Jews being their workers therein. When this slain was found therein, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed qasama therein upon the Jewish dwellers, not the owners. He said: Likewise we say: every slain found in a house or land with a dweller - lessee or borrower - qasama and blood-money is upon the dweller, not its owning lord. Abu Hanifa and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them, said: Blood-money and qasama therein is upon the owner, not the dweller. Part of their proof against Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, was that this slain is not stated in this hadith to have been found at Khaybar after its conquest or before. It may have been struck therein after its conquest - as Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said. It may have been struck while it was truce between the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and its people. If found while truce before conquest, Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, has no proof in this hadith. In the hadith of Abu Layla ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman is what indicates it was then truce: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them: Either they pay blood-money for your companion, or they announce war - said only to those in safety and covenant, in a land at truce between its people and the Muslims. Sulayman ibn Bilal clarified that in his hadith from Yahya ibn Saeed."
"4731","5052 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Maslama al-Qanabi narrated to us, saying: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that Abdullah ibn Sahl ibn Zayd and Muhayyisa ibn Masud ibn Zayd al-Ansari of Banu Haritha went out to Khaybar in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it then being truce with its people Jews. They parted for their needs. Abdullah ibn Sahl was killed, found slain in his water-channel; his companion buried him, then came to Medina. The slain's brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl, Muhayyisa and Huwayyisa walked, mentioning to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Abdullah ibn Sahl's affair and how he was killed. Bushayr ibn Yasar claimed, narrating from Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he met, that he said to them: Will you swear fifty oaths and deserve your slain's blood or your companion's? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we neither witnessed nor attended. He said: Will the Jews exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths? They said: O Messenger of Allah, how can we accept the oaths of disbelieving folk? Bushayr claimed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money. This hadith clarifies to us that at the time Abdullah ibn Sahl was found therein slain, it was a land of truce and covenant; thereby nothing Abu Yusuf, may Allah's mercy be upon him, argued against Abu Hanifa and Muhammad with this hadith binds them, for Khaybar's conquest was only after that. Abu Yusuf, may Allah's mercy be upon him, said: Reflection also supports what we said. We saw a leased or borrowed house in its lessee's or borrower's possession, not its lord's. Do you not see that if they and its lord disputed over a garment found therein, the saying is theirs, not the house lord's. Likewise slain found therein are found in it while in its lessee's or borrower's possession, not its lord's. What qasama and blood-money becomes due thereby is upon whose possession it is in, not upon whom it is not in, though he owned it to him. Part of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan's proof, may Allah have mercy on him, was: I saw their consensus proving qasama is due upon the owner, not the dweller. If a man and his wife had a house in their possession they dwell in, belonging to the husband, and a slain was found therein, qasama and blood-money would be upon the husband's aqila specifically, not the wife's aqila. We know both their hands are upon it, and what garments found therein neither is worthier of than the other, except for a meaning unrelated to ownership and possession. Had qasama been judged upon whose possession the house is in, it would be judged upon wife and husband together, since the house is in both their hands and they dwelt in it. Since what is due therein is upon the husband specifically, not the wife, as its owner, qasama and blood-money in every place slain are found is upon its owner, not its dweller."
"4732","5053 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, saying: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from al-Harith ibn al-Azma, that he said to Umar: Do our wealth not avert our oaths, nor our oaths avert our wealth? He said: No - and he imposed blood-money upon him."
"4733","5054 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, saying: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, saying: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn al-Azma, who said: A slain was killed between Wadia and another clan, the slain nearer to Wadia. Umar said to Wadia: Fifty men of you swear: By Allah we did not kill, nor know a killer - then pay blood-money. al-Harith said to him: We swear and you fine us? He said: Yes."
"4734","5055 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, saying: Uthman ibn Matar narrated to us, from Abu Hariz, from al-Shabi, from al-Harith al-Wadii, who said: They found a slain between two villages and wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab about that. Umar wrote: Measure between the two villages; whichever he was nearer to, take fifty qasama oaths swearing by Allah, then fine them blood-money. al-Harith said: I was among those who swore, then we paid blood-money. This is the qasama judged by the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That matches what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) elsewhere, that he said: If people were given by their claims, men would claim the blood of men and their wealth; but the oath is upon the defendant. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) equated therein wealth and blood, judging them by one ruling, placing the oath in all that upon the defendant. Thus the meaning of Sahl's hadith is also as we interpreted it. That is also proved by what we mentioned in the preceding chapter, from Saeed ibn Ubayd, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathma, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) summoned them with proof; when they mentioned no proof, he said: Will they swear to you? What we mentioned proves what of the Messenger of Allah's judgment therein was this; what exceeds it in the hadith of Yahya ibn Saeed and Abu Layla ibn Abdullah is not upon judgment, but upon the meaning we interpreted them upon. Then this al-Zuhri knew the Messenger of Allah's judgment by qasama. Among what was narrated from him therein is what:"
"4735","5056 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from men of the Ansar, Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that qasama existed in pre-Islamic times; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed it as it was, and judged by it among men regarding a slain they claimed against the Jews."
"4736","5057 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: al-Awzai narrated to us, saying: al-Zuhri narrated to us, saying: Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to us, from men of the Ansar, Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4737","Then al-Zuhri also said regarding qasama what: 5058 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Abu Muawiya al-Darir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged qasama upon those claimed against. That proves qasama is upon those claimed against, not claimants, as al-Zuhri clarified in this hadith of his. He took qasama from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); this was among what he took from them. That matches what we narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, of what he did and judged in the presence of all the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah pleased with them, with none rejecting it. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"4738","Chapter: What livestock damage by night and day. 5059 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Muhayyisa, from al-Bara ibn Azib, that a she-camel of an Ansari man entered a garden and spoiled therein. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged garden folk must guard it by day, and livestock folk liable for what their livestock spoil by night."
"4739","5060 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Haram ibn Saad ibn Muhayyisa, that a she-camel of al-Bara ibn Azib entered a man's garden and spoiled therein. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that garden folk must guard by day, and what livestock spoil at night is guaranteed against them."
"4740","5061 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: al-Khidr ibn Muhammad al-Harrani narrated to us, saying: Abbad ibn Awwam narrated to us, saying: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Muhayyisa, from his father Muhayyisa ibn Masud, that a she-camel of al-Bara ibn Azib entered a garden of the Ansar and spoiled therein. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged its spoiling upon its folk; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged livestock folk must restrain their livestock by night from gardens, and garden folk must guard their gardens by day from livestock."
"4741","5062 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Saeed ibn al-Musayyab and Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dumb animal's act is void of liability, and the mine is void of liability."
"4742","5063 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Saeed, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. The questioner said to him: O Abu Muhammad, was Abu Salama with him? He said: If he was with him, then he is with him."
"4743","5064 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyab and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4744","5065 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4745","5066 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4746","5067 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, saying: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4747","5068 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4748","5069 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, saying: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra saying: I heard Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying - and he mentioned the same."
"4749","5070 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: al-Firyabi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, raising it, the same. Abu Jafar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made what the dumb animal causes void of liability - and jubar is hadar (unclaimed). That abrogates what preceded in the hadith of Abu Muhayyisa, even if interrupted, not constituting proof against us in the view of its user. Even if al-Awzai joined it, Malik and the reliable companions of al-Zuhri cut it. Moreover, the ruling mentioned therein is taken from the ruling of Sulayman the Prophet, peace be upon him, on crops when sheep grazed them by night. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged by that ruling, until Allah brought this law, abrogating what preceded. What proves what we narrated from Jabir and Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with them, came after what is in the hadith of Haram ibn Muhayyisa - his saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged livestock folk must guard their livestock by night, and crop folk must guard their crops by day. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made livestock, when its lord must guard it, liable for what it causes; when he need not guard it, not liable for what it causes - requiring guarantee for what the loose causes by night, since its owner must guard it. Then he said in the hadith: The dumb animal's wound is void - so what it causes in its looseness is void; were it to demolish a wall or kill a man, its owner guarantees nothing, though he must guard it from straying, when it is such as this is feared from. Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith did not consider his duty to guard it, but considered its looseness and imposed nothing for what it caused, the matter returned to night and day being alike. Thus what it causes by night or day, loose, incurs no guarantee upon its lord; if he released it and it caused something in its rush or release, he guarantees it all. This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all; it is worthiest construction of these reports for what we mentioned and clarified."
"4750","Chapter 10: The ghurra of the fetus judged therein - whose is it? 5071 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, that two women of Hudhayl, one threw at the other and she cast her fetus. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged a ghurra: a slave or slave girl."
"4751","5072 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for the fetus of a woman of Banu Lihyan, miscarried dead, a ghurra: a slave or slave girl; and that she against whom ghurra was judged died, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged her inheritance to her sons and husband, and blood-money upon her paternal kin."
"4752","5073 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for the fetus a ghurra: a slave or slave girl. He against whom judgment was made said: Do we pay blood-money for one who neither drank nor ate nor cried aloud? The like is void. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He speaks with a poet's speech - in it a ghurra, a slave or slave girl."
"4753","5074 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, saying: Shuba narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ubayd ibn Nadla, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, that a man had two wives; one struck the other with a tent-pole or stone and she miscarried. That was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The disputant said: How is blood-money paid, or paid for one who neither cried aloud nor drank nor ate? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rhymed speech like the rhyming of Bedouins? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed in it a ghurra, placing it upon her people. Abu Jafar said: A group held the ghurra due for the fetus is due only to the fetus's mother, for the fetus was not known alive at the time its mother was struck. Others opposed them, saying: Rather that judged ghurra is the fetus's, then inherited by whoever would inherit him had he been alive. Part of their proof was what we mentioned in these reports: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged ghurra against the judged one, he said: How is blood-money paid for one who neither ate nor drank nor spoke? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In it a ghurra: a slave or slave girl - without saying to the rhymer: I judged this only for the offence against the woman, not for the fetus. That is also proved by what we narrated earlier in this book: the struck woman died thereafter of the blow, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged blood-money in her case along with judging ghurra. Had the ghurra been for the slain woman, he would not have judged ghurra for her; her ruling would be that of a woman struck by a woman, dying of her blow - upon her is her blood-money, with no compensation due for the blow. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged ghurra along with the woman's blood-money, it is established the ghurra is blood-money for the fetus, not for her; it is inherited from the fetus, as his wealth would be inherited had he been alive then died - following what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"4754","Book 13: Campaigns. Chapter 1: The Imam intending to fight hostile people - must he summon them beforehand or not? 5075 - Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan ibn Saeed al-Thawri narrated to us, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed a man over a company, he said to him: When you meet your polytheist enemy, summon them to one of three traits - or qualities - whichever they answer you to, accept from them and refrain from them: summon them to Islam; if they answer you, accept from them and refrain from them. Then summon them to move from their land to the Muslims' land, informing them that if they do, upon them is what is upon the Emigrants, and for them what is for them. If they refuse, inform them they are as the Bedouins of the Muslims, Allah's ruling applying to them as upon believers, with nothing for them in fay and spoils except if they strive with the Muslims. If they refuse to enter Islam, ask them to pay jizya; if they answer, accept from them and refrain from them. If they refuse, seek Allah's help and fight them. 5076 - Alqama said: I narrated it to Muqatil ibn Hayyan, who said: Muslim ibn Haysam narrated to me, from al-Numan ibn Muqarrin, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4755","5077 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same - except he did not mention the hadith of Alqama from Muqatil from Muslim ibn Haysam."
"4756","5078 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu Salih narrated to us. 5079 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, each of them saying: al-Layth ibn Saad narrated to me, saying: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Shuba ibn al-Hajjaj, from Alqama ibn Marthad al-Hadrami, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4757","5080 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Saad al-Saidi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he directed Ali ibn Abi Talib to Khaybar and gave him the banner, Ali said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I fight them until they become like us? He said: Proceed calmly until you alight in their courtyard, then summon them to Islam and inform them of what is due upon them of Allah Almighty's right. By Allah, for Allah to guide by you one man is better for you than red camels."
"4758","5081 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Safti narrated to us, saying: al-Humaydi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Umar ibn Dharr, from the nephew of Anas ibn Malik, from his uncle, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali ibn Abi Talib to a people to fight them, then sent after him summoning him back, saying to him: Do not come to him from behind him, come to him from before him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Ali not to fight them until he summons them."
"4759","5082 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never fought a people until he summoned them."
"4760","5083 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, saying: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Abi Najih narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4761","5084 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaida narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"4762","5085 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, saying: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hajjaj, and he mentioned with his chain the same. Abu Jafar said: A group held that the Imam and company folk, when intending to fight the enemy, summon them beforehand to what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Burayda; arguing with these reports, saying: If the Imam or any of his company folk fights them without this summons, they did wrong therein. Others opposed them, saying: No harm in fighting them and raiding them though not summoned beforehand. They argued with what:"
"4763","5086 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, saying: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Usama ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Raid Ubna in the morning, then burn."
"4764","5087 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us. 5088 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj and Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us. 5089 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. 5090 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raid the enemy at dawn prayer, listening: if he heard adhan he held back, otherwise he raided."
"4765","5091 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Murra, from Zadham, from Jarir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4766","5092 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, saying: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided a people, he did not raid them until morning; if he heard adhan he held back, if he did not hear adhan he raided. So we alighted at Khaybar; in the morning hearing no adhan, he mounted and we mounted with him. I rode behind Abu Talha, my foot touching the Messenger of Allah's foot. Khaybar's workers met us, having brought out their spades and baskets; seeing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the army, they said: Muhammad and the army - and fled running. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Greatest, Khaybar is ruined; when we alight in a people's courtyard, evil is the morning of the warned."
"4767","5093 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yaqub ibn Utba, from Muslim ibn Abdullah ibn Khubayb al-Juhani, from Jundub ibn Makith al-Juhani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ghalib ibn Abdullah al-Laythi in a company I was among, ordering him to launch a raid upon Ibn al-Mulawwih at al-Kadid. He said: Their livestock of camels and sheep rested in the evening; when they milked, settled and slept secure, we launched the raid upon them, killing and driving off livestock."
"4768","5094 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Asad narrated to us, saying: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, who said: Abu al-Aliya came to me and a companion of mine; we went with him to Nasr ibn Asim al-Laythi. Abu al-Aliya said: Narrate to these two your hadith. He said: Uqba ibn Malik al-Laythi narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a company that raided the people; a man broke away, followed by a man of the company - then he mentioned a long hadith, of which we wanted what mentions the raid."
"4769","5095 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salama ibn al-Akwa, from his father, who said: When we neared the polytheists, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq ordered us and we launched the raid upon them. So in these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered raiding - and raiding cannot be preceded by summons and warning. So one of the two matters we narrated may abrogate the other."
"4770","We looked into that: Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari narrated to us. 5096 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, saying: Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us. 5097 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Ishaq al-Darir narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, saying: I wrote to Nafi asking him about summoning before fighting. He said: That was only at the beginning of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Banu al-Mustaliq while they were heedless, their livestock at water; he killed their fighters, took their captives, and on that day captured Juwayriya bint al-Harith. 5098 - Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me this hadith, being in that army."
"4771","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, the same."
"4772","5099 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, saying: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: All that occurred; we used to raid, summoning and not summoning."
"4773","5100 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salama informed us that Sulayman al-Taymi informed them, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: We used to raid, summoning and not summoning."
"4774","5101 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Mubarak narrated to us, saying: al-Hasan used to say: No summons is due upon the Byzantines, for they were summoned."
"4775","5102 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Talha narrated to us, from Abu Hamza, who said: I said to Ibrahim: Men say the polytheists should be summoned. He said: The Byzantines know upon what they are fought, and the Daylam know upon what they are fought."
"4776","5103 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, saying: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Mansur, who said: I asked Ibrahim about summoning the Daylam. He said: They know what summons is. He ordered summons to convey to them, informing them upon what they are fought. Abu Jafar said: What we narrated clarifies that summons was only at the beginning of Islam, for people then had not been reached by the call, nor knew upon what they are fought; so summons was ordered, to convey to them and inform them upon what they are fought. Then he ordered raiding others - only for a meaning needing no summons with it, since they knew what they would be summoned to if summoned, and what, if they answered, they would not be fought for; so summons is meaningless. Thus Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all, used to say: Every people reached by the call, whom the Imam intends to fight - he may raid them, with no duty to summon them; every people not reached by the call - they should not be fought until clarified to them the meaning upon which they are fought, and the meaning to which they are summoned."
"4777","5104 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, with that also. There was narrated regarding seeking the apostate's repentance and omitting it disagreement from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among that is what:"
"4778","5105 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: Hushaym informed us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, saying: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, saying: When we conquered Tustar, Abu Musa sent me to Umar. When I came to him he said: What became of Hujayba and his companions? They had apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists, and the Muslims killed them. I diverted him with other talk, and he said: What became of the Bakri men? I said: O Commander of the Faithful, they apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists with them, and were killed. Umar said: For them to have been taken peacefully would be dearer to me than such-and-such. I said: O Commander of the Faithful, their way had they been taken peacefully was only killing - a people who apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists. He said: Had they been taken peacefully, I would have offered them the door they exited; if they returned, else I would have lodged them in prison."
"4779","5106 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, who said: Men were seized at Kufa spreading Musaylima the liar's talk. Writing about them to Uthman ibn Affan, Uthman wrote: Offer them the true religion and testimony that there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is Allah's Messenger; whoever accepts it and disavows Musaylima, do not kill him; whoever adheres to Musaylima's religion, kill him. Men of them accepted it and were left; men adhered to Musaylima's religion and were killed."
"4780","5107 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: When Saad and Abu Musa conquered Tustar, Abu Musa sent a messenger to Umar, mentioning a long hadith. He said: Then Umar turned to the messenger, saying: Was there with you strange news? He said: Yes, O Commander of the Faithful, we seized an Arab man who disbelieved after his Islam. Umar said: What did you do with him? He said: We brought him forth and struck his neck. Umar said: Did you not enter him into a house, then seal it upon him, then throw him a loaf for three days - perhaps he would repent or return to Allah's command? O Allah, I did not command, nor witness, nor approve when it reached me."
"4781","5108 - Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari, from his father, from his grandfather, that he said: A man came to Umar from Abu Musa - then he mentioned similar. This Saad and Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with them, did not seek his repentance; Umar liked he be asked to repent. That may have been because he hoped for his repentance, without invalidating their killing him thereby, for they did what they might deem and do, though opposing their Imam's view."
"4782","5109 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu Ghassan narrated to us. 5110 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, saying: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to me, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, saying: Asim ibn Bahdala narrated to us, saying: Abu Wail narrated to me, saying: Ibn Muayz al-Saadi narrated to me, saying: I went seeking a horse of mine at dawn, passing a mosque of Banu Hanifa's mosques; I heard them testifying Musaylima is Allah's Messenger. I returned to Abdullah ibn Masud, mentioning their affair; he sent the police, seizing them; they were brought to him, repented, turned from what they said, saying: We shall not return - so he released them. He brought forth a man of them called Abdullah ibn al-Nawwaha and struck his neck. People said: You seized a people in one affair, releasing some and killing some. He said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Ibn al-Nawwaha came with a man called Hajr ibn Waththal, two envoys from Musaylima. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do you testify I am Allah's Messenger? They said: Do you testify Musaylima is Allah's Messenger? He said to them: I believe in Allah and His Messenger; were I one to kill an envoy, I would kill you both - for that I said this. So this Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, killed Ibn al-Nawwaha without accepting his repentance, knowing such was his character: displaying repentance when seized, then returning to what he was upon when released."
"4783","5111 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Saeed ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Salih ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Mutarrif informed us, from Abu al-Jahm, from al-Bara, that Ali sent him to the people of al-Nahrawan, summoning them three times."
"4784","5112 - Fahd narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, saying: Zaida ibn Qudama narrated to us, from Umar ibn Qays al-Masir, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Ali advanced until he alighted at Dhi Qar, sending Abdullah ibn Abbas to the people of Kufa; they delayed upon him. Then Ammar summoned them and they came out. Zayd said: I was among those who came out with him. He said: He held back from Talha, al-Zubayr and their companions, summoning them until they began, then fought them."
"4785","5113 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, saying: Sharik ibn Abdullah informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that a man was Christian, embraced Islam, then turned Christian; he was brought to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: What carried you to what you did? He said: I found their religion better than yours. He said to him: What say you of Jesus? He said: He is my lord - or: he is Ali's lord. He said: Kill him - and the people killed him. Ali said thereafter: I had asked him to repent three times. Then he recited: Those who believed, then disbelieved, then believed, then disbelieved, then increased in disbelief."
"4786","5114 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Muadh al-Dabbi narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Muawiyah al-Duhni, from Abu al-Tufayl, that a people apostatized, and they had been Christians. Ali ibn Abi Talib sent Ma'qil ibn Qays al-Taymi to them and said to him: When I scratch my head, kill the fighters and take the offspring captive. He came to a group of them and said: Who are you? They said: We were a Christian people, we were given choice between Islam and our religion, so we chose Islam, then we deemed no religion better than the religion we had been upon, so we are Christians. So he scratched his head, and the fighters were killed and the offspring taken captive. Ammar said: Abu Shu'bah informed me that Ali was brought their children and said: Who will buy them from me? So Misqalah ibn Hubayrah al-Shaybani stood and bought them from Ali for one hundred thousand, and brought him fifty thousand. Ali said: I do not accept the money unless complete. So he buried the money in his house and freed them, and joined Muawiyah, and Ali carried out their emancipation."
"4787","Chapter 2: What makes a man a Muslim. 5115 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard al-Nu'man narrating, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Ubaydullah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, from al-Miqdad ibn Amr, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I and a man from the polytheists exchange two blows, and he strikes me and cuts off my hand, then says: There is no god but Allah - shall I kill him or leave him? He said: Rather leave him. I said: Even though he cut off my hand? He said: Yes; if you kill him then you are like him before he said it, and he is in your position before you kill him."
"4788","5116 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Abi Sagheerah narrated to us, from al-Nu'man, that Amr ibn Aws informed him that his father Aws said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Suffah, and he was narrating to us and reminding us, a man came to him and spoke to him privately, so he said: Go and kill him. When the man turned away, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: Does he not testify that there is no god but Allah? The man said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and let him free; for I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allah, then their blood and wealth become inviolable except by its right."
"4789","5117 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to us, that Abu Hurayrah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah; whoever says: There is no god but Allah, his wealth and his life are protected from me except by its right, and his reckoning is upon Allah."
"4790","5118 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4791","5119 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4792","5120 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, and from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4793","5121 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4794","5122 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held that whoever says: There is no god but Allah has thereby become a Muslim, with the rights and obligations of Muslims, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them and said: You have no proof in this hadith, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only fighting a people who did not affirm Allah's oneness; so when one of them affirmed it, his abandonment of what he was fought for was known, but his entry into Islam was not thereby known, since he might have entered another monotheistic creed. Their rule is not to be fought when this doubt arises until proof of the obligation to fight them is established. As for others like the Jews, we see them testify there is no god but Allah while denying the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so they are not Muslims by mere affirmation of oneness; and even if they affirm the Messenger, their exit from Judaism is known but their entry into Islam is not certain, for they may claim he was sent only to the Arabs."
"4795","5123 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the banner to Ali as he directed him to Khaybar he said: Go and do not turn back until Allah grants you victory. Ali went a little, then stopped without turning and shouted: O Messenger of Allah, upon what basis shall I fight them? He said: Fight them until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; if they do that, their blood and wealth are protected from you except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted fighting them even if they affirmed oneness until they also affirm Muhammad is Allah's Messenger, for they were Jews affirming oneness but not the Messenger. Yet even their affirmation of both does not necessarily make them Muslims, for they may intend something other than Islam; so fighting is withheld until their intent is known. Jews came to the Prophet affirming his prophethood yet not entering Islam, and he did not fight them for refusing Islam since they were not thereby Muslims."
"4796","5124 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, Abu Umayyah, Ahmad ibn Dawud, and Abd al-Aziz ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. 5125 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. 5126 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us. 5127 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, from Safwan ibn Assal, that a Jew said to his companion: Come let us ask this Prophet. The other said to him: Do not call him a prophet, for if he hears it he will get four eyes. So they came to him and asked him about this verse: And We gave Musa nine clear signs. He said: Do not associate anything with Allah, do not kill the soul Allah has forbidden except by right, do not steal, do not commit adultery, do not practice magic, do not consume usury, do not take an innocent to a ruler to kill him, do not slander a chaste woman, do not flee from battle, and upon you especially, O Jews, not to transgress the Sabbath. He said: So they kissed his hand and said: We testify that you are a prophet. He said: Then what prevents you from following me? They said: Dawud supplicated that there always remain a prophet among his offspring, and we fear if we follow you the Jews will kill us. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Jews affirmed the prophethood of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) along with affirming Allah's oneness, yet the Messenger of Allah did not order refraining from fighting them until they affirm all that Muslims affirm. This proves they were not Muslims by that statement, and that Islam is only by the meanings indicating entry into it."
"4797","5128 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; when they testify that, pray our prayer, face our qiblah, and eat our slaughtered meat, their blood and wealth become inviolable to us except by its right; they have what Muslims have and upon them what is upon them. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith indicates the meaning by which the blood of disbelievers becomes inviolable and they become Muslims: abandoning all creeds of disbelief and renouncing them. The first meaning of mere affirmation of oneness is only why we refrain from fighting until we know what the speaker intended, so that these reports are all sound without contradiction. A disbeliever does not become a Muslim judged by Islam's rulings until he testifies both testimonies, renounces every religion besides Islam, and dissociates from it."
"4798","5129 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Malik Sa'd ibn Tariq ibn Ashyam narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah, and abandon what they worship besides Allah; when they do that, their blood and wealth become inviolable to me except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah Almighty."
"4799","5130 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the sign of Islam? He said: That you say: I have submitted my face to Allah and dissociated myself, establish prayer, give zakah, and separate from the polytheists to join the Muslims. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered Muawiyah ibn Haydah thus, and dissociation means abandoning all religions for Allah, it is established that whoever does not dissociate from all but Islam is not known to have entered Islam. This is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them all."
"4800","Chapter 3: A boy reaching puberty without wet dream, entering the status of adults in shares of men and in permissibility of killing him in the abode of war if he is a belligerent. 5131 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salih al-Tammar narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, that Sa'd ibn Muadh judged concerning Banu Qurayzah that those among them upon whom the razor had passed be killed, and their wealth and offspring be divided. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: He has judged among them with Allah's judgment pronounced from above seven heavens."
